Guide.content._register("w12", {"id":"w12","order":12,"region":"agents","title":"Foundry, Hosted Agents and Agent 365","short":"Foundry and Agent 365","tagline":"Give the agent a home, an identity and a paper trail.","story":"The agent works on a laptop, but it has nowhere to live and nobody knows who it is. Runtime, protocol, tool path and identity choices decide what it can reach and who answers for it. Preview labels and retirement dates move fast, so every product claim here carries the date it was checked.","oneLiner":"Choose a runtime, open the right doors, wire tools and knowledge, give the agent an identity and put it under a control plane.","youWillLearn":["Compare prompt agents, hosted agents, direct Responses calls, self-hosting and Copilot Studio by control, state and location","Explain hosted agent sessions, sandboxes, versions, scaling and what the 2026-08-20 backend retirement changed","Pick between the Responses, Invocations, Invocations WebSocket, Activity and A2A protocols","Route tools through a Toolbox and choose between Foundry IQ, file search, memory and custom retrieval","Describe Entra Agent ID objects and choose between S2S, OBO and Agentic-User modes","Explain what Agent 365 observes, governs and secures, and how an agent joins it","Label every feature as GA, preview or retiring with the date you checked"],"minutes":120,"prereqs":["w08","w09","w11"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w12-fc-001","front":"What are the three Foundry Agent Service agent types?","back":"Prompt agents (configuration, no code), voice-based prompt agents (Voice Live over WebSocket, preview) and hosted agents (your container or .zip). A direct Responses call gives an ephemeral agent.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w12-fc-002","front":"What is an **ephemeral agent** in Foundry?","back":"An agent whose definition lives in your code and runs through direct Responses API calls. No Foundry agent resource exists, so Foundry versions and publishes nothing.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w12-fc-003","front":"Hosted agent session states and default idle timeout?","back":"Active, Idle and Resumed. The idle timeout is set per agent version from 2 to 60 minutes, default 15. State in `$HOME` and `/files` is persisted on idle and restored on resume.","tags":["hosted"]},{"id":"w12-fc-004","front":"When is a hosted agent session permanently deleted?","back":"After 30 days of inactivity. Anything that must outlive that window belongs in storage you own.","tags":["hosted"]},{"id":"w12-fc-005","front":"Which sandbox sizes do hosted agents offer?","back":"0.5 vCPU with 1 GiB, 1 vCPU with 2 GiB and 2 vCPU with 4 GiB. Docs advise the next size when sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation.","tags":["hosted"]},{"id":"w12-fc-006","front":"Can a hosted agent endpoint split traffic between versions?","back":"No. An endpoint serves one immutable version with 100 percent of traffic. A canary needs a second agent or client-side routing (inference).","tags":["hosted"]},{"id":"w12-fc-007","front":"Which protocols can a hosted agent expose?","back":"Responses, Invocations, Invocations WebSocket, Activity and A2A. One agent can expose several. Activity is bridged from Responses when you publish to Teams or Microsoft 365.","tags":["protocols"]},{"id":"w12-fc-008","front":"Responses or Invocations: who owns the conversation history?","back":"Responses: the platform keeps history and any OpenAI-compatible SDK is the client. Invocations: you manage sessions and write raw SSE.","tags":["protocols"]},{"id":"w12-fc-009","front":"What is a Foundry **Toolbox**?","back":"A group of tools behind one managed MCP-compatible endpoint, with central auth, toolbox-level guardrails and versioning. Promote a version to default and consumers pick it up with no redeploy.","tags":["tools"]},{"id":"w12-fc-010","front":"What do `tool_search` and `call_tool` do?","back":"They are the two meta-tools exposed when tool search is on (preview). Tools stay hidden by default, so a toolbox can hold hundreds without flooding context.","tags":["tools"]},{"id":"w12-fc-011","front":"What replaced Connected Agents in the new Foundry Agent Service?","back":"The A2A tool, which accepts any A2A endpoint, or Workflows (preview, retire 2026-12-01). Docs point new development at Microsoft Agent Framework.","tags":["a2a"]},{"id":"w12-fc-012","front":"Foundry IQ retrieval reasoning effort levels?","back":"Minimal, low or medium. A knowledge base runs on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval, can sync ACLs, honors Purview labels and can run under the caller's Entra identity.","tags":["knowledge"]},{"id":"w12-fc-013","front":"Name the Entra Agent ID objects.","back":"Agent identity blueprint (a template that holds credentials, one-to-many), agent identity (no credentials of its own) and an optional agent user account (Frontier preview).","tags":["identity"]},{"id":"w12-fc-014","front":"Sponsor versus owner: who can re-enable a disabled agent?","back":"Owners can, and can also hard-delete. Sponsors (at least one required, business accountable) can disable or soft-delete but cannot edit auth settings or re-enable.","tags":["identity"]},{"id":"w12-fc-015","front":"S2S, OBO, Agentic-User: who is the token subject?","back":"S2S: the agent identity. OBO: the user, with the agent identity as actor. Agentic-User: the agent's own user account, which needs the Frontier preview program.","tags":["identity"]},{"id":"w12-fc-016","front":"Why can a correct RBAC assignment still return 403?","back":"A wrong token audience. Use the downstream resource id such as `https://storage.azure.com`, not the MCP server URL.","tags":["identity"]},{"id":"w12-fc-017","front":"Agent 365: pillars and GA date?","back":"Observe, govern and secure. GA on 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, licensed per user.","tags":["agent365"]},{"id":"w12-fc-018","front":"What does the Agent 365 SDK not do?","back":"It does not build, host, orchestrate or run tools. It adds identity, governed tooling (Work IQ MCP servers) and notifications. You still host the agent.","tags":["agent365"]},{"id":"w12-fc-019","front":"What replaced the Agent 365 Observability SDK?","back":"The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for .NET, Node.js and Python. It also targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend. The old SDK is deprecated for new work.","tags":["agent365"]},{"id":"w12-fc-020","front":"Four guardrail intervention points for Foundry agents?","back":"User input, tool call (preview), tool response (preview) and output. Agent guardrails are preview, model guardrails are GA. Spotlighting and groundedness do not apply to agents.","tags":["guardrails"]},{"id":"w12-t01-fc1","front":"Name the three Foundry agent types and the fourth entry point.","back":"Prompt agents, voice-based prompt agents and hosted agents. The fourth is calling the Responses API directly, which gives an ephemeral agent with no Foundry resource.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t01"},{"id":"w12-t01-fc2","front":"What are the three runtime objects in Agent Service?","back":"Agent (model, instructions, tools), conversation (persisted history) and response (one execution). Start with a response and add the others when needed.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t01"},{"id":"w12-t02-fc1","front":"Two voice architectures in Foundry voice agents?","back":"Native speech-to-speech (low latency) and cascaded (recognition, text model, synthesis: more choice and control, more latency).","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t02"},{"id":"w12-t02-fc2","front":"Which phone paths do voice-based prompt agents support?","back":"Teams Phone extensibility and Twilio. Direct SIP is not offered. All of this is preview as of 2026-10-13.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t02"},{"id":"w12-t03-fc1","front":"What does Foundry create automatically when a hosted agent is deployed?","back":"A dedicated Microsoft Entra agent identity and a dedicated endpoint. Publishing is not needed for programmatic access.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t03"},{"id":"w12-t03-fc2","front":"Is there an explicit GA statement for hosted agents (checked 2026-10-13)?","back":"UNVERIFIED. The GA table has no hosted row and the concept page has no preview banner. Confirm status before a GA-only policy decision.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t03"},{"id":"w12-t03-fc3","front":"How are hosted agents billed beyond inference?","back":"CPU and memory consumed across active sessions. Numeric rates are on the pricing page and are UNVERIFIED in this guide.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t03"},{"id":"w12-t04-fc1","front":"Default advice for choosing a hosted agent protocol?","back":"Start with Responses. A hosted agent can expose several protocols, so you can add Invocations later.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t04"},{"id":"w12-t04-fc2","front":"Where do you declare the protocols of a hosted agent?","back":"In the `azure.ai.agent` service in `azure.yaml` with `azd`, or with `protocol_versions` in the SDK. Only declared protocols have active endpoints.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t04"},{"id":"w12-t05-fc1","front":"What is persisted when a hosted agent session goes idle?","back":"`$HOME` and `/files` content. State is restored on resume. The session is deleted after 30 days of inactivity.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t05"},{"id":"w12-t05-fc2","front":"Background mode versus resilient execution?","back":"Background keeps work running after the request returns. Resilience lets a later process recover it after a crash. Recovery restarts the handler, so use checkpoints (preview).","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t05"},{"id":"w12-t06-fc1","front":"What identities does a hosted agent involve?","back":"A dedicated Entra agent identity per agent (runtime) and a project managed identity (platform work such as registry pulls). Agent identity gets inference and session storage by default.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t06"},{"id":"w12-t06-fc2","front":"Can a hosted agent endpoint split traffic between versions?","back":"No. An endpoint serves one immutable version at 100 percent of traffic. Build canaries outside the endpoint.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t06"},{"id":"w12-t06-fc3","front":"Cutoff date for private Container Registry on hosted agents?","back":"Only projects created after 2026-06-25 support a private registry.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t06"},{"id":"w12-t07-fc1","front":"What is an ephemeral agent in Foundry?","back":"An agent whose definition lives in your code and is assembled per Responses API call. No Foundry agent resource is created, updated or deleted.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t07"},{"id":"w12-t07-fc2","front":"Three ways to carry context between Responses calls?","back":"Use a conversation, reference a previous stored response, or resend earlier items. Resending grows the input each turn.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t07"},{"id":"w12-t08-fc1","front":"What does a Foundry toolbox give you that a plain MCP server does not?","back":"Curated grouping, central auth, toolbox-level guardrails, versioning with promotion to default, and (preview) tool search and skills.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t08"},{"id":"w12-t08-fc2","front":"Which tools cannot be added to a toolbox?","back":"Function calling (client-side), Grounding with Bing, computer use, image generation, SharePoint, Fabric data agent and Azure Functions. Use direct integration.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t08"},{"id":"w12-t09-fc1","front":"Why is web search a data-boundary decision?","back":"It uses Grounding with Bing. The Microsoft DPA does not apply to data sent there, and transfers occur outside compliance and geographic boundaries.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t09"},{"id":"w12-t09-fc2","front":"How does code interpreter bill and scale?","back":"Extra charges beyond tokens. Each simultaneous conversation creates a session, active one hour by default with a 30 minute idle timeout.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t09"},{"id":"w12-t09-fc3","front":"Is Logic Apps a built-in agent tool in the new service?","back":"UNVERIFIED on 2026-10-13. It is absent from the toolbox support table.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t09"},{"id":"w12-t10-fc1","front":"What is a Foundry IQ knowledge base made of?","back":"Knowledge sources (Blob, SharePoint, OneLake, public web) plus retrieval settings, run on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval and shareable across agents.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t10"},{"id":"w12-t10-fc2","front":"Is Foundry IQ GA?","back":"Partly. GA depends on the Search REST API version, while portal access to all agentic retrieval features is preview-only (checked 2026-10-13).","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t10"},{"id":"w12-t10-fc3","front":"Foundry IQ vs Fabric IQ vs Work IQ?","back":"Foundry IQ: enterprise knowledge layer. Fabric IQ: semantic layer over OneLake and Power BI. Work IQ: Microsoft 365 collaboration signals.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t10"},{"id":"w12-t11-fc1","front":"What replaces Connected Agents in the new Foundry Agent Service?","back":"The A2A tool (any A2A endpoint, including a Foundry agent exposed as one), Foundry Workflows (preview, retire 2026-12-01), or Agent Framework orchestration in your own code.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t11"},{"id":"w12-t11-fc2","front":"Which header makes a Foundry agent serve A2A 1.0?","back":"`A2A-Version: 1.0`. With no version selector Foundry serves 0.3.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t11"},{"id":"w12-t12-fc1","front":"Three phases and three types of Foundry memory?","back":"Phases: extraction, consolidation, retrieval. Types: user profile, chat summary, procedural. All three types are on by default. Memory is preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t12"},{"id":"w12-t12-fc2","front":"Hard limits for memory stores (preview)?","back":"100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, 1,000 search and 1,000 update requests per minute. No VNet integration for memory stores.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t12"},{"id":"w12-t13-fc1","front":"What does Foundry publish to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot?","back":"The agent's stable endpoint, through an Azure Bot Service resource. Versions roll behind the endpoint and you choose the active version.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t13"},{"id":"w12-t13-fc2","front":"Individual vs Organization scope?","back":"Individual: `Shared`, `BotServiceRbac`, no approval, visible to you. Organization: `Tenant`, `BotServiceTenant`, admin approval, listed under Built by your org.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t13"},{"id":"w12-t14-fc1","front":"What does the Foundry agent optimizer change for prompt vs hosted agents?","back":"Prompt: instructions, function-calling tool descriptions, model selection. Hosted: also skills. Limited preview. Prompt candidates promote to a new version. Hosted candidates are applied locally and redeployed.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t14"},{"id":"w12-t14-fc2","front":"Which package replaces the deprecated Agent 365 Observability SDK?","back":"The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for .NET, Node.js and Python. It targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t14"},{"id":"w12-t15-fc1","front":"Where do an agent's credentials live in Entra Agent ID?","back":"On the blueprint, not on the agent identity. The identity is a service principal of type ServiceIdentity with no credentials.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t15"},{"id":"w12-t15-fc2","front":"Sponsor vs owner?","back":"Sponsor: required business accountability, can disable or soft-delete. Owner: optional technical admin (user or service principal), manages credentials and can re-enable or hard-delete.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t15"},{"id":"w12-t15-fc3","front":"Why Conditional Access for agents?","back":"Agents have no MFA. Conditional Access is evaluated at token issuance or refresh, with Continuous Access Evaluation for some resources.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t15"},{"id":"w12-t16-fc1","front":"OBO vs S2S token subjects?","back":"OBO: the user is the subject, the agent identity is the actor, with delegated permissions. S2S: the agent identity is the subject, with application permissions.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t16"},{"id":"w12-t16-fc2","front":"What defines an autopilot?","back":"Identity, not capabilities: an agent with its own agent user account (plus an agent identity). Without the account it is not an autopilot. Frontier preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t16"},{"id":"w12-t17-fc1","front":"Agent 365's three pillars and what backs them?","back":"Observe (registry, map), Govern (admin center, Entra, Purview), Secure (Entra risk-based access, Purview DLP, Defender). GA 2026-05-01, Commercial, per user.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t17"},{"id":"w12-t17-fc2","front":"Does Agent 365 host agents?","back":"No. It is a control plane. The SDK adds identity, governed Work IQ tools and notifications, but you build, host and run the agent elsewhere.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t17"},{"id":"w12-t17-fc3","front":"Agent 365 price?","back":"UNVERIFIED in this guide. Licensing is per user, included in Microsoft 365 E7, and an add-on to E5, A5 or Business Premium.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t17"},{"id":"w12-t18-fc1","front":"Four agent types in the Agent 365 registry?","back":"Microsoft agents, External partner-built agents, Published by your org, Shared by creator. One Copilot Studio agent can appear under more than one type by design.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t18"},{"id":"w12-t18-fc2","front":"Agent 365 registry vs Entra admin center?","back":"Registry: complete inventory, including agents without an Entra identity. Entra: agents with Agent IDs, blueprints, Conditional Access and governance.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t18"},{"id":"w12-t18-fc3","front":"Which Defender coverage needs separate onboarding?","back":"Local agents on endpoints (Defender for Endpoint in active mode) and Copilot Studio real-time protection (Power Platform admin, matching App ID).","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t18"},{"id":"w12-t19-fc1","front":"Decision order for the runtime choice?","back":"First: must code run outside Foundry? Then: who builds it (makers or developers)? Then: custom code, protocols or files needed? Prompt agent unless a requirement forces more.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t19"},{"id":"w12-t19-fc2","front":"How does a self-hosted agent get governance?","back":"Register it in Foundry Control Plane (API Management proxy), add the Agent 365 SDK, or use registry sync for connected platforms. You still host and run it.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t19"},{"id":"w12-t19-fc3","front":"Which runtime lacks a documented GA label as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Hosted agents: no separate GA row and no explicit GA statement found (UNVERIFIED). Voice-based agents are preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w12-t19"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Agents in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Agent types, tools, enterprise features and publishing in one page.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"What are hosted agents?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/hosted-agents","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Protocols, sessions, sandbox sizes, identity, scaling and regions. No separate GA label found on 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r3","title":"What is Toolbox in Foundry?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/toolbox-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Managed MCP endpoint, versioning, tool coverage. Tool search and skills are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Agent identity concepts in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/agent-identity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token exchange, shared versus distinct identity and RBAC for Foundry agents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"Publish agents to Microsoft Copilot and Microsoft Teams","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/publish-copilot","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Scopes, admin approval, roles and the private network caveat.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"What is Foundry IQ?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/what-is-foundry-iq","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Knowledge bases, sources, ACL sync. API GA depends on the Search REST version, portal access is preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r7","title":"Memory in Foundry Agent Service","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/what-is-memory","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Memory phases and types, and when not to use memory.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r8","title":"Guardrails and controls overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Four intervention points and which apply to agents. Agent guardrails are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r9","title":"Foundry portal general availability overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/general-availability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Feature readiness table and retirements.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"What's new in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/whats-new-foundry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Long-running agent articles, autopilot and optimizer. Newest items are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"Migrate hosted agents to the latest version","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/migrate-hosted-agent-preview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Exact versions and dates from the preview backend, plus the Workflows and classic agent retirements.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r12","title":"Overview of Microsoft Agent 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Pillars, GA on 2026-05-01 and licensing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r13","title":"Microsoft Agent 365 SDK overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/agent-365-sdk","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Integration paths and what the SDK does not do. Notifications need the Frontier preview program.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Agent identities in Microsoft Entra Agent ID","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/agent-id/agent-identities","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Anatomy of an agent identity and the blueprint model.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"Conditional Access for agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/identity/conditional-access/agent-id","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Subjects, evaluation points and licensing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Manage agent registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/admin/manage/agent-registry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Registry path, four registry types and admin actions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"Agent 365 identity for developers","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/identity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Object model, execution modes and agent user accounts (Frontier preview).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"Owners, sponsors and managers of agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/agent-id/agent-owners-sponsors-managers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Who holds business accountability and who holds technical control.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"Autopilot overview in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/autopilot-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Agents with an Entra agent user account. Preview status was not stated on the page.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"Microsoft Agent 365 is generally available","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/01/microsoft-agent-365-now-generally-available-expands-capabilities-and-integrations/","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft Security Blog","note":"GA announcement of 2026-05-01 with the GA and preview split by agent mode.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Add an A2A agent endpoint to a Foundry agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/agent-to-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The A2A tool and why Connected Agents is not in the new service.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Deployment types for Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Global, Data Zone, Standard, provisioned and batch deployment types.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r23","title":"Register a custom agent in Foundry Control Plane","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/control-plane/register-custom-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How an agent running elsewhere is proxied through API Management.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Foundry architecture","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Resource and project model, and the renamed Foundry roles.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Agent optimizer overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/agent-optimizer-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What the optimizer tunes and how candidates are promoted. Limited preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r26","title":"Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for Agent 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/microsoft-opentelemetry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Replacement for the deprecated Agent 365 Observability SDK.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r27","title":"Microsoft Entra ID Governance for agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/id-governance/agent-id-governance-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Access packages for agents and how each platform gets agent identities.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r28","title":"Develop AI agents on Azure","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/paths/develop-ai-agents-on-azure/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Hands-on learning path. Check each module for classic versus new service content.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r29","title":"Agent Registry convergence with Microsoft Agent 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/agent-id/agent-registry-convergence","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Agent 365 as the unified registry, Entra as the identity foundation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Build with runtime components in Foundry Agent Service","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/runtime-components","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Agents, conversations and responses, and the three ways to carry context between turns.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Resilience for long-running Microsoft Foundry hosted agents (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/long-running-agent-resilience","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Background execution versus resilient execution, work identity, leases, handler re-entry and stream replay.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Agent tracing overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/concepts/trace-agent-concept","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Tracing is GA for prompt and hosted agents and preview for workflow and external agents. Stored in Application Insights.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Microsoft Copilot Studio overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The low-code agent and workflow studio, its channels and harness choices. App creation is preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Best practices for voice-based agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/voice-agent-best-practice","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Latency, turn detection, interim responses and the 2000 ms latency_threshold_ms default for voice agents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Voice Live API overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/speech-service/voice-live","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Managed speech-to-speech with a Realtime-style WebSocket interface, locales and noise suppression.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"What's new in A2A v1.0","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/whats-new-v1/","type":"spec","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Migration list from A2A 0.3 to 1.0, useful when Foundry tool types a2a and a2a_preview differ.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Agentic retrieval overview (Azure AI Search)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The multi-query pipeline under Foundry IQ. LLM query planning is marked preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr9","title":"File search tool for Microsoft Foundry agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/file-search","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Vector stores, standard setup storage and the extra charges beyond tokens.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Use Code Interpreter with Microsoft Foundry agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/code-interpreter","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Sandboxed Python, session behavior (one hour, 30 minute idle) and extra charges.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr11","title":"Use web search tool in Foundry Agent Service","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/web-search","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Grounding with Bing terms, the data boundary note and domain-restricted search.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr12","title":"Host an A2A-compatible agent endpoint in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/enable-agent-to-agent-endpoint","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Incoming A2A on a Foundry agent: version, binding, modality, auth role and retention limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr13","title":"Use Microsoft Purview to manage data security and compliance for Microsoft Agent 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/purview/ai-agent-365","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Which Purview capabilities support agents and which are enabled automatically.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr14","title":"Enable security for AI agents using Microsoft Defender","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-xdr/security-for-ai/get-started-defender-security-for-ai","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Onboarding steps for Defender with Agent 365, the Microsoft 365 connector, Copilot Studio real-time protection and local agents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Prompt agent","def":"A Foundry agent defined as instructions, a model and tools. No code and no container."},{"term":"Hosted agent","def":"A Foundry agent that runs your code as a container image or .zip inside a per-session, VM-isolated sandbox with its own Entra agent identity."},{"term":"Ephemeral agent","def":"An agent defined in your code and called through the Responses API. No Foundry agent resource exists."},{"term":"Session (hosted agent)","def":"The unit that owns compute and state. It moves between Active, Idle and Resumed, persists `$HOME` and `/files`, and is deleted after 30 days of inactivity."},{"term":"Toolbox","def":"A versioned group of tools behind one managed MCP-compatible endpoint with central auth and guardrails."},{"term":"Foundry IQ","def":"A knowledge base layer on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval that can sync ACLs and honor Purview labels."},{"term":"Agent identity blueprint","def":"The Entra object that holds credentials and creates agent identities. It is a credential boundary."},{"term":"Agent identity","def":"A service principal of type `ServiceIdentity` that has no credentials. Its blueprint requests tokens for it."},{"term":"Agent user","def":"An optional user object marked as an AI agent, 1:1 with an agent identity, with a mailbox and Teams presence. Frontier preview."},{"term":"Sponsor","def":"The required business owner of an agent object. A sponsor can disable or soft-delete it but cannot edit auth settings or re-enable it."},{"term":"OBO","def":"On-behalf-of. The user is the token subject and the agent identity is the actor, so permissions are delegated."},{"term":"Agent 365","def":"The Microsoft 365 control plane to observe, govern and secure agents. GA on 2026-05-01. Its registry inventories agents in the admin center."},{"term":"Invocations protocol","def":"Hosted agent protocol for arbitrary JSON at `/invocations`. You define the schema, manage sessions and write raw SSE. Used for webhooks, batch work and custom streaming."},{"term":"Activity protocol","def":"The protocol for Teams and Microsoft 365 channel delivery. The platform bridges it from Responses when you publish."},{"term":"State store (hosted agents)","def":"A durable key-value store that survives crashes and idle eviction, can be partitioned per end user and ages items out after a 30 day idle window by default."},{"term":"Resilient execution","def":"Preview capability that lets a later process recover long-running work after a crash. Recovery reenters the handler from its beginning, so the handler needs checkpoints and idempotency."},{"term":"Tool search","def":"Preview toolbox feature that hides tools by default and exposes `tool_search` and `call_tool`, so large catalogs do not flood the context window."},{"term":"Knowledge base (Foundry IQ)","def":"A resource that combines knowledge sources with retrieval settings and runs on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval. Many agents can share one."},{"term":"Memory store","def":"Managed store of long-term memory items in Foundry (preview), scoped per user, with extraction, consolidation and retrieval phases."},{"term":"Publish scope","def":"Who can see and call a published Foundry agent: Individual (`Shared`, no approval) or Organization (`Tenant`, admin approval)."},{"term":"Autopilot","def":"A Foundry agent that has an agent user account in addition to its agent identity, so it acts as itself in Microsoft 365, including group chats."},{"term":"Agent registry","def":"The unified inventory of agents in the Microsoft 365 admin center (Agents, All Agents, Registry), part of Agent 365."},{"term":"Frontier preview program","def":"The Microsoft program tenants must join to use agent user accounts and Agent 365 notifications while they are in preview."},{"term":"Foundry Control Plane","def":"Foundry surface where you can register a custom agent that runs elsewhere. API Management sits in front as a proxy for access control and monitoring."}],"levels":[{"id":"w12-l1","order":1,"title":"Pick the agent's home","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"An agent needs somewhere to run. How much code you own, where that code may run and whether each user needs private files and compute are all open. Compare five homes and flip the dials.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five homes for one agent","level":2,"id":"five-homes-for-one-agent"},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry Agent Service has three agent types [[r1]]. A **prompt agent** is instructions, a model and tools as configuration, with no code. A **voice-based prompt agent** adds managed real-time voice over a WebSocket and is preview as of 2026-10-13. A **hosted agent** runs your own code as a container image, or as a .zip that Foundry builds into an image [[r2]].\n\nYou can also skip the agent resource. Call the Responses API directly and the definition lives in your code: an *ephemeral agent*. Two homes sit outside the service, self-hosting on AKS or Container Apps and Copilot Studio, and both can still be governed (level 6). Treat the choice as a dial problem, not a ranking.\n\nAll of it sits in one resource model: a Foundry resource (`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` with kind `AIServices`) holds projects, and a project is the development boundary. Connected Storage, Key Vault and AI Search keep their own governance [[r24]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Where the agent runs","intro":"Status labels were checked on 2026-10-13.","options":[{"name":"Prompt agent","tag":"GA","summary":"Instructions, model and tools as configuration. No code and no container.","pros":["Least to manage, per the agent types page","Supports private networking","No container to build or size"],"cons":["No custom orchestration or protocol surface","No per-session files or compute"],"bestWhen":"Instructions, a model and catalog tools are enough.","avoidWhen":"You need webhooks, AG-UI, voice sockets or files that persist across turns.","meta":[{"k":"You write","v":"Configuration"},{"k":"Status","v":"Agents core GA"}]},{"name":"Hosted agent","tag":"GA on v1 API","summary":"Your code in a per-session, VM-isolated sandbox with its own Entra agent identity and endpoint.","pros":["Python or C# with Agent Framework, LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Anthropic Agent SDK, GitHub Copilot SDK or custom code","Several protocols on one agent","Persisted `$HOME` and bring-your-own VNet"],"cons":["Billing follows active sessions, so concurrency times allocation drives cost","Some hosted features are still preview on 2026-10-13, such as long-running resilience","The initial-preview backend ended on 2026-08-20 and forced a redeploy"],"bestWhen":"You bring code, custom protocols or per-session state.","avoidWhen":"A prompt agent already covers the need, or the code must run outside Azure.","meta":[{"k":"You write","v":"Agent code"},{"k":"Cost driver","v":"CPU and memory of active sessions"}]},{"name":"Direct Responses call (ephemeral agent)","tag":"Pattern","summary":"Your app calls the Responses API. The definition ships with the app and no Foundry agent resource exists.","pros":["Definition versions with your app code","Runs wherever your app runs"],"cons":["Nothing for Foundry to version, publish or give an endpoint to","You own the loop, hosting and scaling"],"bestWhen":"The agent is a feature of one application.","avoidWhen":"Several teams must share, version and publish the agent as a unit.","meta":[{"k":"You write","v":"App plus definition"}]},{"name":"Self-host on AKS or Container Apps","tag":"Your ops","summary":"Run the agent on your own platform, on another cloud or on premises, then register it for governance.","pros":["Code and data can stay on another cloud or on premises","Registry sync, the Agent 365 SDK or Foundry Control Plane registration can still govern it"],"cons":["You run scaling, isolation, patching and identity wiring","Native integration paths give more than inventory; registration alone may not"],"bestWhen":"Residency or an existing platform forces the location.","avoidWhen":"No hard reason exists to leave Foundry.","meta":[{"k":"You write","v":"Agent plus platform"}]},{"name":"Copilot Studio agent","tag":"Low code","summary":"A maker-oriented platform. Its agents appear in the Agent 365 registry and can receive agent identities (preview).","pros":["Makers build without a code pipeline","Visible in the registry, sometimes under more than one type by design"],"cons":["Less control over orchestration and protocols than owned code (inference)","Automatic agent identity assignment is preview as of 2026-10-13"],"bestWhen":"Business teams own the agent and the tools are connector shaped.","avoidWhen":"You need custom protocols or sandboxed code execution.","meta":[{"k":"You write","v":"Low-code flows"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-l1-runtime","title":"Pick a runtime","prompt":"Flip the constraints. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"code","label":"Who owns the logic","values":[{"id":"cfg","label":"Configuration is enough"},{"id":"code","label":"Custom orchestration or protocols"},{"id":"low","label":"Makers, low code"}]},{"id":"state","label":"State and isolation","values":[{"id":"none","label":"Stateless turns"},{"id":"files","label":"Per-session files and compute"}]},{"id":"where","label":"Where it may run","values":[{"id":"foundry","label":"Inside Foundry is fine"},{"id":"away","label":"Another cloud or on premises"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prompt","name":"Prompt agent","summary":"Configuration only."},{"id":"hosted","name":"Hosted agent","summary":"Your code, per-session sandbox."},{"id":"eph","name":"Direct Responses call","summary":"Definition lives in your app."},{"id":"self","name":"Self-host and register","summary":"AKS, Container Apps, other clouds."},{"id":"studio","name":"Copilot Studio","summary":"Low-code maker platform."}],"scores":{"prompt":{"code":{"cfg":{"s":2,"why":"No code or container, the least to manage."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"No custom orchestration or protocols."},"low":{"s":0,"why":"Configuration, but not a maker surface."}},"state":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Stateless turns need no sandbox."},"files":{"s":-2,"why":"No per-session files or compute."}},"where":{"foundry":{"s":1,"why":"Managed in the service, private networking supported."},"away":{"s":-2,"why":"It runs only in Foundry."}}},"hosted":{"code":{"cfg":{"s":-1,"why":"Compute billed per active session is wasted."},"code":{"s":2,"why":"Your code and several protocols."},"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs a code pipeline."}},"state":{"none":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but the sandbox adds cost."},"files":{"s":2,"why":"Persisted `$HOME` and per-session compute."}},"where":{"foundry":{"s":1,"why":"Own endpoint, identity and BYO VNet."},"away":{"s":-1,"why":"The sandbox runs in Foundry, not on premises."}}},"eph":{"code":{"cfg":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you host the loop."},"code":{"s":1,"why":"You own orchestration inside the app."},"low":{"s":-2,"why":"It is code in an app."}},"state":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Stateless calls fit well."},"files":{"s":-1,"why":"You build file persistence yourself."}},"where":{"foundry":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, the app lives in your estate."},"away":{"s":1,"why":"Only model calls leave your environment."}}},"self":{"code":{"cfg":{"s":-2,"why":"Heavy for a configuration-only need."},"code":{"s":1,"why":"Full control, full operations."},"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs an engineering team."}},"state":{"none":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"files":{"s":1,"why":"You own storage and isolation."}},"where":{"foundry":{"s":-1,"why":"You rebuild what the service gives you."},"away":{"s":2,"why":"Code stays where it must, then you register it."}}},"studio":{"code":{"cfg":{"s":0,"why":"Possible, but a different audience."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"Limited orchestration and protocol control (inference)."},"low":{"s":2,"why":"Built for makers."}},"state":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Conversational turns fit."},"files":{"s":-2,"why":"No sandboxed per-session compute."}},"where":{"foundry":{"s":0,"why":"Runs in the Microsoft platform and shows in the registry."},"away":{"s":-1,"why":"Not a place for on-premises code."}}}},"takeaway":"Hosted agents win on code plus state, prompt agents win on least effort, self-hosting wins on location. Name the dial that decided it."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l1-d1","title":"Runtime decision path","alt":"A decision tree: custom code needed leads to a hosted agent when it can run in Foundry or self-hosting when it cannot. No custom code leads to a prompt agent.","src":"flowchart TB\n  Q{Custom code needed?} -->|No| P[Prompt agent]\n  Q -->|Yes| W{May run in Foundry?}\n  W -->|Yes| H[Hosted agent]\n  W -->|No| S[Self-host and register]\n  P --> G[Govern in Agent 365]\n  H --> G\n  S --> G","caption":"Governance is a separate decision from the runtime. All three paths can reach the control plane."},{"t":"table","caption":"Feature status map, checked 2026-10-13","head":["Feature","Status on 2026-10-13","Design consequence"],"rows":[["Agents (core), tracing for prompt and hosted agents","GA [[r9]]","Safe baseline"],["Hosted agents","GA on the v1 API with azure-ai-projects 2.3.0 or later; long-running resilience is preview [[r11]]","Check the label on each hosted feature before a regulated launch"],["Toolboxes","GA, with tool search and skills in preview [[r3]]","Use the toolbox, keep tool search optional"],["Publishing to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot","GA [[r5]]","Safe channel"],["Foundry IQ","API GA depends on the Search REST version, portal preview [[r6]]","Pin the API version"],["Foundry memory","Preview [[r7]]","Needs a preview waiver"],["Agent guardrails","Preview, model guardrails GA [[r8]]","Do not rely on agent-level controls alone"],["Agent optimizer","Limited preview [[r25]]","Treat as an experiment"],["Foundry Workflows","Preview, retires 2026-12-01 [[r11]]","Do not start new work on it"],["Classic agents","Retire 2027-03-31 [[r11]]","Plan a migration"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The bill grows with concurrency on a light agent","cause":"A hosted agent sized above its need, billed on CPU and memory of every active session","fix":"Use a prompt agent if no code is needed, or move to a smaller sandbox. Detect it by watching peak CPU against allocation."},{"symptom":"The portal cannot publish the agent to Teams","cause":"Portal publishing is not supported when the project has public network access disabled","fix":"Publish through the REST API with `enable_m365_public_endpoint`."},{"symptom":"Role assignments from an older runbook do not match the portal","cause":"Azure AI User, Azure AI Owner, Azure AI Account Owner and Azure AI Project Manager were renamed Foundry User, Foundry Owner, Foundry Account Owner and Foundry Project Manager","fix":"The role IDs did not change. Update the runbook names [[r24]]."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Do not say 'hosted agents for everything'. Name what forces code, then name the cost: per-session compute and a preview boundary you checked on a date."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Requirements change: each user session needs files that persist across turns, a custom Invocations endpoint and compute isolated per session. Which home fits best?","options":["Prompt agent","Hosted agent","Copilot Studio agent","Direct Responses call from a stateless web app"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hosted agents give a per-session VM-isolated sandbox, a persisted `$HOME` and several protocols. A prompt agent has no code or sandbox. Copilot Studio is a maker platform without per-session sandboxes. A stateless app would have to build file persistence and isolation itself.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A team cannot publish a hosted agent to Teams from the Foundry portal. Which cause fits the project setting shown?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"project.publicNetworkAccess = Disabled\nportal publish -> not supported"},"options":["The agent exposes no A2A endpoint, which Teams requires before any publishing can start in the portal","The Azure Bot Service provider is registered twice in the subscription","Portal publishing is unsupported with public network access disabled, so use the REST API route","Prompt agents and hosted agents cannot share one project"],"answer":2,"explain":"The publish guide states portal publishing is unsupported when public network access is disabled and points to the REST API with `enable_m365_public_endpoint`. Teams does not need A2A, since the Activity protocol is bridged from Responses. A double provider registration is not a documented cause. Projects can hold both agent types.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"},{"id":"w12-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every statement that holds on 2026-10-13.","options":["A prompt agent needs no container image.","A direct Responses API call creates a Foundry agent resource you can publish.","A hosted agent can be built from a container image or from a .zip.","The Foundry GA table lists hosted agents on their own GA row."],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Prompt agents are configuration only, and hosted agents accept a container image or a .zip that Foundry builds. A direct Responses call is an ephemeral agent with no agent resource, so there is nothing to publish. The migration page states hosted agents are generally available on the GA v1 API (azure-ai-projects 2.3.0 or later), but the GA table has no separate hosted row, so the table row claim is not supported.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r5","r6","r7","r8","r9","r11","r24","r25"],"takeaways":["Runtime is a dial problem: logic ownership, state and location","Hosted agents buy code and per-session state, and cost scales with active sessions","No separate hosted row exists in the GA table, though the migration page says hosted agents are GA on the v1 API","Governance is a separate decision from the runtime"]},{"id":"w12-l2","order":2,"title":"Inside the sandbox","kind":"concept","minutes":14,"brief":"A hosted agent's compute follows the session, not the request. Idle timeout, sandbox size and where state lives are dials you set before launch, and each one moves cost, latency or data loss.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Compute follows the session","level":2,"id":"compute-follows-the-session"},{"t":"p","md":"In a hosted agent, compute follows the *session*, not the request [[r2]]. A session is **Active** while it serves traffic, goes **Idle** after the idle timeout and is **Resumed** by the next request. `$HOME` and `/files` are persisted on idle and restored on resume. The idle timeout is set per agent version from 2 to 60 minutes, default 15. A session is deleted permanently after 30 days of inactivity.\n\nScale is per session, with no replica count and no warm pool. Billing follows the CPU and memory consumed across active sessions, so oversizing multiplies cost by concurrency. The concept page gives no numeric rates and links to the Foundry Agent Service pricing page, so model cost from that page, not from this guide."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l2-d1","title":"Hosted session lifecycle","alt":"A session starts Active, becomes Idle after the idle timeout, is Resumed by a new request and returns to Active. After 30 days idle it is deleted.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Active\n  Active --> Idle: idle timeout\n  Idle --> Resumed: new request\n  Resumed --> Active: state restored\n  Idle --> Deleted: 30 days inactive\n  Deleted --> [*]","caption":"Checked against the hosted agents concept page on 2026-10-13."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where state can live","head":["Store","Scope","Survives idle","Survives a crash","Ages out"],"rows":[["`$HOME` and `/files`","One session","Yes, persisted on idle and restored on resume","Not stated on the concept page","With the session, after 30 days inactive"],["Durable key-value state store","Can partition per end user","Yes","Yes","Items age out after a 30 day idle window by default"],["Your own database or storage","Whatever you design","Yes","Yes","Your retention policy (inference)"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"formula","title":"Active allocation that drives the bill","expr":"allocated_GiB = active_sessions x GiB_per_session","vars":[{"k":"active_sessions","v":"sessions that are Active at the same time, not Idle"},{"k":"size","v":"0.5 vCPU with 1 GiB, 1 vCPU with 2 GiB, or 2 vCPU with 4 GiB"},{"k":"disk","v":"up to 20 GiB at 1 vCPU or larger, about 20 percent reserved, shared with the image and `$HOME`"}],"example":"200 active sessions at 2 vCPU and 4 GiB hold **400 vCPU and 800 GiB**. The same load at 1 vCPU and 2 GiB holds 200 vCPU and 400 GiB. The docs advise the next size up only when sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Uploaded files vanish after about five weeks","cause":"The session was inactive for 30 days and was deleted, taking `$HOME` with it","fix":"Write durable artifacts to storage you own. Alert on sessions that approach 30 idle days (inference)."},{"symptom":"Users see each other's saved preferences","cause":"The key-value state store was not partitioned per end user","fix":"Partition by end user id and test with two users in one agent."},{"symptom":"The first message after a quiet period is slow","cause":"The session moved to Idle and state had to be restored on resume (inference)","fix":"Measure resume latency. A longer idle timeout trades resume latency against more active-session time."},{"symptom":"A deployment stops serving after 2026-08-20","cause":"Support for the initial-preview hosted agent backend ended and routing moved to a dedicated endpoint per agent","fix":"Redeploy on the session-sandbox model and replace the old adapter packages with `azure-ai-agentserver-responses` or `azure-ai-agentserver-invocations` [[r11]]."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"bash","title":"Tooling floor for the migration","code":"pip install \"azure-ai-projects>=2.3.0\"\nazd version\nazd ext install azure.ai.agents","caption":"Minimum versions from the migration guide: azure-ai-projects 2.3.0 and azd 1.23.0, checked 2026-10-13."},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: versions are immutable snapshots","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Each create-version call snapshots the image, resources, environment variables and protocol configuration [[r2]]. An endpoint serves one version and sends 100 percent of traffic to it. Traffic splitting is not supported, so a canary needs a second agent name or a client-side router (inference)."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: long-running work is preview","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The 2026-08 Foundry articles on resilience for long-running hosted agents, crash recovery, steering an in-flight turn, stream with reconnect and a human-in-the-loop approval step were all marked preview on 2026-10-13 [[r10]]. **Background mode** keeps work going after the request returns. **Resilient execution** keeps work going after the hosting process stops. They solve different problems, so name which failure you are covering."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the model deployment is a separate dial","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The agent's model has its own deployment type [[r22]]. `GlobalStandard` is the docs' starting point and may process in any region. `DataZoneStandard` stays inside the US, EU or APAC zone. `Standard` stays inside one geography. `GlobalBatch` gives a 50 percent discount with a 24 hour target. Hosted agents were listed in 31 regions on 2026-10-13, and the list is stated to grow."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A fleet holds 120 active sessions at the 1 vCPU and 2 GiB size. How many GiB of memory are allocated across the active sessions?","answer":240,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"GiB","hint":"allocated_GiB = active_sessions x GiB_per_session","explain":"120 sessions x 2 GiB = 240 GiB. Billing follows CPU and memory consumed across active sessions, so moving to the 2 vCPU and 4 GiB size would double this to 480 GiB at the same concurrency. Idle sessions do not count as active.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Support reports that a user's uploaded working files are gone. The user last opened the agent five weeks earlier. Which cause fits?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"session s-81f2 last active: 35 days ago\nstatus: not found"},"options":["The 2 to 60 minute idle timeout expired and `$HOME` was wiped on idle","The endpoint served a newer version and discarded files from the older one","The files were stored in `/files`, which is deleted on every resume","A session is deleted after 30 days of inactivity and takes its state with it"],"answer":3,"explain":"Idle persists `$HOME` and `/files` and resume restores them, so the idle timeout is not the cause. Versions are immutable snapshots of image, resources and config, not a store that wipes files. `/files` uploads land in `$HOME` and are restored on resume. The 30 day inactivity limit matches 35 days.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-l2-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the life of one hosted session in order.","items":["Requests arrive and the session is Active","No traffic for the idle timeout, so the session is Idle and state is persisted","A new request arrives and the session is Resumed with state restored","No activity for 30 days and the session is permanently deleted"],"explain":"State is persisted when the session goes Idle and restored when it resumes. Permanent deletion is the last step and only follows 30 days of inactivity.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r10","r11","r22"],"takeaways":["Compute follows the session: Active, Idle, Resumed, then deleted after 30 days inactive","Cost is active sessions times allocation, so right-size only after peaks pass about 70 percent","An endpoint serves one immutable version, so traffic splitting needs another design","Durable data belongs in a store you own, with per-user partitioning"]},{"id":"w12-l3","order":3,"title":"Open the right door","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"Callers speak different languages: a chat UI, a webhook, a phone line, another agent, Teams. A hosted agent can expose several protocols, so decide who owns the conversation and which channel the callers use.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five protocols, one agent","level":2,"id":"five-protocols-one-agent"},{"t":"p","md":"A hosted agent can expose several protocols at once [[r2]]. Choose by who owns the conversation and what the caller sends. The docs' default advice is to start with Responses and add Invocations later. Each hosted agent gets its own endpoint at deploy time, so programmatic callers need no publishing step. Publishing is for people in Teams or Microsoft 365 Copilot [[r5]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Hosted agent protocols, checked 2026-10-13","head":["Protocol","Conversation state","Client","Use for","Price of choosing it"],"rows":[["Responses","Platform keeps history","Any OpenAI-compatible SDK","Chatbots, RAG with tools, background work with `background: true`, Teams and Microsoft 365 publishing","Fixed event shape"],["Invocations","You manage sessions","Raw HTTP, with SSE you write","Webhooks, non-conversational batch or extraction, custom streaming such as AG-UI, protocol bridges","You build history and the streaming format"],["Invocations (WebSocket)","You manage sessions","WebSocket client, paired with Pipecat, LiveKit or Voice Live inside the container","Real-time voice","Two-way streaming and reconnect logic"],["Activity","Bridged from Responses","Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot","Chat inside Microsoft 365","Not chosen directly, it follows publishing"],["A2A","Task based, between agents","Another agent","Peer agents. v1.0 is GA, v0.3 is preview","A contract and trust surface to keep stable"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-l3-protocol","title":"Pick a protocol","prompt":"Change the caller, the history owner and the channel.","dials":[{"id":"caller","label":"Who calls","values":[{"id":"ui","label":"Chat UI or OpenAI-compatible SDK"},{"id":"hook","label":"Webhook, batch or custom stream"},{"id":"voice","label":"Real-time voice client"},{"id":"peer","label":"Another agent"}]},{"id":"history","label":"Conversation history","values":[{"id":"platform","label":"Platform keeps it"},{"id":"own","label":"I keep sessions"}]},{"id":"channel","label":"Channel","values":[{"id":"teams","label":"Teams or Microsoft 365"},{"id":"none","label":"No Microsoft 365 channel"}]}],"options":[{"id":"resp","name":"Responses","summary":"Platform history, OpenAI-compatible."},{"id":"inv","name":"Invocations","summary":"Your payload, your SSE."},{"id":"ws","name":"Invocations WebSocket","summary":"Two-way streaming for voice."},{"id":"a2a","name":"A2A","summary":"Peer agents, task based."}],"scores":{"resp":{"caller":{"ui":{"s":2,"why":"Any OpenAI-compatible SDK is the client."},"hook":{"s":-1,"why":"A webhook payload does not fit a conversation shape."},"voice":{"s":-2,"why":"No two-way audio stream."},"peer":{"s":0,"why":"Works as a tool call, but A2A models tasks."}},"history":{"platform":{"s":2,"why":"The platform keeps conversation history."},"own":{"s":-1,"why":"You would fight the managed history."}},"channel":{"teams":{"s":2,"why":"Activity is bridged from Responses on publish."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"No channel advantage."}}},"inv":{"caller":{"ui":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you build what Responses gives."},"hook":{"s":2,"why":"Built for webhooks, batch and custom streams."},"voice":{"s":-1,"why":"Request and response, not a socket."},"peer":{"s":0,"why":"Possible, but not a peer contract."}},"history":{"platform":{"s":-2,"why":"You manage sessions yourself."},"own":{"s":2,"why":"Full control of sessions and SSE."}},"channel":{"teams":{"s":-2,"why":"Activity bridges from Responses, not Invocations."},"none":{"s":1,"why":"Free to shape the payload."}}},"ws":{"caller":{"ui":{"s":-2,"why":"A chat UI does not need a socket."},"hook":{"s":-1,"why":"Overkill for one-shot events."},"voice":{"s":2,"why":"Real-time voice with Pipecat, LiveKit or Voice Live."},"peer":{"s":-2,"why":"Not a peer protocol."}},"history":{"platform":{"s":-1,"why":"Sessions are yours to keep."},"own":{"s":1,"why":"Matches a stateful audio stream."}},"channel":{"teams":{"s":-2,"why":"A real-time audio socket is not a Teams chat path (inference)."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"a2a":{"caller":{"ui":{"s":-1,"why":"Not a chat client contract."},"hook":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"voice":{"s":-2,"why":"Not an audio protocol."},"peer":{"s":2,"why":"Built for agent to agent tasks."}},"history":{"platform":{"s":0,"why":"Task state is protocol defined."},"own":{"s":0,"why":"Task state is protocol defined."}},"channel":{"teams":{"s":-1,"why":"Not the Teams path."},"none":{"s":1,"why":"Fits cross-team calls with no channel."}}}},"takeaway":"Responses is the default and the only route to Teams. Leave it when the payload, the stream or the caller is not a conversation."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","title":"Endpoint shapes","code":"{endpoint} = {project_endpoint}/agents/{name}/endpoint\n\nResponses:   {endpoint}/protocols/openai/responses\nInvocations: {endpoint}/protocols/invocations\nA2A:         {endpoint}/protocols/a2a\nWebSocket:   wss://{account}.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/{project}/agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/invocations_ws?api-version=v1","caption":"From the hosted agents concept page, checked 2026-10-13. Declare protocols in azure.yaml (service azure.ai.agent) with azd, or with protocol_versions in the SDK."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l3-d1","title":"Teams message to hosted agent","alt":"A Teams user message goes to Azure Bot Service, which bridges the Activity to the Responses endpoint of the hosted agent and returns the reply.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as Teams user\n  participant B as Azure Bot Service\n  participant A as Hosted agent\n  U->>B: Message\n  B->>A: Activity bridged to Responses\n  A-->>B: Reply\n  B-->>U: Reply","caption":"Publishing creates the Azure Bot Service resource and users keep a stable endpoint while you roll versions behind it."},{"t":"compare","title":"Publishing scope","intro":"Publishing to Microsoft Copilot and Teams is GA as of 2026-10-13.","options":[{"name":"Individual scope","tag":"GA","summary":"Previously called Shared (`publishScope` Shared). No admin approval.","pros":["Ships a pilot without waiting for approval"],"cons":["Not listed under Built by your org"],"bestWhen":"A pilot or a small group needs the agent this week.","avoidWhen":"The whole organization needs a managed listing."},{"name":"Organization scope","tag":"GA","summary":"`publishScope` Tenant. An admin approves it in the Microsoft 365 admin center, then it is listed under Built by your org.","pros":["Managed company-wide listing","Admin review before wide exposure"],"cons":["Waits for admin approval"],"bestWhen":"The agent is production and widely used.","avoidWhen":"You are still iterating on a prototype."},{"name":"No publishing","summary":"Call the agent's own endpoint programmatically.","pros":["No Bot Service resource","No approval step"],"cons":["No Teams or Microsoft 365 Copilot presence"],"bestWhen":"Callers are services, apps or other agents.","avoidWhen":"People expect to chat with it in Teams."}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"A voice client reconnects and the assistant forgets the conversation","cause":"Invocations (WebSocket) leaves session state to you (inference from the protocol rules)","fix":"Persist state in `$HOME` or the key-value store, keyed by a session id the client resends."},{"symptom":"Organization publish stays pending","cause":"The agent waits for admin approval in the Microsoft 365 admin center","fix":"Request the approval, and use Individual scope for the pilot meanwhile."},{"symptom":"Publishing fails with an authorization error","cause":"A missing role (Azure Bot Service Contributor, Contributor or Owner, plus Foundry User on the project) or an unregistered `Microsoft.BotService` provider","fix":"Grant the roles and register the provider before you publish [[r5]]."},{"symptom":"A peer agent cannot call the endpoint","cause":"The peer speaks a different A2A version. On hosted agents v1.0 is GA and v0.3 is preview","fix":"Declare the version you serve and test the peer against it."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A billing system posts JSON events to the agent and expects its own streaming format. There is no Microsoft 365 channel and the caller keeps its own sessions. Which protocol fits?","options":["Invocations","Responses","Invocations (WebSocket)","A2A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Invocations is for webhooks, batch work and custom streaming, and you manage sessions. Responses forces a conversation shape and platform-kept history. WebSocket is for real-time voice. A2A is a peer-agent task protocol, not a webhook receiver.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-l3-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each caller to the protocol the docs point to.","pairs":[{"l":"Chat UI with platform-kept history and Teams publishing","r":"Responses"},{"l":"Webhook or batch extraction with a custom payload","r":"Invocations"},{"l":"Real-time voice client with Voice Live in the container","r":"Invocations (WebSocket)"},{"l":"Agent owned by another team","r":"A2A"}],"explain":"Responses keeps history and bridges to Activity for Teams. Invocations hands payload and sessions to you. The WebSocket variant carries real-time audio. A2A models tasks between peer agents.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A team wants a pilot in Teams this week and a managed listing for the whole company next quarter. Which sequence matches how the scopes work?","options":["Organization scope first, because Individual scope needs a tenant-wide admin approval before anyone can use the agent in Teams","Individual scope for both phases, because Organization scope is reserved for Microsoft-built agents","Individual scope for the pilot, then Organization scope with admin approval and a Built by your org listing","No publishing at all, because the Activity protocol works without an Azure Bot Service resource"],"answer":2,"explain":"Individual scope needs no admin approval, which suits a pilot. Organization scope (`publishScope` Tenant) needs approval in the Microsoft 365 admin center and then lists under Built by your org. Individual scope does not need tenant approval, Organization scope is not Microsoft-only, and publishing creates the Azure Bot Service resource that carries Activity.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r5"],"takeaways":["Start with Responses, add Invocations or WebSocket when the caller is not a chat","Only Responses bridges to Activity for Teams and Microsoft 365","Individual scope ships a pilot, Organization scope needs admin approval","A2A v1.0 is GA and v0.3 is preview on hosted agents, so state the version you serve"]},{"id":"w12-l4","order":4,"title":"Hands and a library","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"The agent needs tools, knowledge and sometimes other agents. Where each one lives decides who holds the credentials, who versions the definition and where the tokens are spent.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Tools, agents and knowledge","level":2,"id":"tools-agents-and-knowledge"},{"t":"p","md":"A **Toolbox** groups tools behind one managed MCP-compatible endpoint with central auth (Microsoft Entra, OAuth identity passthrough), toolbox-level guardrails and versioning [[r3]]. You create a version, test it, promote it to default and consuming agents pick it up with no redeploy. Any MCP client can consume it. Agent Framework hosted agents use `FoundryToolbox` in Python or `AddFoundryToolboxes` in .NET.\n\nTool authentication can be key based, Microsoft Entra (agent identity or project managed identity), OAuth identity passthrough (on-behalf-of) or unauthenticated where that is appropriate [[r1]]. These options also apply to remote MCP servers."},{"t":"compare","title":"How a tool reaches the agent","intro":"Toolboxes are GA as of 2026-10-13. Tool search and skills inside them are preview.","options":[{"name":"Toolbox endpoint","tag":"GA","summary":"Many tools behind one managed MCP-compatible endpoint with central auth, guardrails and versions.","pros":["One credential and governance point","Versioned promotion with no consumer redeploy","Non-Foundry MCP clients can consume it"],"cons":["Some tools are not toolbox eligible","A promoted default reaches every consumer at once"],"bestWhen":"Several agents share tools and you want central versioning.","avoidWhen":"One agent uses one tool and nothing is shared.","meta":[{"k":"Auth","v":"Entra or OAuth passthrough"},{"k":"Context cost","v":"Lower with tool search (preview)"}]},{"name":"Direct tool integration","summary":"Attach a built-in tool straight to the agent.","pros":["The only route for computer use, SharePoint, image generation, Azure Functions, Grounding with Bing and Fabric data agent"],"cons":["Credentials and guardrails live per agent","No shared versioning (inference)"],"bestWhen":"The tool is not toolbox eligible.","avoidWhen":"Many agents repeat the same wiring."},{"name":"Client-side function calling","summary":"The model asks, your process runs the function.","pros":["Execution and data stay in your process"],"cons":["You own the loop and retries","The agent optimizer cannot evaluate tool execution because it runs client side"],"bestWhen":"Execution must stay inside your own trust boundary.","avoidWhen":"The tool is a shared, governed capability."},{"name":"Plain remote MCP server","summary":"Point an agent at an MCP server and pick an auth option.","pros":["Any MCP server works","Key, Entra or OAuth passthrough auth"],"cons":["Every agent wires auth itself","Catalog sprawl across agents (inference)"],"bestWhen":"One agent needs one external MCP server.","avoidWhen":"You want a governed catalog with versions."}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l4-d1","title":"Tool paths into one agent","alt":"The agent reaches a toolbox MCP endpoint that fronts an MCP server, Azure AI Search, OpenAPI and an A2A peer. It also calls functions in its own process and attaches direct tools.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent] --> T[Toolbox MCP endpoint]\n  T --> M[Remote MCP server]\n  T --> S[Azure AI Search]\n  T --> O[OpenAPI tool]\n  T --> P[A2A peer]\n  A --> F[Function call in your process]\n  A --> D[Direct tool such as SharePoint]","caption":"Checked against the Toolbox overview on 2026-10-13."},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-l4-knowledge","title":"Foundry IQ or something else","prompt":"Change what the content is, who may see it and how much control you need.","dials":[{"id":"content","label":"What the content is","values":[{"id":"org","label":"Curated org content, many sources"},{"id":"user","label":"Documents a user uploads in chat"},{"id":"facts","label":"Per-user preferences and history"}]},{"id":"perm","label":"Permission model","values":[{"id":"open","label":"Same access for everyone"},{"id":"acl","label":"Per-document ACLs and labels"}]},{"id":"control","label":"Control you need","values":[{"id":"managed","label":"Managed defaults are fine"},{"id":"custom","label":"Own chunking, ranking and eval"}]}],"options":[{"id":"iq","name":"Foundry IQ knowledge base","summary":"Managed agentic retrieval over sources."},{"id":"search","name":"Azure AI Search tool","summary":"Your own index behind a tool."},{"id":"file","name":"File search","summary":"Retrieval over user documents."},{"id":"custom","name":"Custom retrieval behind MCP","summary":"Your pipeline as a tool."},{"id":"memory","name":"Foundry memory (preview)","summary":"Per-user extracted memories."}],"scores":{"iq":{"content":{"org":{"s":2,"why":"Sources include Blob Storage, SharePoint, OneLake and the public web."},"user":{"s":-1,"why":"Built for curated content, not per-chat uploads."},"facts":{"s":-2,"why":"Not a user memory store."}},"perm":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but ACL sync is wasted."},"acl":{"s":2,"why":"Syncs ACLs for supported sources, honors Purview labels and can run as the caller."}},"control":{"managed":{"s":2,"why":"Agentic retrieval with minimal, low or medium effort."},"custom":{"s":-1,"why":"Fewer knobs than your own pipeline."}}},"search":{"content":{"org":{"s":1,"why":"Good when you already own the index."},"user":{"s":-1,"why":"An index per chat upload is heavy."},"facts":{"s":-2,"why":"Not a memory store."}},"perm":{"open":{"s":1,"why":"Simple when everyone sees everything."},"acl":{"s":0,"why":"You design the security filters yourself."}},"control":{"managed":{"s":0,"why":"You operate the index."},"custom":{"s":1,"why":"Full control of the index."}}},"file":{"content":{"org":{"s":-1,"why":"Not aimed at large curated corpora."},"user":{"s":2,"why":"The docs point here for user documents."},"facts":{"s":-1,"why":"Documents, not preferences."}},"perm":{"open":{"s":1,"why":"Fine for content the user brought."},"acl":{"s":-1,"why":"No per-document ACL sync (inference)."}},"control":{"managed":{"s":2,"why":"Least setup."},"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"Few retrieval knobs (inference)."}}},"custom":{"content":{"org":{"s":1,"why":"Handles odd sources."},"user":{"s":0,"why":"Possible, but heavy for uploads."},"facts":{"s":-1,"why":"Reinvents memory."}},"perm":{"open":{"s":-1,"why":"More build than the need."},"acl":{"s":1,"why":"Enforce any rule at query time."}},"control":{"managed":{"s":-2,"why":"You build and run everything."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Your chunking, ranking and eval."}}},"memory":{"content":{"org":{"s":-2,"why":"Per-user and LLM-consolidated, wrong for shared policy documents (inference)."},"user":{"s":-1,"why":"Different job: preferences and summaries."},"facts":{"s":2,"why":"User profile, chat summary and procedural memory."}},"perm":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"acl":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"control":{"managed":{"s":1,"why":"Extraction and consolidation are managed."},"custom":{"s":-1,"why":"Few knobs and still preview."}}}},"takeaway":"Foundry IQ leads for curated, permission-aware content. Custom retrieval wins when you need your own ranking and eval. Memory is for the user, not for the policy library."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Prompt tokens balloon after the tool catalog grows","cause":"Hundreds of tool definitions travel in every request","fix":"Turn on tool search (preview). Watch prompt tokens per request to detect it."},{"symptom":"A new toolbox version breaks several agents at once","cause":"Promoting a version to default reaches every consuming agent with no redeploy","fix":"Test the version first, promote deliberately and keep the previous version to roll back (inference)."},{"symptom":"The agent cites a document the user may not read","cause":"The knowledge base ran under a shared identity, or the source has no ACL sync","fix":"Run under the caller's Entra identity and confirm ACL sync for each source [[r6]]."},{"symptom":"Delegation to a sibling agent fails after migration","cause":"The classic Connected Agents tool is not in the new Foundry Agent Service","fix":"Use the A2A tool, or Microsoft Agent Framework orchestration in hosted code [[r21]]."},{"symptom":"Memory returns stale or wrong policy answers","cause":"Memory is per-user and LLM-consolidated, so it is the wrong store for shared documents (inference)","fix":"Put shared documents in Foundry IQ or file search and keep memory for user facts."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: what a toolbox can and cannot hold","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Toolbox coverage on 2026-10-13","head":["In a toolbox","Direct integration only"],"rows":[["MCP, web search, Azure AI Search, code interpreter, file search, OpenAPI","Function calling (client side)"],["A2A, browser automation, Fabric IQ, Work IQ","Grounding with Bing, computer use, image generation"],["Tool search (preview), skills (preview), reminder tool","SharePoint, Fabric data agent, Azure Functions"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Logic Apps does not appear in the toolbox table, and I could not confirm it as a built-in agent tool on 2026-10-13. Check the page before you promise it."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: tool search and skills (preview)","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Tool search hides tools by default and exposes two meta-tools, `tool_search` and `call_tool`, with pin, add-context and auto-pin controls. A toolbox can then hold hundreds of tools without flooding context. **Skills** are versioned, immutable, reusable multi-step workflows delivered through MCP resources. Both work only through a toolbox [[r3]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: agent to agent after Connected Agents","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The classic Connected Agents tool is not available in the new service [[r21]]. The docs name two replacements: the A2A tool, which accepts any A2A endpoint including another Foundry agent exposed as an A2A endpoint, and Workflows. Workflows are preview and retire on 2026-12-01, and the GA page says to use Microsoft Agent Framework for new development [[r11]]. A Foundry agent can also be exposed as an incoming A2A endpoint."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: Foundry IQ and memory","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"A Foundry IQ knowledge base holds knowledge sources and retrieval settings and runs on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval [[r6]]. Retrieval reasoning effort is minimal, low or medium. Fabric IQ and Work IQ are sibling workloads. The API is GA by Search REST API version and portal access is preview as of 2026-10-13.\n\nFoundry memory is preview [[r7]]. It extracts, consolidates (an LLM merges and resolves conflicts) and retrieves. The three types are user profile, chat summary and procedural, all on by default. The docs advise retrieving the user profile early and the chat summary per turn."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l4-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every tool that is direct-integration only and cannot sit inside a toolbox on 2026-10-13.","options":["Computer use","Azure AI Search","SharePoint","Code interpreter","Azure Functions"],"answers":[0,2,4],"explain":"Computer use, SharePoint and Azure Functions are direct only, along with Grounding with Bing, image generation, the Fabric data agent and client-side function calling. Azure AI Search and code interpreter are both toolbox tools.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w12-l4-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Foundry Workflows (preview) retire on 2026-12-01. How many days remain from 2026-10-13 to the retirement date?","answer":49,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"days","hint":"Count the rest of October, all of November and the first of December.","explain":"October 13 to October 31 is 18 days, November adds 30 and December 1 adds 1, so 49 days. Use that window to move delegation to the A2A tool or Agent Framework orchestration.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r11"},{"id":"w12-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After adding many catalog tools, requests carry most of their tokens in tool definitions and answers get worse. What change fits?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"prompt_tokens=38200 tool_definitions=31000\ntools_attached=240"},"options":["Lower the temperature so the model is less likely to pick tools it does not need on each turn","Switch every tool to client-side function calling","Raise the Foundry IQ reasoning effort to medium","Turn on tool search (preview) so tools sit behind `tool_search` and `call_tool`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tool search hides tools by default and exposes two meta-tools, so definitions stop flooding context. Temperature does not shrink the tool list, client-side calling still sends definitions, and Foundry IQ effort concerns retrieval, not tools.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w12-l4-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which store fits the statement 'prefers metric units and a formal tone, remembered across chats' as of 2026-10-13?","options":["Foundry memory, using the user profile type, if the team accepts its preview status","Foundry IQ, because a knowledge base syncs ACLs for every source it connects to and honors Purview labels","File search over whatever documents the user uploaded in the last chat","A toolbox skill, because skills are versioned reusable workflows that persist"],"answer":0,"explain":"User profile memory holds preferences across sessions and is preview. Foundry IQ serves curated org content, file search serves user documents and skills are workflows delivered through MCP resources, not user facts.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r3","r6","r7","r11","r21"],"takeaways":["A toolbox centralizes auth, guardrails and versions, and a promotion reaches every consumer","Some tools are direct only, so check eligibility before promising one catalog","Connected Agents is gone in the new service, and Workflows retire on 2026-12-01","Foundry IQ is for curated permission-aware content, memory is for the user"]},{"id":"w12-l5","order":5,"title":"Who is this agent?","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"An agent calls APIs, reads mail and may join a group chat. Someone must be accountable and each credential must have a boundary. Choose the identity mode and the credential boundary before the permissions.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Objects, modes and accountability","level":2,"id":"objects-modes-and-accountability"},{"t":"p","md":"Agent 365 uses Entra Agent ID to give each agent its own identity [[r17]]. Three objects make up the model, and not every agent uses all three. The **agent identity blueprint** is a template that holds the credentials (federated identity credentials, certificates or keys, client secrets), the permissions and the verified publisher. The **agent identity** is a service principal with `servicePrincipalType` `ServiceIdentity` and `@odata.type` `#microsoft.graph.agentIdentity`, and it has no credentials of its own. The optional **agent user** is a user account marked as an AI agent, 1:1 with its identity, available only to tenants in the Frontier preview program.\n\nOne blueprint creates many identities, so an administrator can apply Conditional Access, revoke a grant or disable every agent of a kind in one step [[r14]]. Agent identities are single tenant. Blueprints can be multitenant."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l5-d1","title":"Blueprint, identities and sponsor","alt":"A sponsor is accountable for a blueprint. The blueprint holds the credentials and creates several agent identities. One identity may have an optional agent user.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[Sponsor] --> B[Blueprint]\n  B --> C[Credentials held here]\n  B --> I1[Agent identity A]\n  B --> I2[Agent identity B]\n  I1 --> U[Agent user, optional]","caption":"The blueprint is the credential boundary, so group only identities that can share credentials and baseline permissions."},{"t":"compare","title":"Three execution modes","intro":"Choose the mode first, then confirm permissions and consent. Source checked on 2026-10-13.","options":[{"name":"S2S (agent acts as itself)","tag":"GA","summary":"No user context. Application permissions, and the agent identity is the token subject.","pros":["Fits scheduled tasks, monitoring and background work","No user needs to be present"],"cons":["Access is not bounded by any user's permissions","Application permissions can be broad if granted carelessly"],"bestWhen":"A job runs on a schedule or an event, with its own scope of work.","avoidWhen":"Access must follow the signed-in user."},{"name":"OBO (on behalf of a user)","tag":"GA","summary":"The signed-in user is the token subject and the agent identity is the actor. Delegated permissions.","pros":["Access is bounded by the user's permissions","Clear accountability to one person"],"cons":["Needs a user present","No single right user in a group chat or an event-triggered run"],"bestWhen":"A chat assistant acts for one person.","avoidWhen":"The context is a team or no user is signed in."},{"name":"Agentic-User (own user account)","tag":"Preview (Frontier)","summary":"The agent acts as its own Entra user account with a mailbox, Teams presence and Word or Outlook interactions.","pros":["Can act as a team member in group chats","Has its own mailbox and presence"],"cons":["Needs the Frontier preview program","One more account and license to govern"],"bestWhen":"The agent must work alongside people as itself.","avoidWhen":"You are GA only, or no Microsoft 365 presence is needed."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-l5-mode","title":"Pick the identity mode","prompt":"Change who is present, what bounds access and what the audience is.","dials":[{"id":"presence","label":"Who is present","values":[{"id":"user","label":"A user is signed in"},{"id":"none","label":"Scheduled or event driven"}]},{"id":"bound","label":"What bounds access","values":[{"id":"user","label":"The user's permissions"},{"id":"agent","label":"The agent's own scope"}]},{"id":"audience","label":"Audience","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One user at a time"},{"id":"team","label":"Team or group context"}]},{"id":"preview","label":"Preview tolerance","values":[{"id":"ok","label":"Preview is acceptable"},{"id":"ga","label":"GA only"}]}],"options":[{"id":"s2s","name":"S2S","summary":"Agent identity, app permissions."},{"id":"obo","name":"OBO","summary":"User subject, agent actor."},{"id":"au","name":"Agentic-User","summary":"Own user account."},{"id":"shared","name":"Shared app registration","summary":"One long-lived secret for many agents."}],"scores":{"s2s":{"presence":{"user":{"s":0,"why":"Ignores the user even when one is present."},"none":{"s":2,"why":"Built for scheduled and background work."}},"bound":{"user":{"s":-2,"why":"The user's permissions do not limit what it reads."},"agent":{"s":2,"why":"Application permissions fit an own scope."}},"audience":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"team":{"s":-1,"why":"No mailbox or Teams presence."}},"preview":{"ok":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"ga":{"s":1,"why":"GA at the 2026-05-01 announcement."}}},"obo":{"presence":{"user":{"s":2,"why":"Needs a signed-in user, who is there."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"No user to act for."}},"bound":{"user":{"s":2,"why":"Delegated permissions bound access to the user."},"agent":{"s":-1,"why":"Over-constrains work with its own scope."}},"audience":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"One accountable user."},"team":{"s":-2,"why":"No single right user in a group chat."}},"preview":{"ok":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"ga":{"s":1,"why":"GA at the 2026-05-01 announcement."}}},"au":{"presence":{"user":{"s":0,"why":"Acts as itself, not as the user."},"none":{"s":1,"why":"Works without a user present."}},"bound":{"user":{"s":-1,"why":"Not bounded by a user."},"agent":{"s":1,"why":"Own account and own scope."}},"audience":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"Heavy for one user."},"team":{"s":2,"why":"Acts as a team member with mailbox and Teams presence."}},"preview":{"ok":{"s":1,"why":"Frontier program access is assumed."},"ga":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs the Frontier preview program."}}},"shared":{"presence":{"user":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but loses who acted."},"none":{"s":1,"why":"Easy for scheduled jobs."}},"bound":{"user":{"s":-2,"why":"Broad shared access ignores the user."},"agent":{"s":-1,"why":"Shared scope grows over time."}},"audience":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"No per-agent accountability."},"team":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"preview":{"ok":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"ga":{"s":1,"why":"Not preview, but outside agent governance."}}}},"takeaway":"OBO when access must follow the user, S2S for scheduled work, Agentic-User when the agent is a teammate. The shared registration never leads on accountability."},{"t":"table","caption":"Sponsors, owners and managers","head":["Role","Required","Who can hold it","Powers"],"rows":[["Sponsor","Yes, at least one on every blueprint and agent identity","Users and some groups. Not role-assignable groups or assigned security groups","Business accountability. Decides lifecycle, can disable or soft-delete, cannot edit auth settings or re-enable"],["Owner","Optional","Users or service principals, never groups","Technical control. Manages credentials, can re-enable or hard-delete"],["Manager","Optional","Not detailed here","Can request access packages for agents"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"A 403 from storage even though RBAC is correct","cause":"The token audience is the MCP server URL, not the downstream resource id such as `https://storage.azure.com`","fix":"Request the token for the resource id. Decode the token and compare its `aud` claim [[r4]]."},{"symptom":"A secret is found in an agent's configuration","cause":"A team expected the agent identity to hold credentials. They live on the blueprint and every identity from it shares them","fix":"Federate the blueprint to an Azure managed identity so no secret is stored, and group only identities that can share credentials."},{"symptom":"A group-chat agent reads one participant's mailbox","cause":"OBO had to choose one user for a shared context","fix":"Give the agent its own account (Agentic-User) or a scoped S2S identity."},{"symptom":"A sponsor disabled an agent and cannot bring it back","cause":"Sponsors can disable or soft-delete but cannot re-enable","fix":"Keep an owner on every agent, and write the recovery path into the runbook."},{"symptom":"A policy plan assumes an MFA prompt for the agent","cause":"Agents do not use passwords or authenticator apps, so MFA does not apply","fix":"Govern with Conditional Access policies at token issue or refresh."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: Conditional Access and ID Governance","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Conditional Access for agents is evaluated when a token is issued or refreshed, and Continuous Access Evaluation covers some resources [[r15]]. It needs Microsoft 365 E7 or an Agent 365 license with at least Entra P1 or Microsoft 365 E3. Entra ID Governance uses access packages for agent identities (security groups, OAuth permissions, Entra roles) under the same license rule [[r27]]. Entra Agent ID itself is available to all Entra customers."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: how each platform gets an identity","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry provisions a blueprint and identity automatically [[r27]]. Hosted agents get a dedicated agent identity at deploy [[r2]]. It can call model inference and session storage by default, and other resources need manual RBAC. A separate project managed identity pulls from Azure Container Registry. User-invoked calls support OAuth On-Behalf-Of, and autonomous calls use the agent identity.\n\nIn the older agent application model, unpublished agents share a project identity and publishing creates a distinct one, so RBAC must be reassigned. The Foundry docs say the publishing experience is changing, so confirm which model your project uses. Copilot Studio can auto-assign agent identities (preview). App Service and Azure Functions can use an agent identity. Third-party agents on AWS Bedrock or n8n use the Entra ID Auth SDK sidecar or workload identity federation."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: autopilot agents","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"An autopilot is a Foundry agent with an Entra agent user in addition to its agent identity, so it acts as itself in Microsoft 365 [[r19]]. You publish a blueprint and teams create instances, each with its own identity and access. Only hosted agents can be published as autopilot blueprints. The identity types are assistive (user context), background service (app-only, no Microsoft 365 actions) and autopilot. The page stated no preview label on 2026-10-13, but agent users need the Frontier program, so treat it as preview."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A hosted agent gets 403 from a storage account although its agent identity holds the right role. What is the likely cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"token aud = https://mcp-tools.example\nRBAC: agent identity has Storage Blob Data Reader\nresult: 403"},"options":["The agent identity needs a client secret stored on its own object","The token audience is the MCP server URL, not the downstream resource id","No sponsor has approved the agent yet","Conditional Access is forcing an MFA prompt the agent cannot answer"],"answer":1,"explain":"A token whose audience is the MCP server URL is not valid for storage, even with correct RBAC. The docs say to use the downstream resource id such as `https://storage.azure.com`. Agent identities hold no credentials, sponsors do not gate tokens, and MFA does not apply to agents.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r4"},{"id":"w12-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A nightly job summarizes tickets and posts to a channel. No user is signed in, the agent has its own scope and the team wants GA features only. Which mode fits?","options":["OBO, because every call needs a user as the token subject","Agentic-User, because every agent needs its own mailbox","S2S, where the agent acts as its own identity with application permissions","A shared app registration with one long-lived secret that every scheduled agent in the tenant reuses"],"answer":2,"explain":"S2S is built for scheduled and background work, with the agent identity as token subject. OBO needs a signed-in user. Agentic-User needs the Frontier preview program and is for user-like presence. A shared secret removes per-agent accountability.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w12-l5-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every true statement about agent identity and accountability.","options":["Every blueprint and agent identity needs at least one sponsor.","A sponsor can re-enable a disabled agent.","Credentials sit on the blueprint, not on the agent identity.","Agent identities can be multitenant, while blueprints are single tenant."],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Sponsors are required, and credentials live on the blueprint, which makes it the credential boundary. Sponsors can disable or soft-delete but cannot re-enable, which is an owner power. The tenancy statement is reversed: identities are single tenant and blueprints can be multitenant.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r18"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r4","r14","r15","r17","r18","r19","r27"],"takeaways":["Pick the mode first: S2S, OBO or Agentic-User, then confirm permissions and consent","The blueprint holds credentials and is the credential boundary","Sponsors carry business accountability, owners carry technical control","A token audience mismatch fails even with correct RBAC"]},{"id":"w12-l6","order":6,"title":"Observe, govern, secure","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Agents run on many platforms, yet an administrator must still see, govern and secure them. Decide how each agent joins the control plane and which loop keeps it healthy after launch.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Agent 365 is a control plane, not a runtime","level":2,"id":"agent-365-is-a-control-plane-not-a-runtime"},{"t":"p","md":"Agent 365 is Microsoft's control plane for agents with three pillars [[r12]]. **Observe** covers the agent registry, the agent map, adoption and health. **Govern** covers lifecycle, access and compliance through the registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center, Entra and Purview. **Secure** covers Entra risk-based access, Purview DLP and information protection, and Defender threat detection.\n\nIt reached general availability on 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment and is licensed per user. At least one user needs a qualifying Agent 365 license. It is included in Microsoft 365 E7 and is an add-on to E5, A5 or Business Premium. The overview page carried no price on 2026-10-13, so quote none. Agent 365 is also the unified registry, while Entra stays the identity foundation [[r29]]. The capabilities announced as the Microsoft Entra Agent Registry continue: agent identity capabilities stay in Entra Agent ID and the registration APIs remain supported, but the full inventory, including agents without an Entra identity, is in Agent 365."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-l6-d1","title":"Many platforms, one registry","alt":"Foundry, Copilot Studio, framework-built and cross-cloud agents all feed the agent registry, which supports observe, govern and secure.","src":"flowchart TB\n  F[Foundry agents] --> R[Agent registry]\n  C[Copilot Studio agents] --> R\n  X[Framework agents with SDK] --> R\n  V[Vertex AI and Bedrock agents] --> R\n  R --> O[Observe: map, health]\n  R --> G[Govern: lifecycle, access]\n  R --> S[Secure: Entra, Purview, Defender]","caption":"Native integration and registry sync add inventory with no code change. The SDK adds code-level capabilities."},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-l6-join","title":"How an agent joins the control plane","prompt":"Change the platform, what you need and who owns the code.","dials":[{"id":"platform","label":"Where it runs","values":[{"id":"ms","label":"Microsoft platform or supported partner"},{"id":"fw","label":"A framework you host"},{"id":"cross","label":"Another cloud platform"}]},{"id":"need","label":"What you need","values":[{"id":"inv","label":"Inventory and governance only"},{"id":"code","label":"Governed tools and notifications in code"}]},{"id":"owner","label":"Code ownership","values":[{"id":"own","label":"You own the code"},{"id":"vendor","label":"A vendor owns it"}]}],"options":[{"id":"native","name":"Native integration","summary":"Appears in the inventory without the SDK."},{"id":"sdk","name":"Agent 365 SDK","summary":"Identity, tooling and notifications in code."},{"id":"sync","name":"Registry synchronization","summary":"For Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock."},{"id":"proxy","name":"Foundry Control Plane registration","summary":"API Management proxy for a custom agent."}],"scores":{"native":{"platform":{"ms":{"s":2,"why":"Microsoft platforms and supported partners appear without the SDK."},"fw":{"s":-1,"why":"Framework agents add the SDK instead."},"cross":{"s":-2,"why":"Connected platforms use registry sync."}},"need":{"inv":{"s":1,"why":"Inventory arrives with no code change."},"code":{"s":-1,"why":"No code-level tools or notifications."}},"owner":{"own":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"vendor":{"s":1,"why":"No change to code you do not own."}}},"sdk":{"platform":{"ms":{"s":0,"why":"Not needed for inventory."},"fw":{"s":2,"why":"The documented path for framework-built agents."},"cross":{"s":-1,"why":"Connected platforms use sync instead."}},"need":{"inv":{"s":0,"why":"More than inventory needs."},"code":{"s":2,"why":"The route to governed Work IQ MCP servers and notifications."}},"owner":{"own":{"s":1,"why":"You can change the code."},"vendor":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot add an SDK to code you do not own."}}},"sync":{"platform":{"ms":{"s":-1,"why":"Native is simpler."},"fw":{"s":-1,"why":"Misses code-level capabilities."},"cross":{"s":2,"why":"The documented path for Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock."}},"need":{"inv":{"s":1,"why":"Inventory with no code change."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"No code-level capabilities."}},"owner":{"own":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"vendor":{"s":1,"why":"Works without touching vendor code."}}},"proxy":{"platform":{"ms":{"s":-1,"why":"A Microsoft platform agent needs no proxy."},"fw":{"s":0,"why":"Possible for a custom agent."},"cross":{"s":1,"why":"Registers an agent that runs elsewhere behind a proxy."}},"need":{"inv":{"s":1,"why":"Access control and monitoring without code change."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"No code-level tools."}},"owner":{"own":{"s":1,"why":"You can register your own agent."},"vendor":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}}},"takeaway":"Native and sync buy inventory with no code change. The SDK buys code-level capabilities but needs code you own. Name which of the two you actually need."},{"t":"table","caption":"The production loop around a Foundry agent, checked 2026-10-13","head":["Control","What it does","Status"],"rows":[["Guardrails [[r8]]","A named set of controls with four intervention points: user input, tool call, tool response and output. Spotlighting and groundedness do not apply to agents. Hosted agents also get network egress controls. They apply only to Foundry Agent Service agents","Model guardrails GA. Agent guardrails preview, including tool call, tool response and egress"],["Tracing [[r9]]","Traces for prompt and hosted agents","GA for prompt and hosted agents. Monitoring is preview"],["Evaluation","Quality, safety and agent evaluators (see world w14). Voice agents support full conversations only, not single-turn or simulated runs","Voice-agent limits are preview limits"],["Agent optimizer [[r25]]","Evaluates an agent and proposes better instructions, tool descriptions, model selection and, for hosted agents, skills. Prompt agent candidates promote to a new version. Hosted candidates are applied to local config and redeployed","Limited preview"],["Telemetry [[r26]]","The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for .NET, Node.js and Python also targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend","Use it. The Agent 365 Observability SDK is deprecated for new work"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"An agent is missing from the inventory","cause":"Native integration covers Microsoft platforms and supported partners. Framework agents need the SDK and other clouds need registry sync","fix":"Pick the join path from the dial board and check the registry after a test run."},{"symptom":"One Copilot Studio agent shows up twice in the registry","cause":"A Copilot Studio agent can appear under more than one registry type by design","fix":"Deduplicate by agent id, not by row, before you report counts."},{"symptom":"New code instruments with the Agent 365 Observability SDK","cause":"That SDK and its packages are deprecated for new work","fix":"Use the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro."},{"symptom":"A team-workflow agent is promised as GA","cause":"Agents in team workflows with their own access were public preview at the 2026-05-01 announcement","fix":"Confirm the current status and accept preview terms before production [[r20]]."},{"symptom":"A team expects the SDK to host the agent","cause":"The Agent 365 SDK does not build, host, orchestrate or run tools","fix":"Host the agent on a runtime from level 1 and add the SDK for identity, tooling and notifications [[r13]]."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"GA and preview inside one announcement","md":"At the 2026-05-01 announcement, agents working on behalf of users and agents behind the scenes with their own access were GA. Agents in team workflows with their own access were public preview. Discovery of local and shadow agents through Defender and Intune was announced as preview. Agent user accounts and notifications need the Frontier preview program. Checked 2026-10-13."},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: the registry","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The registry lives in the Microsoft 365 admin center under Agents, All Agents, Registry [[r16]]. It has four types: Microsoft agents, external partner-built agents, published by your org and shared by creator."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: what the SDK adds","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The Agent 365 SDK ships Python, JavaScript and .NET packages for identity, tooling and notifications [[r13]]. Tooling means governed Work IQ MCP servers for Mail, Calendar, Word, SharePoint and Teams. It is not the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK. Notifications need an agent user account and the Frontier preview program. Three paths exist: native integration, the SDK for framework-built agents (Microsoft 365 Agents SDK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Microsoft Agent Framework, LangChain, CrewAI, LlamaIndex) and registry synchronization for platforms such as Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the Foundry Control Plane proxy","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"You can register a custom agent that runs elsewhere in the Foundry Control Plane, and Foundry puts API Management in front as a proxy for access control and monitoring [[r23]]. For A2A agents the default agent card path is `/.well-known/agent-card.json`. An AI gateway is free to set up. This is a Foundry layer. Agent 365 is the Microsoft 365 layer that ingests agents from Foundry and elsewhere."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"A team assumes adopting the Agent 365 SDK will host their LangChain agent. What is true?","options":["It adds identity, governed tooling and notifications, but you still host and run the agent","It hosts and scales the agent inside the Microsoft 365 tenant","It replaces the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK for building agents","It runs the agent's tools in a sandbox that Agent 365 manages"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Agent 365 SDK provides identity, governed Work IQ tooling and notifications. It does not build, host, orchestrate or run tools, and it is not the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w12-l6-q2","type":"match","tag":"flip","q":"Match each situation to the way the agent joins the control plane.","pairs":[{"l":"A Microsoft platform agent or a supported partner agent","r":"Native integration, no SDK"},{"l":"A LangChain agent you host and can change","r":"Add the Agent 365 SDK"},{"l":"An agent on Vertex AI or AWS Bedrock","r":"Registry synchronization"},{"l":"A custom agent elsewhere that needs gateway access control","r":"Foundry Control Plane registration"}],"explain":"Microsoft platforms and supported partners integrate natively. Framework-built agents add the SDK. Connected platforms such as Vertex AI and Bedrock use registry sync. A custom agent elsewhere can be registered in the Foundry Control Plane, which proxies it through API Management.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w12-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A platform team wants agents that join team workflows with their own access in production, because Agent 365 is GA. Which risk statement is right?","options":["They are GA with the rest of the product, so nothing more is needed before production use","They need Entra P2 before they can run at all","They only run as Foundry hosted agents","They were public preview on 2026-05-01, so confirm status and accept preview terms"],"answer":3,"explain":"The announcement split modes: delegated and behind-the-scenes agents were GA and team-workflow agents with their own access were public preview. A GA product label does not make every mode GA. The Entra P2 and hosted-only claims have no support in the sources.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r20"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r8","r9","r12","r13","r16","r20","r23","r25","r26","r29"],"takeaways":["Agent 365 observes, governs and secures agents. It does not build or host them","Native and sync add inventory, the SDK adds code-level capabilities, and each needs the right code ownership","A GA product can hold preview modes, so check each mode and date","Use the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro, not the deprecated Observability SDK"]},{"id":"w12-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: ship it with a name, a leash and a log","kind":"boss","minutes":22,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to take an agent to production on Microsoft's stack and leaves every dial open. Clarify, compare, pick, name the failures and say how you will measure and roll out.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The five-step defend for a production agent","level":2,"id":"the-five-step-defend-for-a-production-agent"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's setup","known":["An employee-facing agent answers questions and acts in internal systems","It must run on Microsoft's stack","Leadership wants it in production this quarter"],"ask":["Is configuration enough, or does the agent need code, custom protocols or per-session files?","Is a user present for each action, or does it run on a schedule or in group chats?","How are document permissions modelled and how fast must a revocation take effect?","Which preview features does the compliance bar allow?"],"tension":"Speed to launch pulls toward hosted code and broad access. Accountability pulls toward narrow identities and slower approval."},{"t":"flow","title":"Launch gates","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Runtime and protocol","md":"Prompt, hosted, self-hosted. Responses by default.","tag":"level 1 to 3"},{"title":"Identity and sponsor","md":"Mode, blueprint boundary, sponsor and owner.","tag":"level 5"},{"title":"Tools and knowledge","md":"Toolbox, Foundry IQ, ACL sync.","tag":"level 4"},{"title":"Guardrails, traces, evals","md":"Gate on a golden set before widening.","tag":"level 6"},{"title":"Publish and register","md":"Teams scope, Agent 365 registry.","tag":"level 3 and 6"},{"title":"Observe and retire","md":"Dashboards, review, retirement dates.","tag":"level 6"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Defaults to propose and the dial that flips each","head":["Dial","Default","Flips to","When"],"rows":[["Runtime","Prompt agent","Hosted agent, or self-hosted","Code or per-session files are needed, or code must stay elsewhere"],["Protocol","Responses","Invocations, Invocations WebSocket","A webhook caller, or real-time voice"],["Identity mode","OBO","S2S, or Agentic-User (Frontier preview)","A scheduled job, or a team coworker in group chats"],["Knowledge","Foundry IQ","Custom retrieval, file search, memory","Own ranking and eval, user uploads, or per-user facts"],["Governance join","Native integration","Agent 365 SDK, registry sync, Control Plane registration","Framework code you own, another cloud, or a proxy at a gateway"],["Preview use","GA features only","Named preview features with a waiver","A dated, signed-off exception for memory, agent guardrails or tool search"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Date-stamped status. Say 'tool search is preview as of 2026-10-13' and name the retirement dates (Workflows 2026-12-01, classic agents 2027-03-31). Then name who is accountable: the sponsor."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-l7-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Which launch decision best limits the blast radius if one agent's credentials leak?","options":["One blueprint for every agent, so there is a single place to rotate secrets and review permissions for the whole fleet","A client secret in Key Vault shared by all agents for simple rotation","Separate blueprints for agents that cannot share credentials and permissions, federated to a managed identity","Make every sponsor an owner so any of them can recover the agent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Blueprint credentials are shared by every identity created from the blueprint, so the blueprint is the credential boundary. Separating blueprints and federating to a managed identity keeps a leak local and stores no secret. One blueprint or one shared secret widens the leak. Sponsors and owners hold different powers on purpose.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w12-l7-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the launch gates in order.","items":["Choose the runtime and protocol","Choose the identity mode, blueprint and sponsor","Attach tools through a toolbox and knowledge with ACL-aware retrieval","Turn on guardrails, tracing and evaluation","Publish, then confirm the agent appears in the Agent 365 registry"],"explain":"Runtime and protocol decide where identity attaches. Identity must exist before tools and knowledge can run as the right caller. Guardrails and evaluation need tools in place to test. Publishing and registry checks come last, before the rollout widens.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-l7-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A hosted agent's sustained peak CPU is 0.6 vCPU. Docs advise a larger size when peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation. What is the smallest sandbox, in vCPU, that stays at or under 70 percent?","answer":1,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"vCPU","hint":"Sizes are 0.5, 1 and 2 vCPU.","explain":"At 0.5 vCPU the peak is 120 percent of allocation, at 1 vCPU it is 60 percent and at 2 vCPU it is 30 percent. The 1 vCPU size is the smallest that stays under 70 percent, and it costs less per active session than 2 vCPU.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"}],"defend":{"prompt":"We built an agent that answers employee questions and takes actions in internal systems. Take it to production on Microsoft's stack. Walk me through runtime, identity, governance and observability, and tell me how you would know it is safe to widen the rollout.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Is configuration enough, or does it need custom code, protocols or per-session files?","Is a user present for each action, or does it run on a schedule or in group chats?","How are document permissions modelled, and how fast must a revoked permission take effect?","Which channels do people use: Teams, Microsoft 365 Copilot, an API, voice?","Which preview features does the compliance and support bar allow?","Where do other agents already run, and who owns their code?"],"options":[{"name":"Prompt agent, toolbox and Foundry IQ","when":"Configuration is enough and the GA bar is high.","tradeoff":"No custom protocols or per-session files. Least to manage."},{"name":"Hosted agent on Responses","when":"You need custom orchestration, `$HOME` state or per-session isolation.","tradeoff":"Cost follows active sessions, long-running resilience is still preview on 2026-10-13, and the older preview backend retired on 2026-08-20."},{"name":"Self-hosted agent registered for governance","when":"Code must stay on another cloud or on premises.","tradeoff":"You run isolation, scaling and identity wiring. Governance gives inventory, with the SDK adding code-level tools."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would ask whether configuration is enough or code is needed, whether a user is present for each action, how document permissions work and how fast revocation must be, which channels are in play and how much preview the compliance bar allows. Those answers set runtime, identity mode and knowledge path."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** A prompt agent with a toolbox and Foundry IQ is the boring option and has the least to manage. A hosted agent buys code, protocols and per-session state at the price of active-session compute. A self-hosted agent registered with Agent 365 or the Control Plane is for code that must stay elsewhere."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick and flip.** For an employee assistant that acts for the person I would start with a prompt agent. If code or files are needed I move to a hosted agent on Responses, so Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot publishing works. Identity is OBO so access follows the user. Tools sit behind a toolbox with central auth and versions. Curated content goes through Foundry IQ under the caller's identity with ACL sync. Scheduled jobs get S2S under a separate blueprint. A group-chat coworker would need Agentic-User, which is Frontier preview, so I would flag it with the date."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Flip to Invocations when the caller is a webhook or batch system, and to WebSocket for real-time voice.","Flip to custom retrieval when we need our own ranking and evals, and to file search for user uploads.","Flip to S2S when no user is present, and to the SDK when we own framework code and need governed tools."]},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["403 despite correct RBAC from a token audience mismatch","Decode the token and compare `aud` with the downstream resource id"],["One leaked credential exposes many agents","Blueprint inventory: which identities share each blueprint, and are secrets stored at all"],["Hosted bill grows with concurrency","Active-session CPU and memory against allocation, with the 70 percent rule"],["A toolbox promotion breaks consumers","Staged test of the new version, tool-call accuracy before and after promotion"],["A preview dependency is retired","A dated register: Workflows 2026-12-01, classic agents 2027-03-31"],["The registry misses agents","Compare registry counts with the deployment inventory each week"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I would track task success and groundedness on a golden set, tool-call accuracy, guardrail trigger and refusal rates, p95 latency, tokens per task, cost per active session, 403 rate and registry coverage. Rollout starts with an Individual-scope pilot, then Organization scope after admin approval. A canary is a second agent, because an endpoint serves one version. I widen only when evaluations and trace review pass, and every agent has a named sponsor and a retirement plan."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about code need, user presence, permission model and preview tolerance before naming a product","w":3},{"pt":"Compares at least two runtimes and names where each loses","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses an identity mode for the stated dials and explains the token subject","w":3},{"pt":"Separates credential boundaries (blueprints) and names sponsor and owner roles","w":2},{"pt":"Labels preview and retiring features with dates","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with a detection signal each","w":2},{"pt":"Gives metrics and a staged rollout with a canary design that respects one version per endpoint","w":2},{"pt":"States what would flip the choice, including the governance join path","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"Security finds the agent reading one person's mail while answering in a group chat. What changes?","a":"OBO had to choose a user for a shared context. Move that workload to its own identity: Agentic-User if Frontier preview is acceptable, or a scoped S2S identity, and remove delegated mail access from the shared flow."},{"q":"The hosted agent bill triples after launch. What do you check first?","a":"Concurrent active sessions times allocation. Compare sustained peaks with the 70 percent guidance, right-size the next version, and ask whether a prompt agent covers the need."},{"q":"How do you canary a new agent version when the endpoint serves 100 percent to one version?","a":"Deploy it as a second agent, route a small share from the client or gateway, compare evaluation and trace metrics, then promote. Traffic splitting is not supported on the endpoint."}],"redFlags":["Names hosted agents for everything with no cost or preview caveat","Gives every agent one shared secret or one blueprint","Treats a GA product announcement as proof that every mode is GA","Skips sponsors, so nobody is accountable for the agent","Has no detection signal for any failure mode"]},"refs":["r1","r2","r12","r17","r28"],"takeaways":["Ask for the dials first, then pick the boring option and name what flips it","Credential boundary, token audience and sponsor are the identity details that sink answers","Date-stamp every preview and retirement claim","Measure before you widen, and roll out through pilot, organization scope and a second-agent canary"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w12-t01","order":1,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Foundry Agent Service map and agent types","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Foundry Agent Service is a managed platform with a runtime, toolboxes, models, tracing, optimization, identity and publishing. You choose how much you build: a prompt agent, a voice prompt agent, a hosted agent, or a direct Responses API call.","useIf":"You are deciding where an agent should live in Foundry and want the map before the details.","avoidIf":"You already know the agent type and need protocol, session or identity details: jump to the specific topic.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry Agent Service is a managed platform for building, deploying and scaling agents [[r1]]. Its parts are the **Agent Runtime** (hosts prompt and hosted agents, manages conversations and tool calls), **Toolboxes**, **Models** from the Foundry catalog, **Observability**, **Optimization**, **Identity and Security**, and **Publishing** [[r1]]. The runtime itself rests on three objects: an **agent** (model, instructions, tools), a **conversation** (persisted history) and a **response** (one unit of execution) [[tr1]]. A response can run with or without an agent or a conversation, so start with a response and add the others when you need them [[tr1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"You then pick how much to build. A **prompt agent** is configuration only. A **voice-based prompt agent** adds managed real-time voice through Voice Live. A **hosted agent** is your own code, shipped as a container image or a .zip that Foundry builds into an image. Calling the **Responses API directly** from code you already run gives an *ephemeral agent*: the definition lives in your app and no Foundry agent resource exists [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t01-d1","title":"Four ways in, one project endpoint","alt":"Clients reach prompt, voice and hosted agents through the Agent Runtime. Your own app can call the Responses API directly. All of them use the models and tools behind the project endpoint.","src":"flowchart TB\n  C[Client or channel] --> RT[Agent Runtime]\n  RT --> PA[Prompt agent]\n  RT --> VA[Voice prompt agent]\n  RT --> HA[Hosted agent]\n  APP[Your app, Responses API direct] --> M\n  PA --> M[Models and toolbox]\n  VA --> M\n  HA --> M"},{"t":"table","caption":"Status of each part on 2026-10-13 (checked against the live pages)","head":["Part","What it gives you","Status"],"rows":[["Agents (core), prompt agents","Config-only agents run by Foundry","GA [[r9]]"],["Voice-based prompt agents","Managed real-time voice via Voice Live","Preview [[r1]]"],["Hosted agents","Your code, per-session sandbox, dedicated identity","No separate GA row. Explicit GA statement: UNVERIFIED [[r9]]"],["Tracing","End-to-end traces in Application Insights","GA for prompt and hosted agents [[tr3]]"],["Agent optimizer","Proposes better instructions and tool descriptions","Limited preview [[r25]]"],["Agent guardrails","Controls at input, tool call, tool response and output","Preview for agents, GA for models [[r8]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Resource model in one line","md":"The Foundry resource is `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` with kind `AIServices`, and a project is its subresource and the development boundary. Roles were renamed (Foundry User, Foundry Owner, Foundry Account Owner, Foundry Project Manager were Azure AI User, Azure AI Owner, Azure AI Account Owner, Azure AI Project Manager). Role IDs did not change [[r24]]."}],"whereToUse":["Choosing a starting point for a new agent project before any code exists","Explaining to a platform team which parts Foundry runs and which parts you own","Planning a migration off classic agents, which retire on 2027-03-31 [[r11]]","Mapping features to preview risk before a production go-live review"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You want one managed place for runtime, tools, identity, tracing and publishing","You can accept Azure-hosted compute and the Foundry model catalog","You want to start light (prompt agent) and move to code only when a requirement forces it"],"avoid":["Code must stay on another cloud or on premises and cannot move: self-host and only register it","You need a feature that is still preview and your policy forbids preview in production","A single prompt-only chatbot with no tools, identity or publishing needs: a direct model call is simpler"],"flips":["A requirement for webhooks, custom protocols or persistent files flips a prompt agent to a hosted agent","An existing agent that already runs elsewhere flips you to the direct Responses API call or registration","A production ban on preview features removes voice-based agents and memory from the shortlist"]},"alternatives":{"title":"The four entry points compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt agent","No custom orchestration, fastest path, nothing to operate","You need your own code, protocols or files","Per-call inference and tool usage only [[r1]]"],["Voice-based prompt agent","Managed spoken conversation without hosting orchestration","You need direct SIP or full evaluation modes","Preview: no SLA, several limits [[r1]]"],["Hosted agent","Custom code, custom protocols, `$HOME` state, compute control","The agent is light and config would do","Adds container compute per active session [[r1]]"],["Responses API direct (ephemeral)","Definition should ship with your app, no Foundry agent resource","You want a managed endpoint, versions and publishing for the agent","You run and secure the host process [[r1]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Team builds a hosted agent for a simple FAQ bot","cause":"Assuming production means containers","fix":"Start with a prompt agent. The docs name it the least to manage and good for production agents without custom orchestration [[r1]]."},{"symptom":"A doc or sample refers to Connected Agents or a shared project endpoint with `agent_reference`","cause":"Classic or initial-preview material that does not match the new service","fix":"Check the date and the migration page. Connected Agents does not exist in the new service, and the initial-preview hosted backend ended on 2026-08-20 [[r11]]."},{"symptom":"Role assignment fails because the role name is not found","cause":"Docs and portals mix old Azure AI names and new Foundry names","fix":"Search for both names. The role IDs are unchanged [[r24]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t01-s1","title":"Internal helper, small team","situation":"A team wants an internal assistant that answers from a few tools and a knowledge source. Nobody wants to own containers.","constraints":["No custom orchestration logic","Small team, low ops appetite","Text only"],"unknowns":["Does any tool need to run inside our process?","Do we need Teams publishing later?"],"question":"Which entry point do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Prompt agent","verdict":"best","why":"Instructions, model and tools as configuration. Foundry runs it, with an Entra identity and a managed endpoint.","tradeoff":"No custom code path, so a later need for webhooks or files means moving to a hosted agent."},{"name":"Direct Responses API call","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the definition in your repo and still uses Foundry models and tools.","tradeoff":"You host and secure the calling process and get no Foundry agent resource to publish."},{"name":"Hosted agent","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds container build, compute billed per active session and code to maintain for a need that config covers.","tradeoff":"Right when you need your own code, custom protocols or persistent files."}],"flips":[{"if":"The helper must receive webhooks from another system","then":"Hosted agent with the Invocations protocol."},{"if":"The definition must be reviewed and deployed with the app code","then":"Direct Responses API call (ephemeral agent)."}],"takeaway":"Default to the least to manage, and move up only when a named requirement forces it."},{"id":"w12-t01-s2","title":"Phone line, preview allowed","situation":"A business wants a spoken assistant for inbound calls. The team has no speech orchestration code and accepts preview features with a waiver.","constraints":["Real-time voice","Preview allowed with sign-off","Phone channel needed"],"unknowns":["Is direct SIP required?","Do we need to inspect and redact the transcript between stages?"],"question":"How do you build it?","options":[{"name":"Voice-based prompt agent with Teams Phone extensibility or Twilio","verdict":"best","why":"Foundry manages the voice lifecycle through Voice Live, and Teams Phone extensibility and Twilio are the supported phone paths [[r1]].","tradeoff":"Preview: no SLA. Direct SIP is not offered and evaluation covers full conversations only."},{"name":"Hosted agent with Invocations WebSocket and your own voice stack","verdict":"good","why":"Full control over turn handling and tools, paired with Pipecat, LiveKit or Voice Live in the container [[r2]].","tradeoff":"You write and operate the orchestration and pay for compute."},{"name":"Prompt text agent behind a standard Teams card","verdict":"poor","why":"The standard text-agent card is not shown for voice agents, and this does not give spoken turns [[r1]].","tradeoff":"Fine for text chat, not for phone calls."}],"flips":[{"if":"Policy forbids preview features in production","then":"Hosted agent with Invocations WebSocket, after you confirm its status for your policy."},{"if":"Transcripts must be filtered between stages","then":"Use the cascaded architecture so text is inspectable."}],"takeaway":"Voice-based agents buy managed orchestration at the price of preview limits."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which entry point leaves no Foundry agent resource and keeps the agent definition in your application code?","options":["Prompt agent","Direct Responses API call (ephemeral agent)","Hosted agent","Voice-based prompt agent"],"answer":1,"explain":"An ephemeral agent is assembled in your process on each call. Prompt, hosted and voice agents are all persisted Foundry resources.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t01-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each entry point to the cost model the docs give for it.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt agent","r":"Per-call inference plus tool usage"},{"l":"Hosted agent","r":"Inference plus tools plus container compute"},{"l":"Voice-based prompt agent","r":"Voice Live and model usage"}],"explain":"The agent-type comparison table in the overview lists these cost models. Only the hosted agent adds container compute.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t01-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about status on 2026-10-13 are supported by the docs?","options":["Voice-based prompt agents are preview","The agent optimizer is limited preview","Hosted agents have an explicit GA row in the Foundry GA table","Tracing is GA for prompt and hosted agents"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Voice agents and the optimizer carry preview labels and tracing is GA for prompt and hosted agents. The GA table has no separate hosted agent row, so an explicit hosted GA statement is unverified.","ref":"r9"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the three Foundry agent types and the fourth entry point.","back":"Prompt agents, voice-based prompt agents and hosted agents. The fourth is calling the Responses API directly, which gives an ephemeral agent with no Foundry resource."},{"front":"What are the three runtime objects in Agent Service?","back":"Agent (model, instructions, tools), conversation (persisted history) and response (one execution). Start with a response and add the others when needed."}],"related":["w12-t02","w12-t03","w12-t07","w12-t19","w12-l1"],"refs":["r1","r2","r9","r11","r24","r25","r8","tr1","tr3"]},{"id":"w12-t02","order":2,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Prompt and voice agents","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A prompt agent is instructions, a model and tools stored as configuration that Foundry runs. A voice-based prompt agent adds a managed spoken conversation through Voice Live. Voice-based agents are preview.","useIf":"No custom orchestration is needed and you want nothing to build, host or scale, in text or in managed voice.","avoidIf":"You need your own code path, webhooks, custom streaming or per-session files, or you cannot accept preview limits for voice.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **prompt agent** is defined entirely through configuration: instructions, model selection and tools. You can author it in the portal, or define it with the SDK or REST API inside a CI/CD pipeline so it gets version control and review [[r1]]. There is no application code, container or compute to manage, and it gets an Entra agent identity and automatic scaling [[r1]]. The cost model is per-call inference plus tool usage [[r1]]. Function-calling tools run on the client side, so your app executes them [[r3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"A **voice-based prompt agent** adds spoken conversation. You connect over a real-time WebSocket and Foundry uses Voice Live for recognition, turn taking, model interaction and synthesis [[r1]]. Voice Live is a managed speech-to-speech service with a WebSocket interface close to the Azure OpenAI Realtime events, over 140 speech-to-text locales and noise suppression [[tr6]]. There are two architectures, and the choice is a latency versus control trade-off [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t02-d1","title":"Native versus cascaded voice paths","alt":"Native path sends caller audio to a realtime speech model and back as speech. Cascaded path goes through speech recognition, a text model and speech synthesis.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Caller audio] --> N[Realtime speech model]\n  N --> O1[Spoken reply]\n  A --> S[Speech recognition]\n  S --> T[Text model]\n  T --> Y[Speech synthesis]\n  Y --> O2[Spoken reply]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Voice architecture trade-offs per the Foundry docs","head":["Dial","Native speech-to-speech","Cascaded text model"],"rows":[["Latency","Optimized for low latency","Each stage adds latency"],["Control","Settings depend on the model","Explicit control of transcription, phrase lists, turn detection and interim responses"],["Choice","Realtime model and its voices","Broader text model and Azure voice choice"],["Text between stages","None to inspect","Text exists, so you can log or filter it (inference from the stage layout)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview limits, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Voice-based agents are preview with no SLA. Direct SIP is not offered. Phone numbers come from Teams Phone extensibility or Twilio. The standard Teams card is not shown for voice agents. Evaluation supports full conversations only, not single-turn or simulated runs, and the dataset-from-traces action is unavailable. Monitoring cards need Application Insights. `latency_threshold_ms` for interim responses defaults to 2000, and noise reduction accepts `near_field`, `far_field` or `azure_deep_noise_suppression` [[r1]] [[tr5]]."}],"whereToUse":["Internal assistants and production agents that need no custom orchestration [[r1]]","Agents defined in code through the SDK or REST so a pipeline can review and roll them out","Customer-facing and call-center voice experiences without a self-built voice service [[r1]]","Quick proofs of concept in the portal playground, then a code-first definition"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The behavior fits instructions, a model and tools","You want no compute line and automatic scaling","For voice: you want managed turn taking and phone integration through Teams Phone extensibility or Twilio"],"avoid":["You need to receive arbitrary webhooks or run code in the agent process","For voice: you need direct SIP or single-turn and simulated evaluation today","For voice: your policy forbids preview features in production"],"flips":["Latency matters most: native speech-to-speech. Transcript control matters most: cascaded","Orchestration logic outgrows prompts: move to a hosted agent","A tool must execute inside your process: keep a prompt agent and use client-side function calling, or move to hosted"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt agent","Config is enough, least to operate","Custom code, files or protocols are required","Few controls beyond config"],["Voice-based prompt agent","Managed voice, phone via Teams Phone or Twilio","Direct SIP, GA-only policy, richer evaluation","Preview, no SLA [[r1]]"],["Hosted agent with Invocations WebSocket","You own turn handling and tools, Pipecat or LiveKit","You want no code to maintain","Container compute and your own voice stack [[r2]]"],["Direct Voice Live integration","You want Azure Speech features without a Foundry agent","You want Foundry versioning and identity for the agent","You build the glue [[tr6]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Voice agent does not appear in the Teams or Microsoft 365 Copilot channel card","cause":"The standard text-agent card is not shown in the voice-based Channels layout","fix":"Use Teams Phone extensibility or Twilio for telephony and do not treat Teams publishing as the phone path [[r1]]."},{"symptom":"Evaluation run cannot be created for a voice agent","cause":"Single-turn and simulated-conversation evaluation are not supported for voice agents","fix":"Evaluate full conversations from existing sessions [[r1]]."},{"symptom":"Callers hear long silence while a tool runs","cause":"The interim response only starts after `latency_threshold_ms`, which defaults to 2000","fix":"Lower the threshold, as the best-practice page advises [[tr5]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t02-s1","title":"Agent definition must go through review","situation":"A platform team wants every agent change reviewed and rolled out by a pipeline. The agent needs only instructions and approved tools.","constraints":["Change control is mandatory","No custom runtime code","Text channel"],"unknowns":["Who is allowed to edit in the portal?","Do we need a rollback path?"],"question":"How do you define and run the agent?","options":[{"name":"Code-first prompt agent defined through the SDK or REST in the pipeline","verdict":"best","why":"Version control and review apply to the definition while Foundry runs it.","tradeoff":"Portal edits can drift unless you restrict them."},{"name":"Portal-built prompt agent edited by hand","verdict":"poor","why":"Fast to start, but changes bypass review.","tradeoff":"Right for a proof of concept or a single owner."},{"name":"Hosted agent with the definition in code","verdict":"good","why":"Everything is in the repo.","tradeoff":"You build, host and pay compute for logic that configuration already covers."}],"flips":[{"if":"The definition must ship inside the application release","then":"Consider the direct Responses API call so the definition lives in the app repo."},{"if":"The agent needs a webhook receiver","then":"Hosted agent with Invocations."}],"takeaway":"Prompt agents can be code-first, so you get review without hosting."},{"id":"w12-t02-s2","title":"Regulated phone line","situation":"A voice assistant must keep a redacted transcript for audit. Preview is allowed with sign-off. Peak call volume is high.","constraints":["Transcripts must be inspectable before the model sees them","Preview allowed","Phone channel"],"unknowns":["How much extra latency is acceptable?","Is the model deployment allowed in the region?"],"question":"Which voice architecture do you select?","options":[{"name":"Cascaded text model architecture","verdict":"best","why":"Recognition produces text you can log and filter, and you control phrase lists and turn detection.","tradeoff":"Every stage adds latency."},{"name":"Native speech-to-speech","verdict":"poor","why":"Lowest latency, but there is no text stage to redact before the model.","tradeoff":"Right when natural, low-latency conversation matters more than text control."},{"name":"Hosted agent with a custom pipeline","verdict":"good","why":"You control every stage, including redaction.","tradeoff":"More code, compute cost and operations."}],"flips":[{"if":"Latency is the top requirement and audit needs only a post-call transcript","then":"Native speech-to-speech."},{"if":"No preview features are allowed","then":"A hosted agent pipeline, once its status meets your policy."}],"takeaway":"Pick cascaded when text control beats the last milliseconds."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which service do voice-based prompt agents use for recognition, turn taking and synthesis?","options":["Azure AI Search","Voice Live","A hosted container you write","Application Insights"],"answer":1,"explain":"Foundry Agent Service uses Voice Live. Search is retrieval, a hosted container is another agent type, and Application Insights only stores telemetry.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t02-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"What is the default `latency_threshold_ms` for interim responses in a Foundry voice agent?","answer":2000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"ms","hint":"The best-practice page advises lowering it.","explain":"The default is 2000 ms, so callers can wait two seconds before an interim response unless you lower it.","ref":"tr5"},{"id":"w12-t02-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A team cannot reach callers through the standard Teams card for their voice agent. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The standard text-agent card is not shown in the voice-based Channels layout","Voice agents need a hosted container first","Preview agents cannot use Entra identities","Voice Live only supports WebRTC"],"answer":0,"explain":"The docs say the standard card is not shown and telephony uses Teams Phone extensibility or Twilio. Voice agents do get identities, and a container is not required.","ref":"r1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Two voice architectures in Foundry voice agents?","back":"Native speech-to-speech (low latency) and cascaded (recognition, text model, synthesis: more choice and control, more latency)."},{"front":"Which phone paths do voice-based prompt agents support?","back":"Teams Phone extensibility and Twilio. Direct SIP is not offered. All of this is preview as of 2026-10-13."}],"related":["w12-t01","w12-t04","w12-t19","w13-t07","w12-l1"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","tr5","tr6"]},{"id":"w12-t03","order":3,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Hosted agents overview","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A hosted agent is your own agent code, packaged as a container image or a .zip, that Foundry runs with a dedicated endpoint, a dedicated Entra identity, scaling and session state.","useIf":"You bring code, a framework, custom protocols, persistent files or need to choose compute size.","avoidIf":"Instructions, a model and tools already cover the behavior, or the code must stay on another cloud.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Hosted agents are containerized agentic apps that run on Agent Service. Your code does the orchestration and calls models from the Foundry catalog for reasoning [[r2]]. Supported languages are Python and C#, and the docs name Agent Framework, LangGraph, the OpenAI Agents SDK, the Anthropic Agent SDK and the GitHub Copilot SDK, or custom code [[r1]]. You ship a container image, or a .zip of source that Foundry builds into an image [[r1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"On deploy the service pulls the image from Azure Container Registry, assigns a **dedicated Entra agent identity** and exposes a **dedicated endpoint**. At runtime it provisions compute **per session** in a VM-isolated sandbox. Your code can call Foundry models, Toolbox tools and downstream Azure services with the agent identity [[r2]]. The split is clear: you own the code inside the sandbox, the platform owns the endpoint, identity, scaling and session state around it [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t03-d1","title":"Who owns what","alt":"A client calls a dedicated endpoint. Agent Service holds versions, identity and conversations and starts a per-session sandbox with your code. The code calls models, a toolbox and Azure services.","src":"flowchart LR\n  C[Client] --> E[Dedicated endpoint]\n  E --> AS[Agent Service: versions, identity, state]\n  AS --> SB[Per-session sandbox: your code]\n  SB --> M[Foundry models]\n  SB --> TB[Toolbox]\n  SB --> AZ[Your Azure services]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Why choose hosted over a prompt agent (from the hosted agents page)","head":["Need","How hosted agents answer it"],"rows":[["Bring your own code","Any framework or custom code instead of prompt-only definitions"],["Custom protocols","Webhooks and non-OpenAI payloads through the Invocations protocol"],["Control compute","Choose CPU and memory for the session sandbox"],["Stateful work","`$HOME` and the `/files` endpoint persist across turns"],["Long-lived work","Preview resilience features keep in-progress work across process interruptions"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Status and migration, checked 2026-10-13","md":"The Foundry GA table has no separate hosted agent row and the concept page has no preview banner, so an explicit \"hosted agents are GA\" statement is **UNVERIFIED** [[r9]]. Long-running resilience articles are preview [[tr2]]. Support for the initial-preview hosted backend ended on 2026-08-20. Redeploy needs `azure-ai-projects` 2.3.0 or later and `azd` 1.23.0 or later with `azd ext install azure.ai.agents`, and the adapter packages were replaced by `azure-ai-agentserver-responses` and `azure-ai-agentserver-invocations` [[r11]]. The docs list 31 regions on this date and say the list grows [[r2]]."}],"whereToUse":["An existing LangGraph, Agent Framework or OpenAI Agents SDK app that needs a managed endpoint, identity and scaling","Agents that receive webhooks, run batch extraction or expose AG-UI streaming","Agents that need `$HOME` files across turns, such as a working directory or cached data","Agents that call your own code or private services using the agent identity with explicit RBAC"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You need code, a framework or a custom protocol","You need to choose CPU and memory per session","You want Foundry to own identity, scaling and observability around your code"],"avoid":["The agent is light and a prompt agent would do, since hosted adds compute billed per active session","The code must stay on another cloud or on premises","Your policy needs a documented GA label for every component and the hosted status is unconfirmed"],"flips":["Sustained high concurrency of heavy agents flips the cost dial: concurrency times allocation drives the bill","A requirement to run in another cloud flips you to self-hosting with registration for governance","Only a webhook or batch need appears: still hosted, but use Invocations rather than Responses"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Hosted agents versus the neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Hosted agent","Custom code with managed endpoint, identity and session state","Config would do, or code must run elsewhere","Compute per active session [[r2]]"],["Prompt agent","Least to manage","Custom orchestration or files","Less control"],["Direct Responses API call","Definition ships with your app","You want Foundry to host and version it","You host and secure it [[r1]]"],["Self-hosted on AKS or Container Apps","Code must stay in your estate, full network control","You want managed per-session state and identity","You operate scaling, identity wiring and patching"],["Copilot Studio agent","Low-code makers, Microsoft 365 channels","You need code-level control","Different governance surface [[tr4]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A guide tells you to set a capability host or use a shared endpoint with `agent_reference`","cause":"It targets the initial-preview backend that ended on 2026-08-20","fix":"Follow the migration page: dedicated endpoint per agent, new adapter packages, redeploy [[r11]]."},{"symptom":"Agent cannot reach your storage account after deploy","cause":"The agent identity only gets model inference and session storage by default","fix":"Assign RBAC roles to the agent identity for any other resource [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"Costs grow faster than traffic","cause":"Oversized sandbox multiplied by concurrent active sessions","fix":"Right-size per version. The docs advise a larger size only if sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation [[r2]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t03-s1","title":"Graph agent needs a managed home","situation":"A team has a working graph-based agent in Python. They want an endpoint, an Entra identity and scaling without running a cluster.","constraints":["Custom orchestration already exists","Azure regions are acceptable","Small ops team"],"unknowns":["Does it need persistent files?","How many concurrent active sessions at peak?"],"question":"Where do you run it?","options":[{"name":"Hosted agent","verdict":"best","why":"Keeps your framework and code and adds a dedicated endpoint, agent identity, scaling and session state.","tradeoff":"Compute is billed per active session and the hosted GA label is unconfirmed."},{"name":"Self-host on Container Apps","verdict":"good","why":"Full control and it works anywhere.","tradeoff":"You wire identity, scaling, session persistence and observability yourself."},{"name":"Rebuild as a prompt agent","verdict":"poor","why":"The graph logic does not fit configuration and would be lost.","tradeoff":"Right when the logic is a simple tool-calling loop."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent is a plain tool-calling loop","then":"Prompt agent, and drop the container."},{"if":"Policy forbids anything without a GA label","then":"Self-host until the hosted status is confirmed for you."}],"takeaway":"Hosted fits existing code that wants a managed endpoint and identity."},{"id":"w12-t03-s2","title":"Spiky Q&A at high concurrency","situation":"A stateless question-and-answer agent sees thousands of short sessions at peak and almost none overnight.","constraints":["No files or custom protocol","Cost is watched closely","Text only"],"unknowns":["Per-session memory need?","Is any custom code required?"],"question":"Which runtime do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Prompt agent","verdict":"best","why":"No compute line: you pay for inference and tools, and Foundry scales it.","tradeoff":"No custom code path."},{"name":"Direct Responses API call from an existing app","verdict":"good","why":"Also avoids sandbox compute and keeps the definition in code.","tradeoff":"You host and secure the caller."},{"name":"Hosted agent at the largest sandbox","verdict":"poor","why":"Billing follows CPU and memory across active sessions, so oversizing multiplies cost by concurrency.","tradeoff":"Right when sessions need code, files or heavy local compute."}],"flips":[{"if":"Each session needs a working directory or custom code","then":"Hosted agent, sized at the smallest tier that holds the peak."},{"if":"Concurrency is low and logic is complex","then":"Hosted cost is small, so code flexibility wins."}],"takeaway":"Hosted cost is concurrency times allocation, so match the runtime to the work."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t03-q1","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which needs are listed by the docs as reasons to choose a hosted agent over a prompt agent?","options":["Bring your own code or framework","Use custom protocols such as webhooks","Avoid all compute billing","Persist files and state across turns with `$HOME` and `/files`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The hosted agents page lists own code, custom protocols, compute control, stateful files and resilient long-lived work. Hosted adds compute, so it does not avoid billing.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An initial-preview hosted agent stopped working after 2026-08-20. What is the correct fix?","options":["Redeploy on the new model with a dedicated endpoint and the new adapter packages","Raise the idle timeout to 60 minutes","Add a second identity to the project","Switch the protocol to Activity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Support for the initial-preview backend ended and redeploy is required. Idle timeout, identities and protocol choice do not address a retired backend.","ref":"r11"},{"id":"w12-t03-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most directly flips the choice from a hosted agent to a prompt agent?","options":["Need to receive webhooks","Need persistent files","The behavior now fits instructions, a model and tools","Need to choose CPU size"],"answer":2,"explain":"A prompt agent covers behavior expressed as configuration. The other three are the reasons hosted agents exist.","ref":"r1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does Foundry create automatically when a hosted agent is deployed?","back":"A dedicated Microsoft Entra agent identity and a dedicated endpoint. Publishing is not needed for programmatic access."},{"front":"Is there an explicit GA statement for hosted agents (checked 2026-10-13)?","back":"UNVERIFIED. The GA table has no hosted row and the concept page has no preview banner. Confirm status before a GA-only policy decision."},{"front":"How are hosted agents billed beyond inference?","back":"CPU and memory consumed across active sessions. Numeric rates are on the pricing page and are UNVERIFIED in this guide."}],"related":["w12-t04","w12-t05","w12-t06","w12-t19","w11-t25","w12-l2"],"refs":["r1","r2","r9","r11","tr2","tr4"]},{"id":"w12-t04","order":4,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Hosted agent protocols","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A hosted agent can expose Responses, Invocations, Invocations (WebSocket), Activity and A2A. The protocol decides the payload your container receives and how much session, streaming and background work the platform manages for you.","useIf":"You are choosing how clients, channels or other systems will call a hosted agent.","avoidIf":"The agent is a prompt agent, where the platform picks the protocol for you.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A hosted agent container can expose one or more protocols, each provided by a lightweight library that handles the HTTP or WebSocket server, health checks and OpenTelemetry [[r2]]. **Responses** is an OpenAI-compatible `/responses` contract where the platform manages conversation history, streaming events and background execution. **Invocations** takes arbitrary JSON at `/invocations`: you define the schema, manage sessions and write raw SSE. **Invocations (WebSocket)** is a persistent bidirectional connection for real-time voice. **Activity** serves Teams and Microsoft 365 channels, and the platform bridges Responses to Activity when you publish, so no separate wiring is needed. **A2A** handles agent-to-agent delegation, with v1.0 GA and v0.3 preview [[r2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The choice is not permanent: one agent can support several protocols. The docs say to start with Responses and add an Invocations endpoint later [[r2]]. Declare protocols in the `azure.ai.agent` service in `azure.yaml` when using `azd`, or with `protocol_versions` in the SDK. Only the declared protocols have active endpoints [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t04-d1","title":"Picking a protocol","alt":"Decision tree: chat-style client leads to Responses. Real-time voice or two-way streaming leads to Invocations WebSocket. Webhooks, batch work and custom payloads lead to Invocations. Teams publishing adds Activity through an automatic bridge.","src":"flowchart TB\n  Q{Who calls the agent?} -->|Chat client| R[Responses]\n  Q -->|Voice or two-way stream| W[Invocations WebSocket]\n  Q -->|Webhook, batch, custom payload| I[Invocations]\n  R --> T[Activity bridge for Teams and M365]"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","title":"Endpoint shapes from the docs (checked 2026-10-13)","code":"Responses:   {project_endpoint}/agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/openai/responses\nInvocations: {project_endpoint}/agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/invocations\nWebSocket:   wss://{account}.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/{project}/agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/invocations_ws?api-version=v1\nA2A:         {project_endpoint}/agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/a2a","caption":"Each hosted agent gets its own endpoint at deploy time. Publishing is not needed for programmatic access [[r2]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Responses versus Invocations","head":["Dial","Responses","Invocations"],"rows":[["Payload","OpenAI-compatible","Arbitrary JSON you define"],["Client","Any OpenAI-compatible SDK","A custom client"],["Session history","Platform-managed through a conversation ID","You manage it (memory, Cosmos DB and so on)"],["Streaming","Platform-managed events","Raw SSE you write"],["Background work","`background: true`, polling and cancellation built in","Resilient tasks in the AgentServer SDK, you define polling or streaming"]]}],"whereToUse":["Chat assistants and RAG with tools: Responses","Webhook receivers from systems that send their own payload: Invocations","Classification, extraction and batch jobs with structured input: Invocations","AG-UI and other custom streaming protocols: Invocations","Browser or phone voice clients paired with Pipecat, LiveKit or Voice Live in the container: Invocations (WebSocket)"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Responses when the caller is conversational and you want platform-managed history and background mode","Invocations when the caller has a payload you cannot change or you need raw streaming control","WebSocket when audio flows both ways on one connection"],"avoid":["Invocations for a plain chat bot, since you take on session handling and SSE formatting for no gain","Responses for a webhook, since the sender will not match the `/responses` contract","Invocations WebSocket for ordinary request and response calls, since you then manage a long-lived connection you do not need"],"flips":["Publishing to Teams or Microsoft 365 pushes you toward Responses, which the platform bridges to Activity","A second client type later is fine: add an Invocations endpoint to the same agent","Needing recovery after a process restart adds the resilient-execution opt-in on top of either protocol (preview)"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Protocols compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Responses","Chat, RAG with tools, Teams or Microsoft 365","Caller has its own payload","Fits the OpenAI contract only"],["Invocations","Webhooks, batch, AG-UI, protocol bridges","Simple chat","You own sessions and SSE"],["Invocations (WebSocket)","Real-time voice, two-way streaming","Request and response work","Long-lived connections, more client work"],["Activity","Teams and Microsoft 365 delivery","Anything else","Bridged from Responses, not hand-wired [[r2]]"],["A2A","Other agents delegate to this one","Human chat clients","Version negotiation: v1.0 GA, v0.3 preview"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A webhook sender gets 400 errors from the agent","cause":"The agent only declares Responses, so the sender's payload does not match","fix":"Declare Invocations in `azure.yaml` or `protocol_versions` and parse the sender's schema."},{"symptom":"History is lost between calls on an Invocations agent","cause":"Invocations leaves session history to you","fix":"Persist sessions yourself, or use Responses with a conversation ID if the caller is conversational [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"Background work vanishes after the process restarts","cause":"Background mode keeps work alive after the request returns but does not recover after the process stops","fix":"Opt into resilient execution for stored background responses (preview) and keep progress in durable checkpoints [[tr2]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t04-s1","title":"Ticketing system calls the agent","situation":"An external system posts events in its own JSON format and cannot change them. The agent classifies each event and returns structured JSON.","constraints":["Payload is fixed by the sender","No chat, no human in the loop","Medium volume"],"unknowns":["Does the sender expect a synchronous answer?","How long can classification take?"],"question":"Which protocol do you declare?","options":[{"name":"Invocations","verdict":"best","why":"Arbitrary JSON in and out matches webhooks and structured extraction.","tradeoff":"You define the schema and manage any session state."},{"name":"Responses behind a translating service you run","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the OpenAI contract inside Foundry.","tradeoff":"An extra hop and component to operate."},{"name":"Responses directly","verdict":"poor","why":"The sender cannot change its payload to match `/responses`.","tradeoff":"Right when the caller is a chat client."}],"flips":[{"if":"The same agent must also serve a chat UI","then":"Expose both Responses and Invocations on one agent."},{"if":"Work takes minutes and must survive restarts","then":"Use resilient tasks (preview) and a polling endpoint."}],"takeaway":"Invocations is the door for callers whose payload you do not control."},{"id":"w12-t04-s2","title":"Chat assistant published to Teams","situation":"A chat assistant with RAG and tools must appear in Teams and Microsoft 365 and support long answers that stream.","constraints":["Conversational","Teams and Microsoft 365 publishing","Small team"],"unknowns":["Any custom streaming UI?","Do answers run longer than a request timeout?"],"question":"What do you pick?","options":[{"name":"Responses","verdict":"best","why":"Platform-managed history and streaming, any OpenAI-compatible SDK as client, and the platform bridges it to Activity for channels.","tradeoff":"You accept the OpenAI contract shape."},{"name":"Invocations only","verdict":"poor","why":"You would manage sessions and raw SSE and lose the automatic Activity bridge from Responses.","tradeoff":"Right for AG-UI or other custom streaming UIs."},{"name":"Responses plus Invocations","verdict":"good","why":"Covers Teams and a custom client.","tradeoff":"Two contracts to test and secure."}],"flips":[{"if":"A custom AG-UI front end is added","then":"Add Invocations for that client."},{"if":"The assistant becomes voice-first","then":"Add Invocations (WebSocket)."}],"takeaway":"Default to Responses, and add endpoints as clients appear."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t04-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each caller to the protocol the docs recommend.","pairs":[{"l":"Conversational chatbot","r":"Responses"},{"l":"Webhook from a third-party system","r":"Invocations"},{"l":"Microphone in, speech out","r":"Invocations (WebSocket)"},{"l":"Teams or Microsoft 365 channel delivery","r":"Activity (bridged from Responses)"}],"explain":"Responses suits chat, Invocations handles payloads you do not control, the WebSocket variant carries bidirectional audio, and Activity is bridged automatically from Responses for channels.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"On a hosted agent, which A2A versions are GA and preview?","options":["v1.0 preview and v0.3 GA","v1.0 GA and v0.3 preview","Both are GA","Both are preview"],"answer":1,"explain":"The hosted agents page marks A2A v1.0 as GA and v0.3 as preview.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t04-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Your Invocations agent returns an answer that forgets the previous turn. Which statement explains it?","options":["Invocations leaves session history to your code","Invocations deletes sessions after 2 minutes","The Activity bridge strips history","A2A is overriding it"],"answer":0,"explain":"With Invocations you manage sessions yourself, for example in memory or Cosmos DB. Responses is the protocol where the platform keeps history by conversation ID.","ref":"r2"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Default advice for choosing a hosted agent protocol?","back":"Start with Responses. A hosted agent can expose several protocols, so you can add Invocations later."},{"front":"Where do you declare the protocols of a hosted agent?","back":"In the `azure.ai.agent` service in `azure.yaml` with `azd`, or with `protocol_versions` in the SDK. Only declared protocols have active endpoints."}],"related":["w12-t03","w12-t05","w12-t13","w09-t10","w11-t25","w12-l3"],"refs":["r2","r5","tr2"]},{"id":"w12-t05","order":5,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Sessions, sandbox lifecycle and state","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Compute follows the session, not the request. Each session gets a VM-isolated sandbox that moves between Active, Idle and Resumed, with `$HOME` and `/files` persisted across idle periods and deleted after 30 days of inactivity.","useIf":"You are designing where a hosted agent keeps working files, history and checkpoints, and how long they live.","avoidIf":"You run a prompt agent, where the platform owns conversation state and you have no sandbox to manage.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **session ID** names a logical session with persisted state. The platform provisions compute on demand and restores the state onto it [[r2]]. Each session has its own VM-isolated sandbox with a persistent filesystem (`$HOME` and `/files`), which gives scale-to-zero with stateful resume and predictable cold starts [[r2]]. Files uploaded through `/files` land in `$HOME`. State is persisted when a session goes idle and restored on resume [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t05-d1","title":"Session lifecycle","alt":"A session is Active while handling requests, becomes Idle after the idle timeout with state persisted, is Resumed on the next request, and is deleted after 30 days idle.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Active: first request\n  Active --> Idle: idle timeout, state persisted\n  Idle --> Resumed: next request, state restored\n  Resumed --> Idle: idle timeout\n  Idle --> [*]: deleted after 30 days idle"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Idle timeout","value":"2 to 60 min","note":"Set per agent version, default 15 [[r2]]"},{"label":"Session deletion","value":"30 days idle","note":"Permanent [[r2]]"},{"label":"Sandbox sizes","value":"3 tiers","note":"0.5 vCPU 1 GiB, 1 vCPU 2 GiB, 2 vCPU 4 GiB [[r2]]"},{"label":"Disk budget","value":"Up to 20 GiB","note":"At 1 vCPU or larger, about 20 percent reserved, shared with the image and `$HOME` [[r2]]"}]},{"t":"p","md":"Besides the filesystem there is a **durable key-value state store** that survives crashes and idle eviction, can be partitioned per end user, and ages items out after a 30 day idle window by default [[r2]]. With the Responses protocol the platform keeps conversation history by conversation ID. With Invocations you manage sessions yourself [[r2]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Long-running resilience (preview, checked 2026-10-13)","md":"Background execution lets work continue after the request returns. Resilient execution lets a later process recover work after a crash or restart. They solve different problems [[tr2]]. Recovery **reenters the handler from its beginning**. It is not deterministic replay and does not restore local variables, so the handler must use durable checkpoints, idempotency keys or watermarks. Full crash recovery for Responses applies only to stored background responses that opt in [[tr2]]."}],"whereToUse":["Agents that keep a working directory, downloaded files or caches across turns","Per-user workspaces where each end user maps to one session or state-store partition","Long extraction or research jobs that must survive a restart (preview resilience features)","Choosing idle timeouts per version to trade resume latency against active-session cost"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use `$HOME` for files that belong to one session and may sit idle for a while","Use the state store for small key-value state that must outlive a crash","Use external storage you own for anything that must outlive 30 idle days or be shared across sessions"],"avoid":["Do not rely on in-memory variables or paths outside `$HOME` and `/files` surviving idle (inference from what is documented as persisted)","Do not treat the session as a database: it is deleted after 30 days of inactivity","Do not size the sandbox for the whole agent: the cpu and memory you set describe one session, and billing multiplies by active sessions"],"flips":["Data must be shared between users or sessions: move it to external storage","Resume latency hurts the user experience: raise the idle timeout, accepting more active-session time","A job outlives a single process: add resilient execution and idempotent checkpoints"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where state can live","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["`$HOME` and `/files`","Per-session working files across turns","Data must outlive 30 idle days or be shared","Counts against the disk budget, deleted with the session"],["Durable key-value state store","Small per-user state that survives crashes","You need records older than the 30 day idle window","Items age out by default [[r2]]"],["Platform conversation (Responses)","Chat history by conversation ID","Invocations callers or custom schemas","Tied to the Responses contract"],["External database or blob store","Long-lived or shared data, audits","Tiny, short-lived scratch data","You secure, back up and pay for it"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A user returns after five weeks and the workspace files are gone","cause":"The session was permanently deleted after 30 days of inactivity","fix":"Keep long-lived data in storage you own and treat session state as a cache [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"After a crash the agent sends a duplicate email or payment","cause":"Recovery reenters the handler from the beginning","fix":"Persist an idempotency key or side-effect watermark before the unsafe call and check it on re-entry [[tr2]]."},{"symptom":"Sandbox runs out of disk with a small image","cause":"The disk budget is shared with the image and `$HOME` and about 20 percent is reserved","fix":"Right-size the tier, prune uploads and offload large artifacts to storage [[r2]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t05-s1","title":"Per-user data that must last 90 days","situation":"A hosted agent builds a per-user knowledge workspace. Users come back every few weeks and the data must remain for 90 days after the last visit.","constraints":["Data must outlive 30 idle days","Data may be shared across a user's sessions","Azure storage is available"],"unknowns":["Is the data sensitive or regulated?","How large is each workspace?"],"question":"Where does the workspace live?","options":[{"name":"External database or blob storage keyed by user","verdict":"best","why":"It outlives the 30 day session deletion and the 30 day state-store aging, and it can be shared across sessions.","tradeoff":"You build access control, backup and RBAC for the agent identity."},{"name":"Durable key-value state store partitioned per user","verdict":"good","why":"Survives crashes and idle eviction and partitions per end user.","tradeoff":"Items age out after a 30 day idle window by default, so it does not meet 90 days without checking configuration."},{"name":"Files in `$HOME`","verdict":"poor","why":"The session is deleted after 30 days of inactivity, taking `$HOME` with it.","tradeoff":"Right for working files that only matter during an active engagement."}],"flips":[{"if":"Retention only needs to be 14 days","then":"The state store or `$HOME` becomes acceptable and simpler."},{"if":"Data is regulated","then":"External storage with your own retention and deletion controls."}],"takeaway":"Session state is a cache with a 30 day horizon, not a system of record."},{"id":"w12-t05-s2","title":"Forty-minute job that sometimes crashes","situation":"A document extraction run takes about forty minutes and occasionally dies from memory pressure. Clients disconnect while it runs.","constraints":["Preview features are allowed","Steps call an external API that must not be repeated","Clients can poll"],"unknowns":["Can each step be checkpointed?","How large are intermediate artifacts?"],"question":"How do you design for recovery?","options":[{"name":"Resilient execution with durable checkpoints and idempotency keys","verdict":"best","why":"Recovery reenters the handler with the same identities, and checkpoints plus watermarks skip finished work and the unsafe call.","tradeoff":"Preview, and you must write the resume logic."},{"name":"Background mode only","verdict":"poor","why":"It keeps work going after the request returns but does not recover after the owning process stops.","tradeoff":"Right for work that is cheap to rerun or tolerates loss."},{"name":"Foreground call with a client retry","verdict":"good","why":"Simple, and a retry reruns from the start.","tradeoff":"Repeats forty minutes of work and may repeat the external call."}],"flips":[{"if":"The job takes seconds and is safe to rerun","then":"Safe rerun, with no checkpoints."},{"if":"Preview is not allowed","then":"Own the recovery in your framework checkpointer and an external job store."}],"takeaway":"Background is not resilience, and recovery restarts the handler, so design for idempotency."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t05-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the documented life of a hosted agent session.","items":["A request arrives and the sandbox is Active","The idle timeout fires and `$HOME` and `/files` are persisted","A later request restores state and the session is Resumed","After 30 days of inactivity the session is permanently deleted"],"explain":"Active comes first, idle persists state, resume restores it, and deletion happens only after 30 inactive days.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t05-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"At 1 vCPU the disk budget is up to 20 GiB with about 20 percent reserved. About how many GiB remain to share between the image and `$HOME`?","answer":16,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"GiB","hint":"20 times 0.8","explain":"Reserve about 4 GiB (20 percent of 20), leaving about 16 GiB. This is derived from the documented figures, so treat it as an estimate.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After a crash a recovered handler repeats a non-idempotent call. What is the cause?","options":["Background mode replays outputs","The idle timeout is too short","Recovery reenters the handler from the beginning and the handler had no watermark","The session was resumed on a larger sandbox"],"answer":2,"explain":"Resilient recovery is not deterministic replay. The handler restarts from its beginning and must use checkpoints or watermarks. Idle timeout and sandbox size are unrelated.","ref":"tr2"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is persisted when a hosted agent session goes idle?","back":"`$HOME` and `/files` content. State is restored on resume. The session is deleted after 30 days of inactivity."},{"front":"Background mode versus resilient execution?","back":"Background keeps work running after the request returns. Resilience lets a later process recover it after a crash. Recovery restarts the handler, so use checkpoints (preview)."}],"related":["w12-t03","w12-t06","w10-t21","w11-t24","w12-l2"],"refs":["r2","tr2"]},{"id":"w12-t06","order":6,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Versions, scaling, networking and identity of hosted agents","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Hosted agent versions are immutable snapshots served one at a time. Scale is per session with billing on allocated CPU and memory, networking supports bring-your-own VNet, and every agent gets its own Entra identity.","useIf":"You are sizing, releasing, isolating or authorizing a hosted agent in production.","avoidIf":"You are still choosing between agent types: read the overview and runtime matrix first.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Versions.** Each create-version call produces an immutable snapshot of the image, resources, environment variables and protocol configuration. An endpoint serves **one version** and sends 100 percent of traffic to it. **Traffic splitting is not supported** [[r2]]. A rollback is a change of the active version: in the portal you pick \"Always use latest\" or a specific version, and over REST you patch `agent_endpoint.version_selector` with an `agent_version` (the documented example uses a `FixedRatio` rule at `traffic_percentage` 100) [[r5]]. Whether the selector accepts ratios below 100 for other agent types is UNVERIFIED. For a canary on a hosted agent you need routing outside the platform, for example two agent names behind your own gateway (inference)."},{"t":"p","md":"**Scaling.** Scale is per session: no replica count and no warm pool. Billing is CPU plus memory across active sessions, so an oversized tier multiplies cost by concurrency. The docs advise moving to the next size when sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation. Numeric rates are UNVERIFIED here because the docs only link to the pricing page [[r2]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Allocated compute at peak","expr":"allocated vCPU = concurrent active sessions x vCPU per session","vars":[{"k":"concurrent active sessions","v":"Sessions in the Active state at the same time"},{"k":"vCPU per session","v":"0.5, 1 or 2 depending on the tier"}],"example":"200 active sessions at 2 vCPU allocate 400 vCPU. At 1 vCPU they allocate 200 vCPU, half the compute, if sustained usage stays below the 70 percent trigger."},{"t":"p","md":"**Networking.** Prompt agents support private networking. Hosted agents support bring-your-own VNet, with each session in a VM-isolated sandbox connected to your VNet [[r1]]. A private Azure Container Registry works only for projects created after 2026-06-25, and portal publishing is not supported when public network access is disabled [[r2]] [[r5]]. Network egress controls for hosted agents are preview and live in the same guardrail [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Identity.** Each agent gets a dedicated Entra agent identity at deploy, used for model inference, tool access and downstream services. A separate project managed identity does infrastructure work such as pulling from the registry [[r2]]. The agent identity can call inference and session storage by default. Anything else needs manual RBAC [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t06-d1","title":"Two identities, two jobs","alt":"The agent identity reaches models and session storage by default and other resources only with added RBAC. The project managed identity pulls the image from the registry.","src":"flowchart LR\n  AI[Agent identity] --> MI[Model inference]\n  AI --> SS[Session storage]\n  AI -. RBAC added .-> OT[Your storage or APIs]\n  PM[Project managed identity] --> ACR[Container Registry pull]"}],"whereToUse":["Release planning: deciding how to ship a risky change when one version takes all traffic","Capacity reviews that compare tier choices against sustained peak usage","Landing-zone design for projects with private networking and a private registry","Granting an agent least-privilege access to storage, databases and APIs"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Pin each release to an immutable version so you can reason about what is running","Start at the smallest tier that holds your peak and step up on measured saturation","Give the agent identity only the roles it needs on named resources"],"avoid":["Do not plan a weighted rollout on one endpoint: it is not supported","Do not use the project managed identity as the agent's runtime identity: it is for platform infrastructure","Do not use an old project for private-registry designs: it must be created after 2026-06-25"],"flips":["Need a gradual rollout: add routing in your gateway or client across two agents","Public network access must be off: publish through the REST API rather than the portal","Sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation: ship a new version on the next tier"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Release and sizing choices","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Swap the single served version","Change is low risk, fast rollback matters","You want to test on a slice of users","All traffic moves at once"],["Two agents with external routing (inference)","You need a canary or A/B split","You want zero extra moving parts","Two identities, endpoints and RBAC sets to manage"],["Smallest tier, step up on saturation","Cost control, bursty concurrency","Memory-heavy tools OOM at the small tier","Resume or restart pain if undersized"],["Largest tier by default","Rare, heavy single-user agents","High concurrency","Cost multiplies with active sessions [[r2]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"401 or 403 from a downstream service even though RBAC looks right","cause":"Token audience is the MCP server URL instead of the downstream resource ID (for example `https://storage.azure.com`)","fix":"Request the token for the resource ID audience and re-test [[r4]]."},{"symptom":"Agent cannot read your storage account","cause":"Only inference and session storage are allowed by default","fix":"Assign roles to the agent identity on that resource [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"Private registry setup fails in an existing project","cause":"Private Container Registry works only for projects created after 2026-06-25","fix":"Create a new project, or keep the public registry path until you can migrate [[r2]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t06-s1","title":"Ten percent canary","situation":"A new version changes the tool-calling logic and the team wants it to see ten percent of traffic first.","constraints":["Endpoint serves one version","Rollback must be quick","Two teams own the gateway and the agent"],"unknowns":["Is there a gateway in front of the agent?","Can sessions be pinned per user?"],"question":"How do you roll it out?","options":[{"name":"Deploy a second agent and route ten percent in your gateway or client","verdict":"best","why":"The platform does not split traffic, so the split must sit in front of two endpoints (inference).","tradeoff":"Two identities and RBAC sets to keep in sync, and routing logic to maintain."},{"name":"Swap the served version and watch metrics","verdict":"good","why":"Quick and rollback is serving the earlier immutable version.","tradeoff":"All traffic sees the change at once."},{"name":"Configure a 90/10 weight on the endpoint","verdict":"poor","why":"Traffic splitting is not supported on a hosted agent endpoint.","tradeoff":"It would be right on a platform that offers weighted routing."}],"flips":[{"if":"The change is a low-risk prompt tweak","then":"Swap the version and keep the old one for rollback."},{"if":"Users must stay on one version per session","then":"Pin the route per user in the gateway."}],"takeaway":"One endpoint serves one version, so build canaries outside it."},{"id":"w12-t06-s2","title":"Locked-down network","situation":"A regulated team needs a hosted agent in a project with public network access disabled, a private registry and Teams publishing.","constraints":["No public network access","Private Container Registry","Teams publishing required"],"unknowns":["When was the project created?","Is a public Microsoft 365 endpoint acceptable?"],"question":"What do you set up?","options":[{"name":"New project, bring-your-own VNet, private registry, REST publish with `enable_m365_public_endpoint`","verdict":"best","why":"A private registry needs a project created after 2026-06-25, and portal publishing is unsupported without public access, so the REST route is the documented path.","tradeoff":"Heavier setup and an explicit public Microsoft 365 endpoint decision."},{"name":"Re-enable public access and use the portal","verdict":"good","why":"Works with the least effort.","tradeoff":"Breaks the stated network policy."},{"name":"Reuse an older project and add a private registry","verdict":"poor","why":"The private registry works only for projects created after 2026-06-25.","tradeoff":"Right only if the project already meets the date."}],"flips":[{"if":"The project already postdates 2026-06-25","then":"Reuse it and skip the new project."},{"if":"Policy allows public access for the publish step","then":"Portal publishing becomes possible."}],"takeaway":"Check the project creation date and the publish path before promising private networking."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t06-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about hosted agent versions are supported by the docs?","options":["Each create-version call makes an immutable snapshot","An endpoint serves one version and sends it 100 percent of traffic","You can split traffic 90/10 between two versions","A snapshot includes image, resources, environment variables and protocol config"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Versions are immutable snapshots of image, resources, environment and protocol config, and an endpoint serves one version. Traffic splitting is not supported.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t06-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A version is allocated 2 vCPU and sustained peak usage is 1.5 vCPU. What percent of allocation is that? (The docs suggest stepping up above about 70 percent.)","answer":75,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"%","hint":"1.5 divided by 2","explain":"1.5 / 2 = 75 percent, which is above the 70 percent trigger, so ship a new version on the next tier.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w12-t06-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Calls to a storage account fail with the right RBAC role but a token whose audience is the MCP server URL. What fixes it?","options":["Request the token for the downstream resource ID audience","Assign Owner to the project","Switch to the project managed identity","Increase the idle timeout"],"answer":0,"explain":"The audience must be the resource ID, such as `https://storage.azure.com`. A mismatch fails even with correct RBAC. More roles, a different identity or timeouts do not change the audience.","ref":"r4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What identities does a hosted agent involve?","back":"A dedicated Entra agent identity per agent (runtime) and a project managed identity (platform work such as registry pulls). Agent identity gets inference and session storage by default."},{"front":"Can a hosted agent endpoint split traffic between versions?","back":"No. An endpoint serves one immutable version at 100 percent of traffic. Build canaries outside the endpoint."},{"front":"Cutoff date for private Container Registry on hosted agents?","back":"Only projects created after 2026-06-25 support a private registry."}],"related":["w12-t03","w12-t05","w12-t13","w12-t15","w12-t16","w16-t11"],"refs":["r1","r2","r4","r5","r8"]},{"id":"w12-t07","order":7,"group":"Foundry Agent Service","title":"Responses API direct vs agents","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Calling the Responses API from your own code gives an ephemeral agent: the definition lives in your app, nothing is created in Foundry, yet you still use Foundry models, tools, OBO auth and project governance.","useIf":"The agent already runs in your own process and the definition should ship and version with the application.","avoidIf":"You want Foundry to host, version, publish or give a stable endpoint to the agent.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The runtime has three objects: an **agent** (reusable model, instructions and tools), a **conversation** (persisted input and output items) and a **response** (one execution) [[tr1]]. A response can run with or without the other two, so the lightest design is a response alone [[tr1]]. When you call the Responses API directly, the agent's instructions, tools and model live in your code. Each call assembles the agent in your process, so there is nothing to create, update or delete in Foundry [[r1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"You still get catalog models, platform tools, project-scoped data, On-Behalf-Of authentication and project-level observability and governance through your project endpoint [[r1]]. What you give up is Foundry hosting, a versioned agent resource and the publishing flow that hangs off it. **State is a dial:** use a conversation for server-side history, reference a previous stored response, or resend earlier items [[tr1]]. Resending grows the input each turn. Ephemeral does not mean stateless: it only means the definition is not stored in Foundry. The trade-off is ownership. You must keep the definition consistent across app instances, secure the calling process and decide how to version changes, which a Foundry agent resource would otherwise do for you."},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-t07-pick","title":"Responses direct, prompt agent or hosted agent","prompt":"Flip the three dials and watch the ranking change.","dials":[{"id":"where","label":"Where should the agent definition live?","values":[{"id":"app","label":"In app code"},{"id":"foundry","label":"As a Foundry resource"}]},{"id":"code","label":"Custom code inside the agent runtime","values":[{"id":"none","label":"None needed"},{"id":"custom","label":"Required"}]},{"id":"compute","label":"Who runs the process","values":[{"id":"mine","label":"I already run compute"},{"id":"foundry","label":"Foundry runs it"}]}],"options":[{"id":"eph","name":"Responses API direct","summary":"Ephemeral agent defined in your code."},{"id":"prompt","name":"Prompt agent","summary":"Config-only agent that Foundry runs."},{"id":"hosted","name":"Hosted agent","summary":"Your code, run by Foundry."}],"scores":{"eph":{"where":{"app":{"s":2,"why":"The definition ships and versions with the application code [[r1]]."},"foundry":{"s":-2,"why":"There is no Foundry agent resource to hold the definition."}},"code":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Fine, and the app stays small."},"custom":{"s":1,"why":"Your own process can hold any code you need."}},"compute":{"mine":{"s":2,"why":"You already host the caller, so there is no new compute."},"foundry":{"s":-2,"why":"Foundry runs nothing for you in this pattern."}}},"prompt":{"where":{"app":{"s":-1,"why":"Code-first definitions are possible, but the agent still lives in Foundry."},"foundry":{"s":2,"why":"A persisted, versioned resource with a stable endpoint."}},"code":{"none":{"s":2,"why":"Configuration covers it with nothing to maintain."},"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"No place for custom runtime code."}},"compute":{"mine":{"s":-1,"why":"You would still call a Foundry-run agent."},"foundry":{"s":2,"why":"No containers or compute to manage."}}},"hosted":{"where":{"app":{"s":0,"why":"Code is in your repo but deploys as a Foundry resource."},"foundry":{"s":1,"why":"Versions, endpoint and identity are Foundry resources [[r2]]."}},"code":{"none":{"s":-1,"why":"Container effort for no gain."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Your framework, protocols and files [[r2]]."}},"compute":{"mine":{"s":-1,"why":"It runs on Foundry-managed sandboxes, not your hosts."},"foundry":{"s":2,"why":"Foundry provisions per-session compute [[r2]]."}}}},"takeaway":"Where the definition lives and who runs the process decide most of the choice."}],"whereToUse":["A product backend that embeds an agent and releases it with the rest of the application","Agents already running on another platform that need Foundry models, tools and OBO auth","Prototypes and tests where creating Foundry agent resources is overhead","Services that need project-level observability and governance without a hosted runtime"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The definition should live in source control with the app and pass the app's code review","You already operate the process that makes the calls","You want Foundry capabilities without a resource to keep in sync"],"avoid":["You want a stable Foundry endpoint, versions and publishing to Teams or Microsoft 365 for the agent itself","No one on the team wants to host or secure the calling process","You need a persisted agent definition that other teams or tools can discover, version and publish"],"flips":["A non-engineering owner must edit the agent: use a prompt agent","You must run custom code but cannot host it: use a hosted agent","Context grows with each turn: move from resending items to a conversation or stored response reference"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Responses API direct (ephemeral)","Definition in app code, you run the host","You want Foundry to host, version or publish it","You operate and secure the process [[r1]]"],["Prompt agent","Config only, Foundry runs it","Custom runtime code is needed","Little control beyond config"],["Hosted agent","Custom code on managed compute","Behavior is config only","Compute per active session [[r2]]"],["Prompt agent defined code-first","Review and CI/CD for a Foundry-run agent","You want no separate resource","Portal edits can drift"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Follow-up questions lose context","cause":"Each call is a fresh response with no conversation, previous response reference or resent items","fix":"Create a conversation, reference the previous stored response, or resend items on purpose [[tr1]]."},{"symptom":"Team expects to publish the agent to Teams from Foundry","cause":"Publishing works on Foundry agent resources, and an ephemeral agent has none","fix":"Create a prompt or hosted agent for the publish path [[r1]]."},{"symptom":"Agent logic differs between environments","cause":"Definitions are built in each process, and versions of the app drift","fix":"Treat the definition as code: one module, pinned model, reviewed changes."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t07-s1","title":"Agent inside a product backend","situation":"A product team embeds an assistant in its service. The instructions and tools change with every release and the service already runs on its own compute.","constraints":["Definition must version with the app","Service is already hosted and secured","On-Behalf-Of calls needed"],"unknowns":["Does anyone outside engineering edit the agent?","Will it need Teams publishing?"],"question":"How do you build on Foundry?","options":[{"name":"Call the Responses API directly (ephemeral agent)","verdict":"best","why":"The definition ships with the app, and you still get models, tools, OBO and project governance without an agent resource to sync.","tradeoff":"No Foundry-hosted endpoint, versions or publishing for the agent."},{"name":"Prompt agent defined through the SDK","verdict":"good","why":"Foundry runs it and you get a versioned resource.","tradeoff":"The definition now lives in two places unless the pipeline owns it."},{"name":"Hosted agent","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds a container, sandbox compute and a second deployment for logic the service already hosts.","tradeoff":"Right when the agent code should be deployed and scaled separately."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent must be published to Teams","then":"Prompt or hosted agent."},{"if":"Multiple services reuse the same agent","then":"A Foundry agent resource avoids copies."}],"takeaway":"Keep the definition where the code that depends on it lives."},{"id":"w12-t07-s2","title":"Org-wide assistant for Teams","situation":"A business team wants an assistant everyone can find in Teams with a stable endpoint and the ability to roll out new versions behind it. They have no developers.","constraints":["Published to Teams and Microsoft 365","No custom code","Portal authoring"],"unknowns":["Is admin approval available?","Which tools are needed?"],"question":"What do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Prompt agent published through Foundry","verdict":"best","why":"A Foundry agent resource gives versions, a stable endpoint and the publishing flow, with no code.","tradeoff":"Limited to what configuration and tools can express."},{"name":"Responses API direct from a custom service","verdict":"poor","why":"No agent resource to publish, and nobody can host the service.","tradeoff":"Right when developers own an app that embeds the agent."},{"name":"Hosted agent","verdict":"good","why":"Publishable and fully flexible.","tradeoff":"Needs developers and container compute for no gain."}],"flips":[{"if":"The assistant needs custom code later","then":"Move to a hosted agent and keep the published endpoint."},{"if":"Developers own the service and want review in Git","then":"Responses direct or a code-first prompt agent."}],"takeaway":"Publishing and versions belong to Foundry agent resources, not to ephemeral agents."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t07-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"What do you still get when you call the Responses API directly with an ephemeral agent?","options":["Catalog models and platform tools","On-Behalf-Of authentication","A versioned Foundry agent resource","Project-level observability and governance"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The docs list models, tools, project data, OBO and project-level observability and governance. There is no persisted agent resource to version.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most clearly moves you from a direct Responses call to a prompt agent?","options":["A non-engineer must edit and publish the agent to Teams","The definition must ship with every app release","The calling service is already hosted","You want fewer Foundry features"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-engineering editing and publishing need a Foundry agent resource. The other options favor keeping the definition in app code.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t07-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A second turn ignores the first. The app makes two Responses calls with no extra parameters. What is missing?","options":["A conversation, a previous response reference or resent items","A larger model","A hosted agent","Voice Live"],"answer":0,"explain":"Context is carried by a conversation, a reference to a previous stored response, or by resending earlier items. Model size or agent type does not add history.","ref":"tr1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is an ephemeral agent in Foundry?","back":"An agent whose definition lives in your code and is assembled per Responses API call. No Foundry agent resource is created, updated or deleted."},{"front":"Three ways to carry context between Responses calls?","back":"Use a conversation, reference a previous stored response, or resend earlier items. Resending grows the input each turn."}],"related":["w12-t01","w12-t02","w12-t03","w12-t19","w01-t11"],"refs":["r1","r2","tr1"]},{"id":"w12-t08","order":8,"group":"Tools and knowledge","title":"Toolboxes and the managed MCP endpoint","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A toolbox curates tools once and exposes them behind one managed MCP-compatible endpoint with central authentication, guardrails and versioning. Any MCP client can use it, not only Foundry agents.","useIf":"Several agents or runtimes share tools, and you want one credential, governance and release point.","avoidIf":"The tool is not toolbox-eligible or must execute inside your own process.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **toolbox** is a managed resource that groups tools behind a single MCP-compatible endpoint. It centralizes authentication (Entra ID and OAuth identity passthrough), applies guardrails at toolbox level and supports **versioning**: create a version, test it, promote it to default, and every consuming agent picks it up with no redeploy [[r3]]. Toolboxes are created in Foundry but are not limited to Foundry agents: any MCP-compatible runtime can consume one, including Agent Framework, LangGraph or custom code [[r3]]. In Agent Framework hosted agents use `FoundryToolbox` in Python or `AddFoundryToolboxes` in .NET [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t08-d1","title":"One endpoint in front of many tools","alt":"Prompt agents, hosted agents, Agent Framework and other MCP clients connect to one toolbox endpoint, which fronts MCP servers, OpenAPI tools, search, code interpreter and A2A agents.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A1[Prompt and hosted agents] --> TB[Toolbox: one MCP endpoint]\n  A2[Agent Framework or LangGraph] --> TB\n  A3[Any MCP client] --> TB\n  TB --> T1[MCP servers]\n  TB --> T2[OpenAPI]\n  TB --> T3[Search and code interpreter]\n  TB --> T4[A2A agents]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Toolbox lifecycle, from the overview page (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Phase","What you do","Status"],"rows":[["Build","Curate tools, publish once, set auth centrally","GA [[r3]]"],["Discover","Tool search exposes only `tool_search` and `call_tool`, with pin, add-context and auto-pin controls","Preview [[r3]]"],["Consume","Agents connect to one endpoint and discover tools at runtime","GA [[r3]]"],["Skills","Versioned, immutable multi-step workflows delivered as MCP resources","Preview [[r3]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Tool search** matters because every tool definition sent with a request adds input tokens, competes for context and can lower selection accuracy when hundreds of tools are present [[r3]]. Tool search and skills work only through a toolbox [[r3]]. Auth options for tools and remote MCP servers are key-based, Entra (agent identity or project managed identity), OAuth identity passthrough (OBO) and unauthenticated where appropriate [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Not everything fits in a toolbox","md":"In a toolbox: MCP, web search, Azure AI Search, code interpreter, file search, OpenAPI, A2A, browser automation, Fabric IQ and Work IQ. Direct integration only: function calling (client-side execution), Grounding with Bing, computer use, image generation, SharePoint, Fabric data agent and Azure Functions [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["A platform team publishing an approved tool catalog for many agent teams","Mixed runtimes (prompt agents, hosted agents, Agent Framework, LangGraph) that must share the same tools","Releasing a tool change once and rolling it out to every consumer through version promotion","Catalogs large enough that sending every tool definition would waste context (tool search, preview)"],"whenToUse":{"use":["More than one agent needs the same tools","You want credentials and guardrails in one governed place","You want to promote tool versions without redeploying agents"],"avoid":["A single agent with one private tool and no reuse: direct integration is less to manage","The tool is on the direct-only list (for example SharePoint or computer use)","Execution must stay in your process: use function calling"],"flips":["Catalog grows to dozens or hundreds of tools: add tool search, accepting preview status","A tool needs per-user identity: use OAuth identity passthrough (OBO) so the user's permissions apply","Policy forbids preview features: use toolbox without tool search or skills"]},"alternatives":{"title":"How tools can reach an agent","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Toolbox endpoint","Shared tools, central auth, versioned promotion","Tool is direct-only or runs in your process","One more resource to govern [[r3]]"],["Direct tool integration","One agent, or a direct-only tool","Many agents repeat the same config","Credentials and configs duplicated"],["Function calling","Execution must stay in your process","You want platform-run tools","Client executes the tool, so the optimizer does not evaluate it [[r25]]"],["Plain remote MCP server connection","Single MCP server, no curation","You need grouping, versions, tool search","No toolbox-level guardrails or promotion"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Model picks the wrong tool or ignores the right one","cause":"Hundreds of tool definitions compete for context and selection accuracy drops","fix":"Use tool search (preview) with pinned critical tools and organization terms as added context [[r3]]."},{"symptom":"A tool call returns 401 although the toolbox is configured","cause":"The token audience is the MCP server URL rather than the downstream resource ID","fix":"Set the audience to the resource ID such as `https://storage.azure.com` [[r4]]."},{"symptom":"Consumers broke after a tool update","cause":"A new toolbox version was promoted to default without testing","fix":"Create a version, test it, then promote. Skills are immutable per version, so pin the one you tested [[r3]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t08-s1","title":"Platform team serving many agents","situation":"A platform team supports ten agent teams that use five common tools. Credentials must be managed centrally and tool changes must roll out without redeploying agents.","constraints":["Mixed runtimes","Central credential control","Promotion without redeploy"],"unknowns":["Do any tools need per-user identity?","Is preview allowed for tool search?"],"question":"How do you expose the tools?","options":[{"name":"One toolbox with versioned promotion","verdict":"best","why":"One endpoint, central auth and guardrails, and promoting a default version updates every consumer.","tradeoff":"A new governed resource that must be tested before each promotion."},{"name":"Copy each tool configuration into every agent","verdict":"poor","why":"Credentials and configs drift across ten teams and a change needs ten redeploys.","tradeoff":"Fine for a single agent with one private tool."},{"name":"Plain MCP server connection per agent","verdict":"good","why":"Reuses the server and is simple to start.","tradeoff":"No grouping, versioning or toolbox-level guardrails."}],"flips":[{"if":"The catalog grows to hundreds of tools","then":"Add tool search (preview) so tool definitions do not flood context."},{"if":"Only one agent uses the tools","then":"Direct integration is less to manage."}],"takeaway":"Toolboxes pay off when sharing and release control matter."},{"id":"w12-t08-s2","title":"Tool that is not toolbox-eligible","situation":"An agent must search a SharePoint site and drive a browser UI through computer use. The platform team wants everything in the toolbox.","constraints":["SharePoint and computer use are required","Toolbox is the team's standard","Low volume"],"unknowns":["Is there an alternative route to the SharePoint content, such as a knowledge base?","Who approves the direct credentials?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Use direct tool integration for those tools and keep the rest in the toolbox","verdict":"best","why":"SharePoint and computer use appear in the direct-only column of the support table.","tradeoff":"Two paths to govern, and those tools sit outside toolbox versioning."},{"name":"Insist on the toolbox for all tools","verdict":"poor","why":"The toolbox does not support those tools, so the requirement cannot be met.","tradeoff":"Right for tools in the supported list."},{"name":"Wrap them as client-side functions","verdict":"good","why":"You can reach the same systems from your own code.","tradeoff":"You build, secure and run the wrappers, and the optimizer cannot evaluate their execution."}],"flips":[{"if":"SharePoint content can come through a Foundry IQ knowledge base","then":"Consume the knowledge base instead of the direct tool."},{"if":"The tool list in the docs expands to include them","then":"Move them into the toolbox."}],"takeaway":"Check the support table before you standardize on a path."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t08-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each tool to where the docs say it can be used.","pairs":[{"l":"Code interpreter","r":"Toolbox and direct integration"},{"l":"SharePoint","r":"Direct integration only"},{"l":"Function calling","r":"Direct only, executed client-side"},{"l":"Tool search","r":"Toolbox only"}],"explain":"Code interpreter is in both columns. SharePoint, computer use and Azure Functions are direct only. Function calling executes client-side. Tool search and skills exist only through a toolbox.","ref":"r3"},{"id":"w12-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"What is the main reason to add tool search to a large toolbox?","options":["It makes tools run faster","It removes the need for authentication","It keeps tool definitions out of the context until needed","It lets function calling run server-side"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tool search hides tools by default and exposes `tool_search` and `call_tool`, which saves input tokens and improves selection. It does not change execution speed, auth or function calling. It is preview.","ref":"r3"},{"id":"w12-t08-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are documented toolbox capabilities?","options":["One MCP-compatible endpoint","Versioning with promotion to default","Use only by Foundry agents","Centralized authentication and toolbox-level guardrails"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Toolboxes front tools with one endpoint, support version promotion and centralize auth and guardrails. Any MCP-compatible client can consume them, so they are not Foundry-only.","ref":"r3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does a Foundry toolbox give you that a plain MCP server does not?","back":"Curated grouping, central auth, toolbox-level guardrails, versioning with promotion to default, and (preview) tool search and skills."},{"front":"Which tools cannot be added to a toolbox?","back":"Function calling (client-side), Grounding with Bing, computer use, image generation, SharePoint, Fabric data agent and Azure Functions. Use direct integration."}],"related":["w12-t09","w12-t11","w08-t15","w08-t16","w11-t05","w12-l4"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r25"]},{"id":"w12-t09","order":9,"group":"Tools and knowledge","title":"Built-in tools","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Foundry agents act through built-in tools such as file search, code interpreter, web search, Azure AI Search, OpenAPI, SharePoint, Azure Functions and function calling. Each has a different cost, data boundary and toolbox eligibility.","useIf":"You are choosing which tool gives an agent facts, computation or actions, and what each choice costs in data exposure.","avoidIf":"You need permission-aware retrieval across many enterprise sources: look at Foundry IQ knowledge bases first.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Agents act on the world through **tools**. Foundry offers built-in tools such as web search, file search, code interpreter and memory, and lets you add custom tools through functions, OpenAPI specs and MCP servers [[r1]]. Pick by what the agent needs, then check cost, data boundary and whether the tool fits a toolbox [[r3]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t09-d1","title":"Which tool for which need","alt":"Need leads to a tool: user files lead to file search, computing on data to code interpreter, public web facts to web search, your REST API to OpenAPI or function calling, curated organization content to a Foundry IQ knowledge base.","src":"flowchart TB\n  N{What does the agent need?} -->|Facts in user files| F[File search]\n  N -->|Compute on data| C[Code interpreter]\n  N -->|Public web facts| W[Web search]\n  N -->|Call your REST API| O[OpenAPI or function calling]\n  N -->|Curated org content| K[Foundry IQ knowledge base]"},{"t":"p","md":"Three questions narrow the choice. First, does the agent need facts or computation? File search and web search retrieve, while code interpreter computes. Second, where does the data go? File search keeps files in your storage in the standard setup, but web search sends queries to Grounding with Bing under separate terms. Third, who executes the call? Platform tools run in Foundry, while function calling executes in your process. Cost also differs: file search and code interpreter add charges beyond token fees [[tr9]] [[tr10]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Tool notes from the live pages (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Tool","What to know","In a toolbox?"],"rows":[["File search","Grounds the agent in your documents through a vector store. In the standard setup files stay in your storage and your Azure AI Search resource ingests them. Extra charges beyond tokens [[tr9]]","Yes [[r3]]"],["Code interpreter","Model writes and runs Python in a sandbox for analysis and charts. Extra charges. Two simultaneous conversations create two sessions, each active one hour by default with a 30 minute idle timeout [[tr10]]","Yes [[r3]]"],["Web search","Real-time public web with inline citations through Grounding with Bing. The Microsoft DPA does not apply to data sent to it and transfers occur outside compliance and geographic boundaries [[tr11]]","Yes [[r3]]"],["Azure AI Search, OpenAPI, MCP, A2A","Query an index, call REST APIs by spec, reach MCP servers and A2A agents","Yes [[r3]]"],["SharePoint, Azure Functions, computer use, image generation","Direct integration only. Custom MCP servers on Azure Functions use the `/runtime/webhooks/mcp` endpoint [[r1]]","No [[r3]]"],["Function calling","Your code executes the call, so it stays in your process","No, client-side [[r3]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Logic Apps: UNVERIFIED","md":"Logic Apps does not appear in the toolbox support table or the overview's tool list on 2026-10-13. Whether it exists as a built-in tool in the new Agent Service is **UNVERIFIED**. Do not promise it until you confirm in the portal."}],"whereToUse":["File search for assistants that answer from documents users upload or from a modest managed document set","Code interpreter for spreadsheet analysis, math and chart generation","Web search for current public facts where exposure to Bing grounding terms is acceptable","OpenAPI or MCP for actions on your own services, and Azure Functions where the function app already exists"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the managed tool when it matches the need, since Foundry runs and scales it","Prefer toolbox-eligible tools when several agents share them","Read the data-boundary note before enabling web search"],"avoid":["Do not use file search as a substitute for permission-aware multi-source retrieval","Do not enable web search on agents that handle data that must stay inside compliance boundaries","Do not leave code interpreter open to unbounded concurrency: each conversation can open a session with extra charges"],"flips":["Content is curated, shared and permission-sensitive: use a Foundry IQ knowledge base","Execution must stay in your process: use function calling","Data may not leave the compliance boundary: drop web search and use curated sources"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Retrieval and action options","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["File search","User-provided or small document sets","Many sources, ACL-aware answers","Extra charges, and an Azure AI Search resource ingests files [[tr9]]"],["Foundry IQ knowledge base","Curated multi-source content with ACL sync","A few ad hoc files in a chat","Partly preview, more setup [[r6]]"],["Azure AI Search tool","You already own a tuned index","You want managed ingestion and sources","You maintain the index"],["Web search","Fresh public facts","Sensitive data or a strict boundary","DPA does not apply to data sent to Bing grounding [[tr11]]"],["Function calling","Logic must run in your process","You want platform-run tools","You build and secure the executor"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Security review blocks the agent after launch","cause":"Web search was on by default and data goes to Grounding with Bing outside the DPA","fix":"Review the terms first and restrict with admin controls or a domain-restricted search [[tr11]]."},{"symptom":"Costs spike on a data-analysis agent","cause":"Each simultaneous conversation opens its own code interpreter session, active one hour by default","fix":"Limit concurrency, shorten sessions and watch the extra charges [[tr10]]."},{"symptom":"File upload fails for the developer","cause":"Missing Storage Blob Data Contributor on the project's storage account or Foundry User on the project","fix":"Assign both roles and retry [[tr9]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t09-s1","title":"Fresh facts under a data boundary","situation":"An assistant should answer questions about recent industry news. Policy says customer data must not leave the compliance boundary.","constraints":["Compliance boundary is strict","Freshness is moderate","A content team can curate sources"],"unknowns":["How fresh must answers be, hours or weeks?","Can legal approve Bing grounding terms?"],"question":"How do you give it current knowledge?","options":[{"name":"Curated sources in a knowledge base or file search, refreshed on a schedule","verdict":"best","why":"Data stays in your boundary and a content team controls what the agent can cite.","tradeoff":"Freshness is only as good as your refresh cycle."},{"name":"Domain-restricted web search after legal review","verdict":"good","why":"Limits results to listed public domains.","tradeoff":"Data sent still falls under Grounding with Bing terms, outside the DPA."},{"name":"General web search enabled by default","verdict":"poor","why":"Data transfers occur outside compliance and geographic boundaries.","tradeoff":"Right for public-information agents with no sensitive inputs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Answers must be minutes-fresh and the inputs carry no sensitive data","then":"General web search is acceptable."},{"if":"Sources span many systems with different permissions","then":"Use a Foundry IQ knowledge base."}],"takeaway":"Web search trades data boundary for freshness."},{"id":"w12-t09-s2","title":"Spreadsheet analysis for each user","situation":"Users upload spreadsheets and ask for aggregates and charts. Volumes are small today and could grow.","constraints":["Computation, not retrieval","Per-user files","Cost must not surprise"],"unknowns":["Peak concurrent conversations?","Do charts need to persist?"],"question":"Which tool do you add?","options":[{"name":"Code interpreter","verdict":"best","why":"The model writes and runs Python in a sandbox, including charts.","tradeoff":"Extra charges, and one session per simultaneous conversation."},{"name":"File search","verdict":"poor","why":"It retrieves passages and does not compute aggregates or render charts.","tradeoff":"Right for answering questions from document text."},{"name":"Function calling to an analytics service you host","verdict":"good","why":"Deterministic and under your control.","tradeoff":"You build and run the service and the client executes the call."}],"flips":[{"if":"Analyses are fixed reports","then":"Function calling to a tested service is more predictable."},{"if":"Concurrency grows sharply","then":"Budget code interpreter sessions or move heavy jobs to a service."}],"takeaway":"Match the tool to retrieval versus computation."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Code interpreter costs doubled when two users chatted at the same time. What explains it?","options":["Each simultaneous conversation creates its own code interpreter session","File search was enabled","The toolbox duplicated the tool","Idle timeout is 30 hours"],"answer":0,"explain":"Simultaneous calls in two conversations create two sessions, each active for one hour by default with a 30 minute idle timeout. The other options do not match the documented behavior.","ref":"tr10"},{"id":"w12-t09-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which statements about the web search tool are supported by the docs?","options":["It uses Grounding with Bing Search and Bing Custom Search","The Microsoft DPA applies to data sent to it","Data transfers occur outside compliance and geographic boundaries","It returns inline citations"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"The page states the Bing grounding basis, the data-transfer boundary note and inline citations. It also says the DPA does not apply to data sent to these services.","ref":"tr11"},{"id":"w12-t09-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match the need to the best built-in tool.","pairs":[{"l":"Answer from user-uploaded documents","r":"File search"},{"l":"Compute aggregates and draw charts","r":"Code interpreter"},{"l":"Current public facts with citations","r":"Web search"},{"l":"Run logic inside your own process","r":"Function calling"}],"explain":"File search retrieves from your documents, code interpreter runs Python, web search grounds in the public web, and function calling leaves execution to your code.","ref":"r3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is web search a data-boundary decision?","back":"It uses Grounding with Bing. The Microsoft DPA does not apply to data sent there, and transfers occur outside compliance and geographic boundaries."},{"front":"How does code interpreter bill and scale?","back":"Extra charges beyond tokens. Each simultaneous conversation creates a session, active one hour by default with a 30 minute idle timeout."},{"front":"Is Logic Apps a built-in agent tool in the new service?","back":"UNVERIFIED on 2026-10-13. It is absent from the toolbox support table."}],"related":["w12-t08","w12-t10","w12-t12","w08-t17","w05-t13","w12-l4"],"refs":["r1","r3","r6","tr9","tr10","tr11"]},{"id":"w12-t10","order":10,"group":"Tools and knowledge","title":"Foundry IQ knowledge bases","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Foundry IQ is a managed, permission-aware knowledge layer. A knowledge base combines knowledge sources with retrieval settings and runs on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval, so several agents can share one governed source of grounding.","useIf":"Agents need grounded answers from scattered enterprise content with citations and per-user permissions.","avoidIf":"You only need a few ad hoc files in a chat, or one well-tuned index with fixed query logic and a tight latency budget.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A Foundry IQ **knowledge base** is a configurable, multi-source resource made of **knowledge sources** (Azure Blob Storage, SharePoint, OneLake, public web) and parameters that control retrieval. Azure AI Search provides the indexing and retrieval underneath, and many agents can share one knowledge base [[r6]]. For indexed sources it can automate chunking, embedding generation and metadata extraction, and schedule recurring indexer runs for incremental refresh [[r6]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Queries go through **agentic retrieval**: a multi-query pipeline that decomposes a complex question into subqueries, runs them in parallel, semantically reranks results and returns a unified response, optionally using an LLM for query planning [[r6]] [[tr8]]. The knowledge base sets a **retrieval reasoning effort** of minimal, low or medium for that LLM processing [[r6]]. More effort should mean more LLM work and more latency (inference, not a vendor figure)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t10-d1","title":"From question to cited answer","alt":"An agent asks a knowledge base. Agentic retrieval plans subqueries, searches sources in parallel with the caller identity, reranks, and returns extractive results with citations.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent] --> KB[Knowledge base]\n  KB --> P[Plan subqueries]\n  P --> S1[Blob]\n  P --> S2[SharePoint]\n  P --> S3[OneLake]\n  P --> S4[Public web]\n  S1 --> R[Rerank and merge]\n  S2 --> R\n  S3 --> R\n  S4 --> R\n  R --> A"},{"t":"p","md":"**Permissions.** Foundry IQ synchronizes ACLs for supported sources, honors Microsoft Purview sensitivity labels and can run queries under the caller's Entra identity so permissions are enforced at query time [[r6]]. You can use it from Agent Service, Microsoft Agent Framework or any app through the knowledge base REST API or Azure SDKs, and connect it to a Copilot Studio agent [[r6]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Some Foundry IQ features are GA and others preview, depending on the Search REST API version you call. The Foundry and Azure portals continue to provide preview-only access to all agentic retrieval features, and LLM query planning is marked preview in the Search docs [[r6]] [[tr8]]. Foundry IQ is one of three IQ workloads: **Fabric IQ** (semantic layer for analytics in OneLake and Power BI) and **Work IQ** (Microsoft 365 collaboration signals) are the siblings [[r6]]."}],"whereToUse":["Enterprise assistants over SharePoint, Blob Storage and OneLake that must respect source permissions","Several agents (prompt, hosted, Agent Framework, Copilot Studio) that need the same governed grounding","Complex questions that benefit from decomposition into subqueries","Answers that need extractive content and citations that trace back to source documents"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Content is curated, shared across agents and spread over several sources","Permissions matter and you want ACL sync, Purview labels and caller identity enforced","Questions are multi-part and one flat vector query would miss pieces"],"avoid":["A handful of user-provided files in one chat: file search is lighter","A single well-tuned index and a tight latency budget where LLM planning is wasted","Your policy needs every retrieval feature to be GA and the portal path is preview-only"],"flips":["Latency budget tightens: lower the retrieval reasoning effort or query the index directly","Corpus is small and stable: file search or long context can replace a knowledge base","Questions are single-hop lookups: skip LLM planning"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Grounding options in Foundry","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry IQ knowledge base","Multi-source, permission-aware, shared across agents","Tiny corpus or fixed-query lookups","LLM planning adds latency and cost (inference), partly preview"],["File search","User-provided documents in a chat","ACL-aware multi-source retrieval","Extra charges, limited governance [[tr9]]"],["Azure AI Search tool on your own index","You own the index design and query logic","You want managed sources and indexers","You run ingestion and ACL sync"],["Foundry memory","User-specific facts across sessions","Shared policy or curated content","Preview, LLM-consolidated per-user store [[r7]]"],["Long context","Small, stable material","Large or changing corpora","Token cost on every call"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A user sees content from a source they cannot open","cause":"The source is not supported for ACL sync, or the query ran under a shared identity","fix":"Run queries under the caller's Entra identity and confirm ACL sync support for each source [[r6]]."},{"symptom":"Answers are slow on simple questions","cause":"LLM query planning and reasoning effort are higher than the question needs","fix":"Lower the retrieval reasoning effort to minimal or low and test quality (inference on cost and latency) [[r6]]."},{"symptom":"Code that worked in the portal breaks in production","cause":"The portal exposes preview-only agentic retrieval features, while the GA surface depends on the Search REST API version","fix":"Check the migration guide for what is GA by API version before pinning [[r6]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t10-s1","title":"Many sources, per-user permissions","situation":"A company assistant must answer from SharePoint sites, a Blob container and OneLake data. Each user may see only what their groups allow, and three agents need the same grounding.","constraints":["Per-user permissions are mandatory","Three agents share the knowledge","Questions are often multi-part"],"unknowns":["Are all sources supported for ACL sync?","How fast must a revocation take effect?"],"question":"How do you ground the agents?","options":[{"name":"One Foundry IQ knowledge base with ACL sync and caller-identity queries","verdict":"best","why":"Shared across agents, permission-aware and built for multi-part questions with citations.","tradeoff":"Partly preview, LLM planning cost and latency, and revocation depends on ACL sync timing."},{"name":"Azure AI Search tool over a custom index with security filters","verdict":"good","why":"Full control of index and filters.","tradeoff":"You build ingestion, ACL sync and agentic planning yourself."},{"name":"File search per agent","verdict":"poor","why":"Per-agent vector stores duplicate content and do not enforce source permissions.","tradeoff":"Right for user-uploaded files in a single chat."}],"flips":[{"if":"Revocation must take effect within seconds","then":"Add a live source check on sensitive hits, whatever the retrieval layer."},{"if":"Policy forbids preview features","then":"Build on the GA Search surface and own more of the pipeline."}],"takeaway":"Shared, permission-aware grounding is what a knowledge base is for."},{"id":"w12-t10-s2","title":"Tight latency, one known index","situation":"A support widget answers single-hop lookups against one well-tuned search index. The latency budget is tight and questions are short.","constraints":["Strict latency budget","One index already tuned","No multi-part questions"],"unknowns":["What are p95 latency targets?","Does the index already hold ACLs?"],"question":"What retrieval do you use?","options":[{"name":"Query the existing index directly or with the Azure AI Search tool","verdict":"best","why":"No planning step, and you keep your tuned query logic.","tradeoff":"No decomposition or managed multi-source features."},{"name":"Knowledge base at minimal reasoning effort","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the managed layer with the least LLM planning.","tradeoff":"Still extra hops compared with a direct query."},{"name":"Knowledge base at medium reasoning effort","verdict":"poor","why":"Heavy planning for single-hop lookups spends latency and tokens for little gain.","tradeoff":"Right for ambiguous, multi-part questions."}],"flips":[{"if":"Questions become multi-part","then":"Move to the knowledge base with low or medium effort."},{"if":"Sources multiply","then":"A knowledge base removes custom connector work."}],"takeaway":"Do not pay for planning that the question does not need."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t10-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which values does Foundry IQ allow for retrieval reasoning effort?","options":["Low, medium, high","Minimal, low, medium","None, auto, max","Fast, balanced, deep"],"answer":1,"explain":"The knowledge base page lists minimal, low or medium. The other sets are not Foundry IQ values.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w12-t10-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which permission features does Foundry IQ document?","options":["ACL synchronization for supported sources","Honoring Purview sensitivity labels","Queries under the caller's Entra identity","Automatic removal of all prompt injection"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The page lists ACL sync, Purview labels and caller-identity queries. It makes no claim to remove prompt injection, which needs separate controls.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w12-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most favors dropping a knowledge base for a direct index query?","options":["A new SharePoint source appears","A second agent needs the same content","Questions are single-hop and latency is tight","Users complain answers lack citations"],"answer":2,"explain":"Single-hop questions under a tight latency budget do not need LLM planning. The other changes favor a shared knowledge base.","ref":"tr8"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a Foundry IQ knowledge base made of?","back":"Knowledge sources (Blob, SharePoint, OneLake, public web) plus retrieval settings, run on Azure AI Search agentic retrieval and shareable across agents."},{"front":"Is Foundry IQ GA?","back":"Partly. GA depends on the Search REST API version, while portal access to all agentic retrieval features is preview-only (checked 2026-10-13)."},{"front":"Foundry IQ vs Fabric IQ vs Work IQ?","back":"Foundry IQ: enterprise knowledge layer. Fabric IQ: semantic layer over OneLake and Power BI. Work IQ: Microsoft 365 collaboration signals."}],"related":["w12-t09","w12-t12","w05-t13","w07-t11","w07-t13","w12-l4"],"refs":["r6","r7","tr8","tr9"]},{"id":"w12-t11","order":11,"group":"Tools and knowledge","title":"Connected agents vs A2A tool","minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"The classic Connected Agents tool is not available in the new Foundry Agent Service. Use the A2A tool for peer agents, Agent Framework orchestration inside hosted code, or Workflows (preview, retiring 2026-12-01).","useIf":"One Foundry agent must delegate to another agent, in or outside Foundry, and you are migrating off Connected Agents.","avoidIf":"The peer is a plain function or API: use a tool. Use A2A only when the peer is a real agent with its own reasoning.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The Connected Agents tool from the classic Agents API is **not available** in the new Foundry Agent Service. The docs name two replacements: the **A2A tool**, which connects to any A2A-compatible endpoint including another Foundry agent exposed as an A2A endpoint, and **Workflows**, which orchestrate Foundry agents declaratively [[r21]]. Workflows are preview and retire on 2026-12-01, and classic agents retire on 2027-03-31 [[r11]]. For new multi-agent work in code, the GA page points to Microsoft Agent Framework (see the Agent Framework chapter) [[r9]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The A2A tool has two tool types. `a2a` targets A2A protocol 1.0 and is **GA**. `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and stays **preview** for existing integrations [[r21]]. When your agent calls a remote agent through the tool, the remote agent processes the request and your agent uses the response, then keeps managing the conversation [[r21]]. A2A v1.0.0 was released on 2026-03-12, with breaking changes from 0.3 such as renamed operations [[tr7]]. Store credentials in a project connection and consider adding the tool through a toolbox [[r21]]. Creating the A2A project connection needs Foundry Project Manager, and testing needs Foundry User [[r21]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t11-d1","title":"A2A tool call path","alt":"A user asks a Foundry agent. The agent calls a remote A2A agent through the a2a tool. The remote agent responds and the Foundry agent answers the user and keeps the conversation.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant F as Foundry agent\n  participant R as Remote A2A agent\n  U->>F: Question\n  F->>R: Request through the a2a tool\n  R-->>F: Response\n  F-->>U: Answer, conversation stays with F"},{"t":"table","caption":"Calling a Foundry agent as an A2A endpoint: limits on 2026-10-13","head":["Aspect","Documented behavior"],"rows":[["Version and binding","A2A v1.0 over JSON-RPC only. With no version selector Foundry serves 0.3, so send `A2A-Version: 1.0`"],["Modality and streaming","Text only, no streaming"],["Prerequisite","The agent must use the Responses protocol"],["Authentication","Entra only, caller needs the Foundry Agent Consumer role or higher"],["Retention","Tasks and contexts are kept 60 days from last write"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Agent card path","md":"For agents registered in Foundry Control Plane the default agent card path is `/.well-known/agent-card.json` [[r23]]."}],"whereToUse":["A Foundry agent delegating a subtask to another team's agent in a separate runtime","Exposing a Foundry agent as an A2A endpoint so external agents can call it","Replacing Connected Agents calls when moving classic agents to the new service","Cross-vendor delegation where the peer's internals must stay opaque"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The peer is a separately owned agent that you cannot or should not embed","You want a standard protocol rather than a Foundry-only mechanism","You need the peer to keep its own state and reasoning"],"avoid":["All agents live in one codebase and one team: orchestrate in-process with Agent Framework instead of network hops","New designs built on Workflows, which are preview and retire on 2026-12-01","The peer is a stateless function: expose it as an OpenAPI or MCP tool"],"flips":["Peer needs streaming or non-text modalities: check whether the incoming A2A limits block you","Preview is not allowed: use `a2a` (GA, v1.0) and avoid `a2a_preview`","The pattern must be declarative and short-lived: Workflows work today but plan the retirement"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Agent-to-agent options in Foundry","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["A2A tool (`a2a`, GA)","Peer is a separate agent or another vendor","Everything is in one process","Network hop, version negotiation, auth setup [[r21]]"],["Agent Framework orchestration in hosted code","One team, code-defined control flow","Peers are external or opaque","You write and test the orchestration"],["Foundry Workflows (preview)","Declarative sequential or group-chat flows","Anything long-lived","Retire 2026-12-01 [[r11]]"],["Connected Agents (classic)","Nothing new: it exists only in classic agents","Always, in the new service","Classic agents retire 2027-03-31 [[r11]]"],["MCP server that wraps an agent","Peer should look like a tool","You need task states and opaque reasoning","Loses A2A task semantics"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Remote Foundry agent answers in an older protocol shape","cause":"With no version selector Foundry serves A2A 0.3","fix":"Send the `A2A-Version: 1.0` header [[tr12]]."},{"symptom":"Caller gets 403 on a Foundry A2A endpoint","cause":"The calling identity lacks the Foundry Agent Consumer role on the target project","fix":"Grant Foundry Agent Consumer or higher and use Entra auth [[r21]]."},{"symptom":"A new design depends on Workflows and the team plans a rewrite","cause":"Workflows (preview) retire on 2026-12-01","fix":"Pick Agent Framework code orchestration or A2A for new work [[r11]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t11-s1","title":"A peer team owns the specialist agent","situation":"A Foundry agent needs a tax-rules specialist that another team runs in its own container and exposes over A2A. The specialist's internals are private.","constraints":["Separate ownership and runtime","Peer already exposes A2A","GA features preferred"],"unknowns":["Which A2A version does the peer serve?","How does the peer authenticate callers?"],"question":"How do you connect them?","options":[{"name":"A2A tool with the `a2a` (GA, v1.0) type and a project connection","verdict":"best","why":"Standard protocol, credentials in a connection, peer stays opaque.","tradeoff":"You depend on the peer's version support and uptime."},{"name":"Wrap the peer as an MCP tool","verdict":"good","why":"Easy to consume as a tool.","tradeoff":"You lose A2A task semantics and an opaque agent looks like a function."},{"name":"Copy the specialist's logic into your agent","verdict":"poor","why":"Duplicates ownership and goes stale.","tradeoff":"Right only when the peer is retired and you inherit it."}],"flips":[{"if":"The peer only serves A2A 0.3","then":"Use `a2a_preview` (preview) until it upgrades."},{"if":"The peer is a plain REST API","then":"OpenAPI tool instead."}],"takeaway":"A2A fits separately owned agents."},{"id":"w12-t11-s2","title":"Three agents, one team, one release","situation":"One team owns three Foundry agents that run in a fixed order with shared state and a single release train. They used Connected Agents in the classic service.","constraints":["Same codebase and team","Fixed control flow","Long-lived, must outlive 2026-12-01"],"unknowns":["Do any steps need to run in parallel?","Where does shared state live?"],"question":"What replaces Connected Agents?","options":[{"name":"Agent Framework orchestration inside one hosted agent","verdict":"best","why":"Code-defined control flow, no network hops, no retiring dependency.","tradeoff":"You write and test the orchestration, and the hosted GA label is unconfirmed."},{"name":"A2A tool between the three agents","verdict":"good","why":"Works and gives separation.","tradeoff":"Extra hops, auth and version handling for agents the team already owns."},{"name":"Foundry Workflows (preview)","verdict":"poor","why":"Retires on 2026-12-01 and the design must outlive it.","tradeoff":"Fine for short-lived experiments before the retirement."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agents will be split among teams","then":"A2A between them."},{"if":"The flow is a short pilot","then":"Workflows are acceptable until the retirement."}],"takeaway":"Do not build long-lived designs on a preview feature with a retirement date."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which tool type targets A2A protocol 1.0 and is GA in Foundry?","options":["`a2a_preview`","`a2a`","`connected_agent`","`workflow`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`a2a` is GA and targets 1.0. `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and is preview. Connected Agents is not in the new service.","ref":"r21"},{"id":"w12-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are documented limits of calling a Foundry agent as an A2A endpoint?","options":["Text modality only","No streaming","Requires the Responses protocol","Supports gRPC and HTTP+JSON bindings"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The incoming A2A endpoint is v1.0 over JSON-RPC only, text only, without streaming, and needs the Responses protocol. gRPC and HTTP+JSON are not offered.","ref":"tr12"},{"id":"w12-t11-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A design must stay in service for years. Which option is a poor base because of a published retirement date?","options":["Agent Framework orchestration in hosted code","A2A tool with `a2a`","Foundry Workflows (preview)","Direct tool calls to an OpenAPI service"],"answer":2,"explain":"Workflows (preview) retire on 2026-12-01. The other options have no announced retirement in the sources checked on 2026-10-13.","ref":"r11"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What replaces Connected Agents in the new Foundry Agent Service?","back":"The A2A tool (any A2A endpoint, including a Foundry agent exposed as one), Foundry Workflows (preview, retire 2026-12-01), or Agent Framework orchestration in your own code."},{"front":"Which header makes a Foundry agent serve A2A 1.0?","back":"`A2A-Version: 1.0`. With no version selector Foundry serves 0.3."}],"related":["w09-t02","w09-t10","w11-t23","w12-t08","w12-t19","w12-l4"],"refs":["r9","r11","r21","r23","tr7","tr12"]},{"id":"w12-t12","order":12,"group":"Tools and knowledge","title":"Memory in Foundry","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Foundry memory (preview) is managed long-term memory: an LLM extracts facts from conversations, consolidates them into a memory store and retrieves them across sessions, scoped per user.","useIf":"An agent should remember user preferences, past issues or learned routines across sessions.","avoidIf":"The content is shared policy or documents, the project needs VNet integration for memory stores, or preview is not allowed.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry memory is designed for **long-term** memory: knowledge retained across sessions. Short-term memory is the current session's conversation and is handled by the orchestration framework [[r7]]. Memories live as items in a managed **memory store**. The system works in three phases: **extraction** (pull preferences, facts and context from a conversation), **consolidation** (an LLM merges duplicates and resolves conflicts, such as a new allergy), and **retrieval** (search the store for relevant memories) [[r7]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t12-d1","title":"Memory phases","alt":"Conversation text is extracted into candidate memories, consolidated by an LLM into the store, and retrieved into later sessions.","src":"flowchart LR\n  C[Conversation] --> E[Extraction]\n  E --> K[Consolidation by LLM]\n  K --> S[(Memory store)]\n  S --> R[Retrieval in later sessions]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Memory types, all enabled by default (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Type","Holds","Retrieve"],"rows":[["User profile","Durable preferences and personal context","Early in each conversation"],["Chat summary","Distilled summaries of prior threads","Per turn from current messages"],["Procedural","Reusable how-to routines inferred from past work","When a recurring workflow is requested"]]},{"t":"p","md":"You use memory in two ways: attach the **memory search tool** to a prompt agent so it reads and writes during conversations, or call the **memory store APIs** for direct control over items, retention and lifecycle [[r7]]. Controls include item-level create, read, update, list and delete, a store-level default TTL set at creation, and direct remember-or-forget commands [[r7]]. Low-level APIs need an explicit `scope` on every request, and automatic scope resolution from the caller's identity works only with the memory search tool and `scope` set to `{{$userId}}` [[r7]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Scopes per store","value":"100","note":"Maximum [[r7]]"},{"label":"Memories per scope","value":"10,000","note":"Maximum [[r7]]"},{"label":"Search and update rate","value":"1,000 per min","note":"Each of the two operations [[r7]]"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Memory and the Memory Store API are preview, with no SLA. Memory needs compatible Azure OpenAI chat and embedding deployments. **VNet integration is not supported for memory stores.** Because an LLM extracts and consolidates memories, prompt injection and memory corruption are risks: use Content Safety prompt injection detection and adversarial testing [[r7]]."}],"whereToUse":["Support or shopping agents that should recall a user's preferences and past issues","Planning agents that reuse stable constraints such as seat or dietary preferences","Agents that learn recurring workflows (procedural memory) for a given user","Any agent where a user may ask to remember or forget something explicitly"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Facts are user-specific, learned over time and should persist across sessions","You want extraction and conflict resolution done for you","You need item-level delete and TTL for privacy and retention"],"avoid":["Shared policy or curated documents: use a Foundry IQ knowledge base. Memory is per-user and LLM-consolidated (inference)","User-provided documents in a chat: use file search","Networks that require VNet integration for the store, or policies that forbid preview"],"flips":["Memory must be auditable and deterministic: store structured facts yourself and keep LLM extraction out","Volume exceeds 10,000 memories per scope: shard scopes or prune with TTL","A regulator needs proof of deletion: use item-level delete and log the deletion"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where remembered information can live","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry memory (preview)","Per-user facts across sessions, managed extraction","Shared content, VNet-bound stores, GA-only policy","Preview, LLM consolidation can store wrong facts [[r7]]"],["Foundry IQ knowledge base","Curated organization content","Personal context","Not personal by design [[r6]]"],["File search","Documents the user provides","Learned preferences","Extra charges [[tr9]]"],["Your own database with structured profile","Deterministic, auditable facts","You want free-form extraction","You build extraction, retention, deletion"],["Resending chat history","Short threads","Long histories and many sessions","Tokens grow each turn"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"One user's memories surface for another user","cause":"Low-level API calls used the wrong or a shared `scope`","fix":"Set `scope` explicitly per request, or use the memory search tool with `scope` set to `{{$userId}}` [[r7]]."},{"symptom":"The agent confidently repeats a wrong 'fact' about a user","cause":"A bad statement or injected text was extracted and consolidated","fix":"Filter inputs with prompt injection detection, review and delete items, and test adversarially [[r7]]."},{"symptom":"TTL or procedural memory settings cannot be changed later","cause":"Some defaults, such as procedural memory and default TTL, are configured at store creation","fix":"Plan them at creation and verify post-create updates in your API version [[r7]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t12-s1","title":"Remember each customer across visits","situation":"A customer assistant should recall a user's preferences and the last unresolved issue the next time they return, and forget on request.","constraints":["Per-user facts","Forget-me requests expected","Preview is acceptable with sign-off"],"unknowns":["Is the project VNet-isolated?","How long may memories be kept?"],"question":"How do you provide continuity?","options":[{"name":"Memory store with the memory search tool, `scope` set to `{{$userId}}` and a default TTL","verdict":"best","why":"Managed extraction and consolidation, per-user scope and item-level delete for forget requests.","tradeoff":"Preview, and LLM-extracted facts can be wrong."},{"name":"Resend full chat history each session","verdict":"good","why":"No new store.","tradeoff":"Token cost grows and history from old sessions is hard to prune."},{"name":"Put shared policy documents into memory","verdict":"poor","why":"Memory is per-user and LLM-consolidated, so curated content gets rewritten and scoped wrongly.","tradeoff":"Use a knowledge base for shared content."}],"flips":[{"if":"Auditors need deterministic facts","then":"Own structured profile table with explicit writes."},{"if":"Preview is forbidden","then":"Own store until memory reaches GA."}],"takeaway":"Memory is for user-specific facts, not shared knowledge."},{"id":"w12-t12-s2","title":"Isolated network project","situation":"A project runs behind private networking with VNet integration required for every data store. The agent needs per-user preference recall.","constraints":["VNet integration is mandatory","Per-user preferences","Moderate volume"],"unknowns":["Is a private database already approved?","What retention applies?"],"question":"What do you do for memory?","options":[{"name":"Build a small preference store in a VNet-integrated database and retrieve it in code","verdict":"best","why":"Memory stores do not support VNet integration, so the requirement rules the managed store out.","tradeoff":"You write extraction, retention and deletion logic."},{"name":"Use Foundry memory anyway","verdict":"poor","why":"It breaks the VNet integration requirement.","tradeoff":"Right when the network rule does not apply."},{"name":"Resend recent chat history","verdict":"good","why":"Works for short horizons with no store.","tradeoff":"No long-term recall and rising token cost."}],"flips":[{"if":"Docs later list VNet support for memory stores","then":"Re-evaluate the managed store."},{"if":"Only the agent needs private networking, not the memory store","then":"Foundry memory is possible."}],"takeaway":"Check the limitations list against your network rules before adopting a preview feature."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t12-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A memory store allows at most 100 scopes and 10,000 memories per scope. What is the maximum number of memories one store can hold?","answer":1000000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"memories","hint":"100 times 10,000","explain":"100 x 10,000 = 1,000,000. This is derived from the documented quotas, which apply to the preview.","ref":"r7"},{"id":"w12-t12-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"When does Foundry resolve `scope` automatically from the caller's identity?","options":["With every memory API call","Only with hosted agents","Never","Only with the memory search tool and `scope` set to `{{$userId}}`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Low-level APIs need an explicit scope in each request. Automatic resolution is supported only for the memory search tool with `{{$userId}}`.","ref":"r7"},{"id":"w12-t12-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which content belongs in a Foundry IQ knowledge base rather than in memory?","options":["A user's dietary preference","A summary of last week's chat","The company's curated policy documents","A recurring personal workflow"],"answer":2,"explain":"Memory is for user-specific facts learned over time. Curated organization content belongs in a knowledge base. The other items match the profile, chat summary and procedural memory types.","ref":"r7"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three phases and three types of Foundry memory?","back":"Phases: extraction, consolidation, retrieval. Types: user profile, chat summary, procedural. All three types are on by default. Memory is preview."},{"front":"Hard limits for memory stores (preview)?","back":"100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, 1,000 search and 1,000 update requests per minute. No VNet integration for memory stores."}],"related":["w12-t10","w10-t19","w10-t20","w11-t12","w12-l4"],"refs":["r6","r7","tr9"]},{"id":"w12-t13","order":13,"group":"Publish and operate","title":"Publishing: Teams, Microsoft 365 Copilot and the Entra Agent Registry","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Publishing exposes an agent's stable endpoint to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot through an Azure Bot Service resource. Scope decides who can see and call it, and you roll new versions behind the same endpoint.","useIf":"People should reach a Foundry agent from the tools where they already work, with an approval path and version control behind it.","avoidIf":"The agent is called only from your own application (no publish needed) or the data flows into Microsoft 365 and Teams are not approved.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"When you publish to Microsoft Copilot and Teams, you publish the agent's **stable endpoint**, so users always meet one consistent agent while you roll out new versions behind it [[r5]]. You pick the **active version**: always use latest, or a specific version [[r5]]. Foundry validates your metadata (name, three-part publish version, descriptions), compiles a Teams app manifest `.zip`, submits it to the Copilot and Teams catalogs, enables the `activity` protocol and sets an authorization scheme based on scope [[r5]]. Publishing creates an **Azure Bot Service** resource, which needs the `Microsoft.BotService` resource provider [[r5]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t13-d1","title":"Publish path and scope","alt":"A tested version is served by a stable endpoint. Publishing creates an Azure Bot Service resource. Individual scope is visible only to you with no approval. Organization scope needs admin approval and then appears under Built by your org.","src":"flowchart LR\n  V[Tested version] --> E[Stable endpoint]\n  E --> B[Azure Bot Service resource]\n  B --> S{Publish scope}\n  S -->|Individual| I[Visible to you, no approval]\n  S -->|Organization| A[Admin approval]\n  A --> O[Built by your org]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Scopes (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Scope","REST value and scheme","Who sees it","Approval"],"rows":[["Individual (portal: you only)","`publishScope` `Shared`, `BotServiceRbac`","Only you. In a Teams chat, participants with the needed Foundry permissions on the project can use it","None [[r5]]"],["Organization (portal: People in your organization)","`publishScope` `Tenant`, `BotServiceTenant`","Everyone in the tenant, under Built by your org","Admin approval in the Microsoft 365 admin center [[r5]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"You need permission to create the bot and configure its channels (`Microsoft.BotService/botServices/write` and `channels/write`). The **Azure Bot Service Contributor** role grants exactly these, Contributor or Owner also work, and Foundry roles do not grant them. You also need **Foundry User** on the project [[r5]]. For projects with public network access disabled, the portal cannot publish: use the REST API and enable the source-IP-filtered public Activity route with `enable_m365_public_endpoint` [[r5]]. The overview also lists the **Entra Agent Registry** as a place to share agents [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Data and identity caveats","md":"Microsoft 365 and Teams process and store data tied to publishing and to agent responses, under their own terms and residency commitments. Review before you publish [[r5]]. In the older agent application model, publishing creates a distinct identity and you must reassign RBAC, while the newer hosted model gives each agent its own identity at deploy. The docs say the publishing experience is changing, so confirm which model your project is on [[r4]]. Voice-based agents do not show the standard Teams card [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["Pilots shared with a small group in Teams chats","Organization-wide assistants listed under Built by your org after approval","Teams where the agent must live in Microsoft 365 Copilot and Teams rather than a custom web UI","Rolling new agent versions behind a stable address without telling users"],"whenToUse":{"use":["People already work in Teams and Microsoft 365 and should not need a new app","You need an admin approval step and a catalog listing for org-wide reach","You want a stable endpoint that decouples users from version changes"],"avoid":["A data residency or compliance review has not covered the Microsoft 365 and Teams data flows","The only client is your own application: programmatic access needs no publishing","A voice agent: the standard Teams card is not shown for it"],"flips":["Project has public network access disabled: publish by REST with `enable_m365_public_endpoint`","Reach grows from a pilot to the tenant: change scope to Organization and go through approval","You need controlled rollouts: pin a specific active version instead of always using latest"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to put an agent in front of users","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Publish, Individual scope","Pilot or personal use, no approval","Broad discovery is needed","Visible only to you in the stores [[r5]]"],["Publish, Organization scope","Tenant-wide reach with approval","You need a quick pilot","Admin approval and wider data exposure"],["Call the endpoint from your own app","Custom UI, existing product","Users live in Teams","You build and secure the client; publishing is not needed [[r2]]"],["Copilot Studio agent","Makers and Microsoft 365 channels","Code-level control","Different authoring and governance surface [[tr4]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Publish fails with an authorization error although you are Foundry Owner","cause":"Foundry roles do not grant Azure Bot Service write permissions","fix":"Assign Azure Bot Service Contributor (or Contributor or Owner) in the resource group [[r5]]."},{"symptom":"Publish fails because the bot resource cannot be created","cause":"The `Microsoft.BotService` provider is not registered in the subscription","fix":"Register it, for example with `az provider register --namespace Microsoft.BotService` [[r5]]."},{"symptom":"Users see a half-tested version","cause":"Active version is set to always use latest","fix":"Pin a specific tested version and promote deliberately [[r5]]."},{"symptom":"Agent works after publish but RBAC to downstream resources is gone","cause":"In the older agent application model publishing creates a distinct identity","fix":"Reassign RBAC to the new identity and confirm your project's model [[r4]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t13-s1","title":"Pilot with a handful of colleagues","situation":"A team wants five colleagues to try an assistant in a Teams chat this week, before anyone signs off on wider release.","constraints":["Five users","No admin approval yet","All five have Foundry permissions on the project"],"unknowns":["Is the data flow into Teams already reviewed?","Is the project public network access enabled?"],"question":"Which scope do you publish with?","options":[{"name":"Individual scope and add the agent to a Teams chat","verdict":"best","why":"No admin approval, and chat participants with the needed Foundry permissions can use it.","tradeoff":"Not discoverable by others, and participants need project permissions."},{"name":"Organization scope","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs admin approval and exposes an untested agent to the whole tenant.","tradeoff":"Right once the pilot passes and an admin approves the listing."},{"name":"Skip publishing and share the Foundry endpoint","verdict":"good","why":"Programmatic access needs no publish.","tradeoff":"Colleagues need a client, so Teams is not used."}],"flips":[{"if":"Pilot users lack Foundry permissions","then":"Grant them or move to Organization scope after approval."},{"if":"The pilot grows to the whole company","then":"Organization scope with admin approval."}],"takeaway":"Start narrow: scope controls both visibility and the call authorization scheme."},{"id":"w12-t13-s2","title":"Org-wide release in a locked-down project","situation":"An approved assistant must be listed for all employees. The project has public network access disabled, and the security team asked for a version pin.","constraints":["Public network access is off","Tenant-wide listing","Release is gated by a pinned version"],"unknowns":["Has the Microsoft 365 data flow been approved?","Who approves in the admin center?"],"question":"How do you publish?","options":[{"name":"REST publish with Organization scope, `enable_m365_public_endpoint` and a pinned active version","verdict":"best","why":"The portal cannot publish from this project, Tenant scope gives org-wide listing after approval, and the pin keeps the release controlled.","tradeoff":"You allow a source-IP-filtered public Activity route and manage the REST call yourself."},{"name":"Portal publish","verdict":"poor","why":"Not supported for projects that disable public network access.","tradeoff":"Right for projects with public access enabled."},{"name":"Individual scope per employee","verdict":"good","why":"Avoids admin approval.","tradeoff":"No tenant listing and per-user setup that does not scale."}],"flips":[{"if":"Security refuses any public route","then":"Do not publish to Teams from this project. Use a private client."},{"if":"Latest is trusted","then":"Always use latest."}],"takeaway":"Network posture decides the publish path, and scope decides the audience."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t13-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which publish scope enables `BotServiceTenant` and needs admin approval in the Microsoft 365 admin center?","options":["Individual (`Shared`)","Organization (`Tenant`)","Project","Preview"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tenant scope enables `BotServiceTenant` and requires admin approval, after which the agent appears under Built by your org. Shared scope uses `BotServiceRbac` and needs no approval.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w12-t13-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Publishing fails. Which are documented prerequisites you should check?","options":["Azure Bot Service Contributor, Contributor or Owner in the resource group","Foundry User on the project","The `Microsoft.BotService` resource provider is registered","Foundry Account Owner alone is sufficient"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Bot write permissions come from Azure roles, not Foundry roles, so Foundry Account Owner alone does not suffice. Foundry User and the provider registration are also required.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w12-t13-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A project disables public network access. How must you publish to Teams?","options":["Use the portal as usual","Switch the agent to a voice agent","Publish is impossible","Use the REST API with `enable_m365_public_endpoint`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Portal publishing is unsupported for such projects. The REST route with the source-IP-filtered public Activity endpoint is the documented path. Voice agents do not show the standard Teams card.","ref":"r5"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does Foundry publish to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot?","back":"The agent's stable endpoint, through an Azure Bot Service resource. Versions roll behind the endpoint and you choose the active version."},{"front":"Individual vs Organization scope?","back":"Individual: `Shared`, `BotServiceRbac`, no approval, visible to you. Organization: `Tenant`, `BotServiceTenant`, admin approval, listed under Built by your org."}],"related":["w12-t04","w12-t06","w12-t18","w12-t19","w12-l3"],"refs":["r1","r2","r4","r5","tr4"]},{"id":"w12-t14","order":14,"group":"Publish and operate","title":"Tracing, evaluation and the agent optimizer","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Foundry traces every agent run into Application Insights using OpenTelemetry conventions. The agent optimizer (limited preview) closes the loop by evaluating an agent against a dataset and proposing better instructions, tool descriptions, skills or models.","useIf":"You need to see why an agent behaved as it did, and turn that evidence into measured improvements.","avoidIf":"You need only a quick prototype with no quality bar, or your policy forbids limited-preview features in the improvement loop.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Tracing.** Foundry captures inputs, outputs, tool usage, retries, latencies and cost for each agent run, using OpenTelemetry semantic conventions, and stores the telemetry in Azure Monitor Application Insights [[tr3]]. Tracing is **GA for prompt and hosted agents**, and preview for workflow and external agents [[tr3]]. To view traces you need a tracing-enabled project, access to the connected Application Insights resource and the Log Analytics reader role [[tr3]]. Telemetry storage can incur costs by volume and retention [[tr3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**The optimizer.** It automatically improves prompt and hosted agents by evaluating behavior and generating candidates. It scores the baseline against a dataset, generates candidates (rewritten instructions, skills, tool descriptions, model choices), evaluates them on the same dataset, ranks by a composite score from 0.0 to 1.0 and marks the best [[r25]]. Prompt agents start from an Optimize wizard in the portal and promote the winner to a new version. Hosted agents need optimizer-ready code (`load_config()`), then you apply the candidate locally and redeploy [[r25]]. Datasets can be generated from traces [[r25]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t14-d1","title":"From traces to a better agent","alt":"Traces become an evaluation dataset. The optimizer scores the baseline, generates and scores candidates, ranks them, and the winner is promoted as a new prompt-agent version or applied and redeployed for a hosted agent.","src":"flowchart LR\n  T[Traces in App Insights] --> D[Dataset]\n  D --> B[Score baseline]\n  B --> C[Generate candidates]\n  C --> E[Score candidates]\n  E --> R[Rank by 0.0 to 1.0 score]\n  R --> P[Promote or redeploy]"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Optimizer runs execute your tools","md":"The optimizer invokes the agent against every task in the dataset, so external calls (APIs, databases, third-party services) run on each evaluation. Use test endpoints or mocks to avoid charges, state changes or rate limits [[r25]]. Function-calling tools run on the client side, so the optimizer can improve their descriptions but cannot evaluate their execution [[r25]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"The agent optimizer is **limited preview** [[r25]]. Voice-based agents have monitoring that needs Application Insights, a single trace-oriented view, full-conversation evaluation only and no dataset-from-traces action [[r1]]. The Agent 365 Observability SDK is deprecated for new work. Use the **Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro** (.NET, Node.js, Python), which also targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend [[r26]]. Evaluator catalogs and metric design are covered in the evaluation chapter."}],"whereToUse":["Debugging a wrong answer by walking the trace to the step that introduced the error","Building regression datasets from real traces","Tuning instructions, tool descriptions or model choice against a measured score","Reviewing latency, retries and token use across tool-heavy agents"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Always turn tracing on for prompt and hosted agents in anything beyond a prototype","Use the optimizer when you have representative tasks and clear evaluators","Use traces to build datasets so tests reflect real use"],"avoid":["Running the optimizer against live tools that charge money or mutate state","Treating the optimizer score as ground truth when the dataset or evaluators are weak","Capturing sensitive content in traces without a retention and access policy"],"flips":["Policy forbids limited-preview features: tune manually with your own evaluation harness","The agent is not a Foundry agent: use the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro to send traces to Foundry or another backend","Voice agent: evaluate full conversations, since single-turn and simulated evaluation are unavailable"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to improve and observe agents","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry tracing in Application Insights","Prompt or hosted agents on Foundry","You need a vendor-neutral backend","Telemetry costs; Log Analytics reader role required [[tr3]]"],["Agent optimizer","Measured prompt, tool-description and model tuning","Tools have side effects, weak datasets","Limited preview; candidate runs spend model tokens [[r25]]"],["Manual prompt iteration","Small changes, no dataset yet","Many scenarios and regressions","Slow, subjective"],["OpenTelemetry Distro to any backend","Mixed estates, Agent 365 or Azure Monitor","You want Foundry-only portal views","You manage exporters and retention [[r26]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Traces are missing in the Foundry portal","cause":"Tracing is not enabled for the project, or you lack access to Application Insights or the Log Analytics reader role","fix":"Enable tracing, connect Application Insights and grant the reader role [[tr3]]."},{"symptom":"Optimizer run creates real orders or sends emails","cause":"Tools call live endpoints for every dataset task","fix":"Point tools at test endpoints or mocks before running [[r25]]."},{"symptom":"Optimizer reports no gain for tool failures","cause":"Function-calling tools run client side, so execution is not evaluated, only descriptions","fix":"Test tool execution in your own harness and use the optimizer for descriptions [[r25]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t14-s1","title":"Prompt agent that calls a payment tool","situation":"A prompt agent mis-selects tools. The team has a month of traces and wants a measured improvement. One tool issues refunds.","constraints":["Limited preview is acceptable","A tool changes real state","Representative traces exist"],"unknowns":["Is there a test endpoint for the refund tool?","Which evaluators match the failure?"],"question":"How do you run the optimizer?","options":[{"name":"Build a dataset from traces, point tools at a test endpoint or mock, then optimize","verdict":"best","why":"Uses real tasks and avoids live refunds, since every dataset task executes the tools.","tradeoff":"You maintain a mock and must keep it faithful to production behavior."},{"name":"Iterate prompts by hand","verdict":"good","why":"No preview dependency and no tool side effects.","tradeoff":"Slow and hard to prove gains across many scenarios."},{"name":"Run the optimizer against the live refund endpoint","verdict":"poor","why":"Each evaluation executes real refunds.","tradeoff":"Fine for read-only tools."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools are read-only","then":"Live endpoints are acceptable if rate limits allow."},{"if":"Policy forbids limited preview","then":"Manual iteration with your own evaluation harness."}],"takeaway":"Optimizer runs are test runs that execute tools, so isolate side effects first."},{"id":"w12-t14-s2","title":"Observability code on a deprecated SDK","situation":"A team instrumented an agent with the Agent 365 Observability SDK a year ago and now needs telemetry in Azure Monitor and Foundry.","constraints":["Mixed runtime estate","New work must not use deprecated packages","Backends include Azure Monitor"],"unknowns":["Which languages are used?","Do we need an OTLP backend as well?"],"question":"What do you instrument with?","options":[{"name":"Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro","verdict":"best","why":"It replaces the deprecated SDK for new work and targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend.","tradeoff":"A migration of existing instrumentation, and language coverage is .NET, Node.js and Python."},{"name":"Keep the Agent 365 Observability SDK","verdict":"poor","why":"It is deprecated for new work.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only as a short bridge during migration."},{"name":"Hand-roll OpenTelemetry spans","verdict":"good","why":"Vendor-neutral and under your control.","tradeoff":"You maintain semantic conventions and exporters yourself."}],"flips":[{"if":"Your language is not covered by the Distro","then":"Use plain OpenTelemetry for that service."},{"if":"Only Foundry-hosted agents are involved","then":"Foundry tracing may be sufficient."}],"takeaway":"Instrument once with OpenTelemetry and route to the backends you need."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t14-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"How does the agent optimizer rank candidates?","options":["By a composite score between 0.0 and 1.0, marking the best with a star","By token count only","By user votes in Teams","By latency alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The optimizer ranks results by composite score from 0.0 to 1.0 and marks the best candidate. It is not based only on token count, votes or latency.","ref":"r25"},{"id":"w12-t14-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about tracing in Foundry are supported on 2026-10-13?","options":["It is GA for prompt and hosted agents","It stores telemetry in Application Insights","It is GA for external agents","Viewing needs the Log Analytics reader role"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Workflow and external agents are preview. GA applies to prompt and hosted agents, telemetry goes to Application Insights and you need the Log Analytics reader role to view it.","ref":"tr3"},{"id":"w12-t14-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"An optimizer run on a hosted agent triggered real emails. Why?","options":["The optimizer was GA and bypassed guardrails","Hosted agents cannot be sandboxed","Traces replayed old emails","The optimizer invokes the agent on every dataset task, so live tools execute"],"answer":3,"explain":"The optimizer evaluates by invoking the agent per task, which runs external tool calls. Use test endpoints or mocks. It is limited preview, not GA, and traces are not replayed.","ref":"r25"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the Foundry agent optimizer change for prompt vs hosted agents?","back":"Prompt: instructions, function-calling tool descriptions, model selection. Hosted: also skills. Limited preview. Prompt candidates promote to a new version. Hosted candidates are applied locally and redeployed."},{"front":"Which package replaces the deprecated Agent 365 Observability SDK?","back":"The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for .NET, Node.js and Python. It targets Foundry, Azure Monitor and any OTLP backend."}],"related":["w14-t08","w14-t11","w15-t08","w11-t26","w12-t17","w12-l6"],"refs":["r1","r25","r26","tr3"]},{"id":"w12-t15","order":15,"group":"Identity and governance","title":"Entra Agent ID: blueprints, identities and agent users","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Entra Agent ID models an agent as a blueprint that holds credentials, agent identities that carry no credentials, and an optional agent user account. Sponsors and owners give each agent a named accountable human.","useIf":"You need to understand what an agent is in Entra, who owns it, and how credentials and lifecycle are controlled.","avoidIf":"You only call a model from an app and do not yet need a distinct identity for the agent.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Entra Agent ID has four objects: an **agent identity blueprint**, a **blueprint principal**, an **agent identity** and an optional **agent user** [[r17]]. The **agent identity** is a service principal with `servicePrincipalType` `ServiceIdentity` and `@odata.type` `#microsoft.graph.agentIdentity`, and it has **no credentials** [[r17]]. The **blueprint** holds the credentials (federated identity credentials, certificates or keys, or client secrets) and requests tokens for the identities it creates. It is a credential boundary and can federate to an Azure managed identity [[r17]]. Agent identities are single tenant, while blueprints can be multitenant [[r17]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t15-d1","title":"Object model","alt":"A blueprint holds credentials and has a blueprint principal. It creates agent identities. Each agent identity may have one optional agent user account.","src":"flowchart TB\n  BP[Blueprint: holds credentials] --> BPP[Blueprint principal]\n  BP --> AI1[Agent identity A]\n  BP --> AI2[Agent identity B]\n  AI1 --> AU1[Agent user, optional]"},{"t":"p","md":"The **agent user** is an optional second account: a user object marked as an AI agent, 1:1 with its agent identity. It can hold licenses, a mailbox and OneDrive and be reached through Teams, Outlook and Word comments. It is available only to tenants in the **Frontier preview program** [[r17]]. Foundry provisions a blueprint and identity automatically; in the agent application model unpublished agents share a project identity and publishing creates a dedicated one [[r27]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Accountability roles on agents (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Role","Required?","Can do","Cannot do"],"rows":[["Sponsor","At least one on every blueprint and agent identity","Decide lifecycle, disable or soft-delete","Edit auth settings, re-enable"],["Owner","Optional","Manage credentials, re-enable, hard-delete","Be a group (users or service principals only)"],["Manager","Optional","Request access packages for agents","Not stated as lifecycle authority"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Blast radius and governance","md":"Because the blueprint holds the credentials for every identity it creates, group only identities that can share credentials and a baseline of permissions [[r17]]. Agents have **no MFA**, so Conditional Access (evaluated when a token is issued or refreshed, plus Continuous Access Evaluation for some resources) is the control. It needs Microsoft 365 E7 or an Agent 365 license with at least Entra P1 or Microsoft 365 E3. Entra Agent ID itself is available to all Entra customers [[r15]]. Role-assignable groups and assigned security groups cannot be sponsors [[r18]]."}],"whereToUse":["Giving each agent a distinct identity so access, logs and incidents trace to one agent","Setting sponsors and owners so every agent has a named human accountable for it","Applying Conditional Access and ID Governance access packages to agents instead of shared secrets","Deciding whether an agent needs a user account, for mailbox, Teams presence or licenses"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use a separate agent identity per agent instance so permissions and audit stay scoped","Use sponsors for business accountability and owners for technical control","Add an agent user only when the agent must act as a team member in Microsoft 365"],"avoid":["Do not put identities with different sensitivity under one blueprint, since credentials are shared at the blueprint","Do not use groups as sponsors that Entra disallows, or as owners","Do not plan on agent users in tenants outside Frontier preview"],"flips":["Agent needs a mailbox or Teams presence: add an agent user (Frontier preview)","Different permission tiers: split blueprints by tier","Credentials must not be stored: federate the blueprint to an Azure managed identity"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Identity options for an agent workload","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Agent identity (via blueprint)","Per-agent scoping, Conditional Access, governance","You need a user mailbox or licenses","Blueprint credentials are a shared boundary [[r17]]"],["Agent identity plus agent user","Agent acts as a coworker in Microsoft 365","Tenant is not in Frontier preview","Preview, 1:1 user per identity [[r17]]"],["Shared app registration and secret","Quick prototype","Any production agent","No per-agent audit, hard to revoke one agent"],["Azure managed identity directly","Plain Azure resource access from compute","Entra agent governance and sponsors are required","No sponsor model, different governance surface"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Auditors cannot tell which agent called a service","cause":"Several agents run under one shared app registration or the project identity","fix":"Give each agent its own agent identity and log by identity [[r27]]."},{"symptom":"A disabled agent cannot be turned back on by its business owner","cause":"Sponsors can disable or soft-delete but cannot re-enable or edit auth settings","fix":"Assign an owner (user or service principal) who can re-enable [[r18]]."},{"symptom":"Creating a sponsor with a group fails","cause":"Role-assignable groups and assigned security groups cannot be sponsors","fix":"Use users, or an allowed group type [[r18]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t15-s1","title":"One sensitive agent among many routine ones","situation":"A platform team will register forty agents. Thirty-nine are low risk. One can read confidential records.","constraints":["Blueprints hold the credentials of all their identities","Audit must show per-agent actions","One team administers all forty"],"unknowns":["Do the routine agents share a baseline of permissions?","Who rotates the blueprint credentials?"],"question":"How do you structure blueprints?","options":[{"name":"A blueprint for the routine group and a separate blueprint for the sensitive agent","verdict":"best","why":"A compromise of one blueprint's credentials then does not expose the sensitive agent, and routine agents still share a baseline.","tradeoff":"Two credential sets to rotate and monitor."},{"name":"One blueprint for all forty","verdict":"poor","why":"The blueprint holds credentials for every identity it creates, so one compromise reaches the sensitive agent.","tradeoff":"Right when all agents share trust level and permissions."},{"name":"One blueprint per agent","verdict":"good","why":"Strongest isolation.","tradeoff":"Forty credential sets and heavy operations."}],"flips":[{"if":"All forty are equally sensitive","then":"One blueprint with strict federation and monitoring."},{"if":"Each agent belongs to a different team","then":"Blueprint per team."}],"takeaway":"Group by shared credentials and baseline permissions, not by convenience."},{"id":"w12-t15-s2","title":"Who answers for the agent","situation":"A business unit launches an agent. The product manager wants to be accountable, and a platform engineer manages credentials.","constraints":["Every agent needs a named human","Lifecycle decisions are business decisions","Technical rotation is an engineering task"],"unknowns":["Will the PM change roles soon?","Is there a service principal for automation?"],"question":"How do you assign sponsors and owners?","options":[{"name":"Sponsor: the product manager (user). Owner: the platform engineer (user or service principal)","verdict":"best","why":"Sponsors decide lifecycle and can disable. Owners manage credentials and can re-enable or hard-delete.","tradeoff":"Two people to keep current when staff change."},{"name":"Sponsor: a role-assignable group","verdict":"poor","why":"Role-assignable groups cannot be sponsors.","tradeoff":"Some other group types may be allowed, so check type first."},{"name":"Sponsor only, no owner","verdict":"good","why":"Meets the minimum of one sponsor.","tradeoff":"No technical owner to manage credentials or re-enable after a disable."}],"flips":[{"if":"The sponsor leaves","then":"Reassign a sponsor immediately, since at least one is required."},{"if":"Credentials are fully federated","then":"Owner duties shrink, but keep one for re-enable and delete."}],"takeaway":"Sponsors carry accountability, owners carry control."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t15-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Entra Agent ID object to its role.","pairs":[{"l":"Agent identity blueprint","r":"Holds credentials and creates identities"},{"l":"Agent identity","r":"Service principal with no credentials"},{"l":"Agent user","r":"Optional user object for mailbox and licenses"},{"l":"Sponsor","r":"Required business accountability, no auth edits"}],"explain":"The blueprint is the credential boundary. The agent identity is a credential-less service principal. The agent user is optional and Frontier-only. Sponsors are required but cannot edit authentication settings.","ref":"r17"},{"id":"w12-t15-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Why group only similar agents under one blueprint?","options":["Blueprints limit the number of tokens","Agent identities cannot be multitenant otherwise","MFA is enforced per blueprint","The blueprint holds credentials for every identity it creates"],"answer":3,"explain":"A compromised blueprint exposes all its identities. Agents have no MFA, and identities are single tenant regardless of blueprint choice.","ref":"r17"},{"id":"w12-t15-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which statements about owners and sponsors are supported by the docs?","options":["Sponsors are required, at least one per blueprint and agent identity","Owners can be groups","Owners can re-enable or hard-delete","Sponsors can edit authentication settings"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Sponsors are required and owners are optional technical admins who can re-enable or hard-delete. Owners are users or service principals, never groups, and sponsors cannot edit auth settings.","ref":"r18"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where do an agent's credentials live in Entra Agent ID?","back":"On the blueprint, not on the agent identity. The identity is a service principal of type ServiceIdentity with no credentials."},{"front":"Sponsor vs owner?","back":"Sponsor: required business accountability, can disable or soft-delete. Owner: optional technical admin (user or service principal), manages credentials and can re-enable or hard-delete."},{"front":"Why Conditional Access for agents?","back":"Agents have no MFA. Conditional Access is evaluated at token issuance or refresh, with Continuous Access Evaluation for some resources."}],"related":["w12-t16","w12-t17","w12-t18","w17-t07","w12-l5"],"refs":["r14","r15","r17","r18","r27"]},{"id":"w12-t16","order":16,"group":"Identity and governance","title":"Autonomous vs on-behalf-of agents","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"An agent can act as itself (app permissions), on behalf of a signed-in user (delegated permissions) or as its own user account. Pick the mode first, then confirm permissions and consent, because the mode decides whose access bounds the data and who is accountable.","useIf":"You are deciding whose permissions an agent uses when it reads data or takes actions.","avoidIf":"The agent touches no protected resources, so identity mode has no effect.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Entra Agent ID defines three execution modes [[r17]]. In **S2S** (autonomous), the agent acts as itself with application permissions and the agent identity is the token subject. In **OBO** (on-behalf-of), the user is the token subject, the agent identity is the actor and permissions are delegated. In **Agentic-User**, the agent acts as its own user account, which needs the Frontier preview. The docs say to pick the mode first, then confirm permissions and consent [[r17]]. Hosted agents follow this pattern: with a user token the platform supports OAuth 2.0 OBO, without one the agent uses its own identity, and in both cases it keeps its dedicated Entra identity for authentication, authorization and auditability [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t16-d1","title":"Choosing the mode","alt":"If a user is present and their access should bound the data, use OBO. If the work runs with no user, use S2S. If the agent must act as a team member with a mailbox or Teams presence, use the agent user mode.","src":"flowchart TB\n  Q{Whose access bounds the data?} -->|The signed-in user| O[OBO: user subject, agent actor]\n  Q -->|The agent itself, no user| S[S2S: agent identity subject]\n  Q -->|A team member agent in M365| A[Agentic-User: needs Frontier preview]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Foundry agent types by identity (autopilot overview, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Type","Identity","Acts","Cannot"],"rows":[["Assistive","Agent identity with signed-in user context","On behalf of a user, within that user's permissions","Act beyond the user's access"],["Background service","Agent identity, app-only","Autonomously as itself","Perform Microsoft 365 actions"],["Autopilot","Agent identity plus agent user account","As itself in Microsoft 365, including group settings","Be an autopilot without an agent user account: the distinction is binary"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Why autopilots exist.** Without an agent user, Microsoft 365 actions happen only on behalf of a signed-in user. That fails when no user is in the loop, such as event triggers, and in group chats, where any chosen user's permissions would apply to the whole thread, so actions are misattributed and members can see content they cannot access [[r19]]. At the 2026-05-01 Agent 365 GA announcement, delegated agents and agents behind the scenes with their own access were GA, and agents in team workflows with their own access were public preview [[r20]]. The autopilot preview status itself is **UNVERIFIED** [[r19]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Token audience","md":"A token whose audience is the MCP server URL instead of the downstream resource ID (for example `https://storage.azure.com`) fails even with correct RBAC. Check the audience first when an OBO or S2S call returns 401 or 403 [[r4]]."}],"whereToUse":["Chat assistants that read or write the signed-in user's own mail, files or calendar: OBO","Scheduled, event-driven and backend automation with no user present: S2S","Digital coworkers that join group chats and hold a role: agent user (autopilot, Frontier preview)","Reviewing least privilege per agent: decide mode, then application or delegated permissions, then consent"],"whenToUse":{"use":["OBO when access must follow the signed-in user and revocation of the user should revoke the agent's reach","S2S when there is no user, and permissions are scoped to the agent itself","Agentic-User when the agent must act as a team member and needs a mailbox or Teams presence"],"avoid":["OBO in group chats or event triggers, where no single user is the right subject","S2S with broad application permissions for work that is really about one user's data","Agentic-User when the tenant is outside Frontier preview or preview is not allowed"],"flips":["A user is present and the data is personal: OBO. The same workload run nightly: S2S","Chat moves from one-to-one to a group: reconsider and likely move to an agent user","Preview is not allowed: stay with OBO and S2S, which were GA at the 2026-05-01 announcement"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Execution modes compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["OBO (delegated)","Access must be bounded by the user","No user, group chats","Needs user token and consent; bounded by user permissions [[r17]]"],["S2S (application permissions)","Background work with no user","Data belongs to one user","Over-broad app permissions expose all users' data"],["Agentic-User (autopilot)","Coworker role in group settings","Tenant outside Frontier preview","Preview, license and mailbox management [[r19]]"],["Shared service account","Nothing in production","Always","No per-agent audit and hard to revoke one agent"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A group-chat agent shows content that some members cannot access","cause":"The agent acted on behalf of one user and that user's permissions applied to the whole thread","fix":"Give the agent its own account (agent user) or remove it from group contexts [[r19]]."},{"symptom":"A nightly job fails with consent errors under OBO","cause":"There is no signed-in user to delegate","fix":"Use S2S with application permissions scoped to the job, and review consent [[r17]]."},{"symptom":"401 or 403 despite correct roles","cause":"Wrong token audience","fix":"Request the token for the downstream resource ID audience [[r4]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t16-s1","title":"Nightly reconciliation job","situation":"At 2 a.m. an agent reads shared finance data, compares it with a ledger and posts a summary to a queue. No one is signed in.","constraints":["No user present","Data is shared, not personal","No Microsoft 365 actions"],"unknowns":["Which resources need access?","Who is the sponsor?"],"question":"Which mode do you use?","options":[{"name":"S2S with the agent identity and narrowly scoped application permissions","verdict":"best","why":"No user exists to delegate, and the agent identity is the token subject with an audit trail.","tradeoff":"Application permissions are not bounded by any user, so scope them tightly."},{"name":"OBO","verdict":"poor","why":"There is no signed-in user to act for.","tradeoff":"Right for interactive assistants."},{"name":"Agentic-User","verdict":"good","why":"Works if the agent also needs a mailbox.","tradeoff":"Frontier preview and license overhead for no Microsoft 365 need."}],"flips":[{"if":"The job must send mail as the agent","then":"Agentic-User."},{"if":"A user triggers it and its output is personal","then":"OBO."}],"takeaway":"No user in the loop means the agent acts as itself."},{"id":"w12-t16-s2","title":"Personal mailbox assistant","situation":"An assistant summarizes the signed-in user's inbox and drafts replies. Thousands of users, each with different access.","constraints":["Access must match each user","Interactive","Revoking a user must stop access"],"unknowns":["Is consent granted by users or admins?","Which scopes are needed?"],"question":"How does the agent access mail?","options":[{"name":"OBO with delegated permissions","verdict":"best","why":"The user is the token subject and the agent is the actor, so access follows each user.","tradeoff":"Consent and token handling, and it fails when no user is present."},{"name":"S2S with an application mail permission","verdict":"poor","why":"It would grant access to every mailbox in scope, far beyond the user in front of the agent.","tradeoff":"Right for a compliance scanner with explicit approval."},{"name":"Agentic-User","verdict":"good","why":"The agent has its own mailbox.","tradeoff":"It would read its own mailbox, not the user's, so it does not meet the need."}],"flips":[{"if":"The task runs on a schedule with no user","then":"S2S with per-resource scoping."},{"if":"Many users share one conversation","then":"Agent user."}],"takeaway":"Personal data calls for delegated access."},{"id":"w12-t16-s3","title":"Coworker in team chats","situation":"A release coordinator agent joins a group chat, answers questions, sends updates and reacts to events when nobody is online.","constraints":["Group settings","Event-triggered actions","Frontier preview allowed"],"unknowns":["Who is its manager and sponsor?","Which data may it see?"],"question":"Which identity mode fits?","options":[{"name":"Agentic-User (autopilot) with its own agent user account","verdict":"best","why":"It acts as itself, so it serves the whole team and responds without a user, with a named manager accountable.","tradeoff":"Frontier preview and lifecycle for an extra account."},{"name":"OBO restricted to one-to-one chats","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps access bounded by one user.","tradeoff":"Loses group and event-driven use."},{"name":"OBO inside the group chat","verdict":"poor","why":"One member's permissions would apply to everyone in the thread.","tradeoff":"Right when the chat has one participant."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview is not allowed","then":"One-to-one OBO assistants, and keep group work out of scope."},{"if":"The agent needs no Microsoft 365 actions","then":"Background service with S2S."}],"takeaway":"In groups there is no right user, so the agent needs its own."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t16-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each mode to its token subject or character.","pairs":[{"l":"S2S","r":"Agent identity is the token subject"},{"l":"OBO","r":"User is the subject, agent identity is the actor"},{"l":"Agentic-User","r":"Agent acts as its own user account"},{"l":"Background service agent","r":"App-only, cannot do Microsoft 365 actions"}],"explain":"These follow the identity developer docs and the autopilot overview. Agentic-User needs the Frontier preview.","ref":"r17"},{"id":"w12-t16-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"An assistant that worked one-to-one now joins group chats. What is the main risk of keeping OBO?","options":["Token expiry","Loss of Conditional Access","No audit logs","One user's permissions apply to the whole thread"],"answer":3,"explain":"In a group the agent has to guess a user, and that user's permissions apply to everyone. Expiry, Conditional Access and audit are not the documented problem.","ref":"r19"},{"id":"w12-t16-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A nightly OBO job returns consent errors. What is the correct fix?","options":["Ask users to sign in at night","Add an agent user","Remove Conditional Access","Use S2S with scoped application permissions"],"answer":3,"explain":"OBO needs a user token. A scheduled job has none, so S2S with the agent identity fits. An agent user is for Microsoft 365 presence, and removing Conditional Access weakens control.","ref":"r17"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"OBO vs S2S token subjects?","back":"OBO: the user is the subject, the agent identity is the actor, with delegated permissions. S2S: the agent identity is the subject, with application permissions."},{"front":"What defines an autopilot?","back":"Identity, not capabilities: an agent with its own agent user account (plus an agent identity). Without the account it is not an autopilot. Frontier preview."}],"related":["w12-t15","w12-t17","w07-t13","w17-t01","w12-l5"],"refs":["r2","r4","r17","r19","r20"]},{"id":"w12-t17","order":17,"group":"Identity and governance","title":"Agent 365: observe, govern, secure","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Microsoft Agent 365 is a control plane that observes, governs and secures the agents in an organization. It is GA since 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, licensed per user, and it does not build or host agents.","useIf":"You must see, govern and protect agents from many platforms in one admin surface.","avoidIf":"You expect it to build, host, orchestrate or run an agent: it does none of these.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Agent 365 gives organizations three pillars [[r12]]. **Observe**: real-time visibility through a central agent registry and agent map, with adoption, activity and health. **Govern**: lifecycle, access and compliance through the registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center, Microsoft Entra and Microsoft Purview. **Secure**: Entra risk-based access, Purview information protection and DLP, and Defender threat detection. It is a control plane over agents from Foundry, Copilot Studio, partners and other clouds, not a Foundry feature [[r29]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t17-d1","title":"Control plane over many runtimes","alt":"Agents from Foundry, Copilot Studio, partners and other clouds feed Agent 365, which provides observe, govern and secure through the registry, Entra, Purview and Defender.","src":"flowchart LR\n  F[Foundry agents] --> A365[Agent 365 control plane]\n  C[Copilot Studio agents] --> A365\n  P[Partner agents] --> A365\n  X[Other clouds and frameworks] --> A365\n  A365 --> O[Observe: registry and map]\n  A365 --> G[Govern: Entra and Purview]\n  A365 --> S[Secure: Defender and DLP]"},{"t":"table","caption":"How an agent joins Agent 365 (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Path","For","What you get"],"rows":[["Native integration","Microsoft platforms and supported partner agents","Inventory without the SDK"],["Agent 365 SDK","Agents built with the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Microsoft Agent Framework, LangChain, CrewAI or LlamaIndex","Identity, governed tooling and notifications; you still host and run the agent"],["Registry synchronization","Connected platforms such as Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock","Inventory, not code-level capabilities"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The **Agent 365 SDK** (Python, JavaScript, .NET) covers identity, tooling (governed Work IQ MCP servers for Mail, Calendar, Word, SharePoint and Teams) and notifications. It does **not** build, host, orchestrate or run tools, and it is not the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK. Notifications need an agent user account and the Frontier preview program [[r13]]. Its Observability SDK is deprecated for new work: use the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro [[r26]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Availability, licensing and status","md":"Agent 365 became GA on **2026-05-01** for the Commercial segment, licensed per user. At least one user needs a qualifying Agent 365 license, and Microsoft E5 is described as the best-fit prerequisite. It is included in Microsoft 365 E7 and available as an add-on to E5, A5 or Business Premium. A numeric price is **UNVERIFIED** [[r12]]. At the GA announcement, delegated agents and agents behind the scenes with their own access were GA, agents in team workflows with their own access were public preview, and discovery of local and shadow agents through Defender and Intune was announced as preview [[r20]]."}],"whereToUse":["A central AI admin view of every agent across Microsoft and partner platforms","Governing agent lifecycle, access reviews and compliance with Entra and Purview","Applying risk-based access, DLP and threat detection to agents","Bringing agents built on other frameworks or clouds into one inventory"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Many teams and platforms are creating agents and nobody has a full inventory","Security and compliance teams need the same controls for agents as for users","You want code-level governed Work IQ tools or notifications for framework-built agents"],"avoid":["You need a place to host or orchestrate an agent: use a runtime such as Foundry","You need only Entra identity and Conditional Access: Entra Agent ID is available to all Entra customers","Your tenant is outside the Commercial segment, which is where GA was announced"],"flips":["Agent is on another cloud: use registry sync for inventory, and the SDK only if you need Work IQ tools or notifications","Preview is not allowed: rely on GA modes (delegated, behind-the-scenes own access) and treat team-workflow agents and shadow discovery as pilots","Licensing is the blocker: confirm the plan against the service description before design"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Governance options","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Agent 365","Cross-platform inventory, governance and security","Only one platform and no licensing budget","Per-user licensing; some features preview [[r12]]"],["Entra Agent ID alone","Identity, Conditional Access, access packages","You need registry, map, Purview and Defender views","CA and governance need E7 or an Agent 365 license plus Entra P1 or E3 [[r15]]"],["Foundry Control Plane","Foundry-centered fleet, custom agent registration through API Management","Estate spans Microsoft 365 admin processes","Different surface from Agent 365 [[r23]]"],["Copilot Studio governance","Maker-built agents in Microsoft 365","Code-first agents elsewhere","Covers Studio agents, not all runtimes [[tr4]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Team plans to deploy agents through the Agent 365 SDK","cause":"The SDK is mistaken for a runtime or builder","fix":"Host the agent yourself or on Foundry, and use the SDK only for identity, tooling and notifications [[r13]]."},{"symptom":"Notifications do not work in a pilot","cause":"They need an agent user account and the Frontier preview program","fix":"Enroll the tenant in Frontier or drop notifications from scope [[r13]]."},{"symptom":"Code uses the Agent 365 Observability SDK","cause":"It is deprecated for new work","fix":"Move to the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro [[r26]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t17-s1","title":"Framework-built agent on its own infrastructure","situation":"A team runs a LangChain-based agent in its own container platform. The business wants it in the inventory with governed access to Mail and Calendar tools.","constraints":["Agent stays where it runs","Governed Work IQ tools are wanted","Code can be changed"],"unknowns":["Is notification support needed?","Is the tenant in the Frontier preview?"],"question":"How does it join Agent 365?","options":[{"name":"Add the Agent 365 SDK to the agent","verdict":"best","why":"It gives identity and governed Work IQ MCP tools for a framework-built agent while you keep hosting.","tradeoff":"Code change, and notifications need an agent user and Frontier."},{"name":"Registry synchronization only","verdict":"good","why":"Inventory with no code change.","tradeoff":"No code-level tooling or notifications."},{"name":"Expect the SDK to host and run the agent","verdict":"poor","why":"The SDK does not build, host, orchestrate or run tools.","tradeoff":"Right only for a runtime product, which this is not."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only an inventory entry is required","then":"Registry sync or native integration."},{"if":"The agent moves to Foundry","then":"Native integration, plus the SDK only if you need extra tooling."}],"takeaway":"The SDK adds identity, tools and notifications, not hosting."},{"id":"w12-t17-s2","title":"Shadow agent inventory by quarter end","situation":"Security wants a count of all agents, including ones employees run locally, by the end of the quarter. Policy forbids preview features in production controls.","constraints":["No preview in production controls","Known platforms exist","Shadow agents are suspected"],"unknowns":["Which platforms are in use?","What evidence does audit need?"],"question":"How do you plan the inventory?","options":[{"name":"Native integration and registry sync for known platforms, with preview shadow discovery as a labeled pilot","verdict":"best","why":"Uses GA paths for the controlled estate and keeps preview discovery outside production controls.","tradeoff":"Coverage of local agents stays partial and unproven until discovery reaches GA."},{"name":"Wait until discovery is GA","verdict":"good","why":"Complies with the policy.","tradeoff":"No shadow signal this quarter."},{"name":"Report full coverage from the preview discovery","verdict":"poor","why":"Preview features carry no SLA and the announcement labeled local and shadow discovery as preview.","tradeoff":"Acceptable as a pilot finding, not as a control."}],"flips":[{"if":"Policy allows preview with a waiver","then":"Include shadow discovery in production reporting with caveats."},{"if":"All agents run on Foundry","then":"Native integration covers the estate."}],"takeaway":"Label preview coverage honestly in governance reports."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t17-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"On what date did Agent 365 become GA for the Commercial segment?","options":["2025-11-01","2026-05-01","2026-08-20","2026-12-01"],"answer":1,"explain":"Agent 365 became GA on 2026-05-01. The other dates are the Content Understanding API version, the end of the initial-preview hosted backend and the retirement of Foundry Workflows.","ref":"r12"},{"id":"w12-t17-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about the Agent 365 SDK are correct?","options":["It adds identity, governed tooling and notifications","It builds and hosts your agent","It is the same as the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK","Notifications need an agent user account and Frontier preview"],"answers":[0,3],"explain":"The SDK adds identity, tooling and notifications. It does not build, host, orchestrate or run tools, it is not the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK, and notifications need an agent user plus Frontier.","ref":"r13"},{"id":"w12-t17-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match the platform to its Agent 365 integration path.","pairs":[{"l":"Microsoft platforms and supported partners","r":"Native integration"},{"l":"LangChain or CrewAI agents","r":"Agent 365 SDK"},{"l":"Vertex AI or AWS Bedrock","r":"Registry synchronization"}],"explain":"The SDK overview lists these three paths: native integration, the SDK for framework-built agents, and registry synchronization for connected platforms.","ref":"r13"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Agent 365's three pillars and what backs them?","back":"Observe (registry, map), Govern (admin center, Entra, Purview), Secure (Entra risk-based access, Purview DLP, Defender). GA 2026-05-01, Commercial, per user."},{"front":"Does Agent 365 host agents?","back":"No. It is a control plane. The SDK adds identity, governed Work IQ tools and notifications, but you build, host and run the agent elsewhere."},{"front":"Agent 365 price?","back":"UNVERIFIED in this guide. Licensing is per user, included in Microsoft 365 E7, and an add-on to E5, A5 or Business Premium."}],"related":["w12-t15","w12-t16","w12-t18","w17-t07","w17-t03","w12-l6"],"refs":["r12","r13","r15","r20","r23","r26","r29","tr4"]},{"id":"w12-t18","order":18,"group":"Identity and governance","title":"Agent 365 registry, Purview and Defender","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"The Agent 365 registry is the unified inventory of agents. Purview audits, classifies and protects the data they touch, and Defender discovers agents and detects threats. Entra stays the identity layer through Agent ID.","useIf":"You administer or secure agents and need to know what you can see, what is on by default and what needs separate onboarding.","avoidIf":"You only need to build an agent: use the runtime topics first.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Registry.** In the Microsoft 365 admin center go to Agents, All Agents, Registry. It lists four types: Microsoft agents, External partner-built agents, Published by your org and Shared by creator [[r16]]. The summary shows total agents, **agents without owners** and **unmanaged agents** (created or managed outside Agent 365, without its risk protection and observability). You can filter by status, publisher type, channel, platform and data source [[r16]]. One Copilot Studio agent can appear more than once with different publisher types. That is by design, not an inventory error, and removing one entry does not remove the other [[r16]]. Agent 365 is the unified registry and control plane, while Entra keeps agent identity, Conditional Access and governance [[r29]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t18-d1","title":"Who does what for an agent instance","alt":"An agent instance appears in the Agent 365 registry and map. Purview audits and classifies its activity. Defender discovers it and detects threats. Entra provides identity and Conditional Access.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent instance] --> R[Registry and map]\n  A --> P[Purview: audit and classification]\n  A --> D[Defender: discovery and threat detection]\n  A --> E[Entra Agent ID: identity and Conditional Access]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Purview.** When you create an agent instance for Agent 365 it is automatically enabled for audit, sensitive-data detection with data classification, and the Compliance Manager assessments for AI regulations. For other capabilities (DLP, Insider Risk Management, communication compliance, eDiscovery, data lifecycle) you include the instance in policies as you would a user [[tr13]]. Audit covers agent-to-human, human-to-agent, agent-to-tools and agent-to-agent interactions, with prompts and responses in the unified audit log. Encryption without sensitivity labels is not supported, and labeled encrypted content needs the EXTRACT usage right as well as VIEW for AI to return it [[tr13]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Defender.** After you onboard to Agent 365, security for AI agents (discovery, posture assessment, threat detection) is enabled automatically. Add the Microsoft 365 connector (Entra ID Management events and Microsoft 365 activities) for investigation and advanced hunting. Copilot Studio **real-time protection** scans tool invocations for suspicious behavior and cross-prompt injection and blocks malicious actions, and it needs a Power Platform admin with the same App ID. **Local agents** are onboarded separately through Defender for Endpoint in active mode [[tr14]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Roles, licensing and status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Seeing all agents needs the **AI Reader** role (recommended least privilege) or AI Administrator and no specific license. Managing identities needs Agent ID Administrator. Conditional Access and identity governance need the Entra Agent ID licensing [[r29]]. Discovery of local and shadow agents through Defender and Intune was announced as preview [[r20]]. Product-level licensing for Purview and Defender capabilities beyond what these pages state is **UNVERIFIED**."}],"whereToUse":["An AI admin reviewing the full agent inventory and agents without owners","A compliance team auditing agent prompts, responses and tool interactions in Purview","A SOC investigating agent activity and blocking prompt injection in Copilot Studio agents","An identity team managing blueprints, permissions and Conditional Access in Entra"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the registry as the source of truth for what agents exist, and the Entra admin center for identity actions","Include agent instances in Purview policies the same way you include users","Turn on the Microsoft 365 connector so Defender alerts and hunting have data"],"avoid":["Do not read duplicate-looking rows as errors and delete one: they are separate lifecycle records","Do not assume local agents are covered by cloud onboarding","Do not assume blueprints created earlier appear automatically in the registry"],"flips":["Agents run only on Microsoft platforms: native integration may cover the inventory with no code","You need controls on agents outside Microsoft platforms: use the SDK or registry sync, and expect lighter coverage","A GA-only policy applies: treat local and shadow discovery as a labeled pilot"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where each task lives","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Agent 365 registry (M365 admin center)","Full inventory, owners, unmanaged agents","You need to edit identities and policies","Needs AI Reader or higher [[r29]]"],["Entra admin center","Agent identities, blueprints, Conditional Access, governance","You need agents that have no Entra agent identity","Shows only agents with an Entra Agent ID [[r29]]"],["Purview","Audit, classification, DLP and compliance for agent data","You need runtime threat blocking","Capabilities beyond audit and classification need policies [[tr13]]"],["Defender","Threat detection, hunting, Copilot Studio real-time protection","You need data governance records","Connector, Power Platform admin and endpoint onboarding needed [[tr14]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Defender shows no alerts for a Copilot Studio agent that was blocked","cause":"Real-time protection still blocks without the Microsoft 365 connector, but alerts and incidents do not appear","fix":"Connect the Microsoft 365 connector so alerts and hunting have data [[tr14]]."},{"symptom":"Local agents are missing from Defender","cause":"Local agents are onboarded separately from cloud agents","fix":"Set up AI agent runtime protection in Defender for Endpoint, in active mode [[tr14]]."},{"symptom":"A blueprint created earlier is not in the registry","cause":"Some previously created blueprints may not appear","fix":"Register the blueprint in the Agent 365 registry as described in the Entra guidance [[r29]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t18-s1","title":"Agents without owners","situation":"The registry shows many agents without owners and a growing count of unmanaged agents. Leadership asks for a plan.","constraints":["Owners left the company","Some agents run on other platforms","Compliance review is due"],"unknowns":["Which platforms host the unmanaged agents?","Are sponsors set on Entra blueprints?"],"question":"What is your first move?","options":[{"name":"Assign owners and sponsors, register blueprints, and bring unmanaged agents under Agent 365 through native integration, SDK or registry sync","verdict":"best","why":"It fixes accountability and brings agents under risk protection and observability.","tradeoff":"Cross-team effort and some code changes for framework-built agents."},{"name":"Block every unmanaged agent","verdict":"good","why":"Stops risk quickly.","tradeoff":"Breaks business processes and hides which agents mattered."},{"name":"Delete duplicate-looking rows to clean the list","verdict":"poor","why":"Duplicates are separate lifecycle records by design, and removing one does not remove the other.","tradeoff":"Cleanup is right only after you confirm a row is truly stale."}],"flips":[{"if":"An agent is confirmed abandoned and risky","then":"Block or remove it after notifying stakeholders."},{"if":"All agents are on Foundry","then":"Native integration and Entra sponsors cover most of it."}],"takeaway":"Fix ownership first, then coverage."},{"id":"w12-t18-s2","title":"Injection protection for maker-built agents","situation":"The security team wants cross-prompt injection blocked in Copilot Studio agents and wants alerts in the Defender portal. Some employees run local agents too.","constraints":["Real-time blocking and alerts","Power Platform admin available","Local agents exist"],"unknowns":["Is Defender for Endpoint in active mode?","Who owns the Power Platform App ID?"],"question":"How do you set up Defender coverage?","options":[{"name":"Onboard Agent 365, connect the Microsoft 365 connector, enable Copilot Studio real-time protection with the Power Platform admin, and onboard local agents through Defender for Endpoint","verdict":"best","why":"Each population has its own onboarding, and the connector gives alerts and hunting.","tradeoff":"Several teams must coordinate, and one App ID must match across portals."},{"name":"Rely on the default enablement after Agent 365 onboarding","verdict":"good","why":"Discovery, posture and threat detection are on automatically.","tradeoff":"No Copilot Studio real-time protection and no local agents until you onboard them."},{"name":"Assume local agents are covered by cloud onboarding","verdict":"poor","why":"Local agents are onboarded separately and need Defender for Endpoint in active mode.","tradeoff":"Right only after the endpoint setup is done."}],"flips":[{"if":"Power Platform admin is unavailable","then":"Defer real-time protection and rely on detection, noting the gap."},{"if":"Policy forbids preview discovery","then":"Report local discovery as a pilot."}],"takeaway":"Cloud, Copilot Studio and local agents each need their own onboarding step."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t18-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are enabled automatically when you create an agent instance for Agent 365, according to the Purview page?","options":["Audit","Detection of sensitive data with data classification","Compliance Manager assessments for AI regulations","DLP policies applied without configuration"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Audit, data classification and the Compliance Manager assessments are automatic. DLP and the other capabilities need the agent instance included in policies.","ref":"tr13"},{"id":"w12-t18-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Copilot Studio real-time protection blocked an action, but no alert appears in Defender. What is the likely cause?","options":["Real-time protection is disabled","The agent is a local agent","Agent 365 is not GA","The Microsoft 365 connector is not connected"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without the Microsoft 365 connector, real-time protection continues to block, but alerts and incidents do not show. A disabled protection would not block, and local agents use a separate path.","ref":"tr14"},{"id":"w12-t18-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which role does the registry guidance recommend for viewing all agents with least privilege?","options":["Global Administrator","AI Reader","Agent ID Administrator","Security Administrator"],"answer":1,"explain":"AI Reader is the recommended least-privilege role for viewing. Agent ID Administrator manages identities, and the others are broader.","ref":"r29"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Four agent types in the Agent 365 registry?","back":"Microsoft agents, External partner-built agents, Published by your org, Shared by creator. One Copilot Studio agent can appear under more than one type by design."},{"front":"Agent 365 registry vs Entra admin center?","back":"Registry: complete inventory, including agents without an Entra identity. Entra: agents with Agent IDs, blueprints, Conditional Access and governance."},{"front":"Which Defender coverage needs separate onboarding?","back":"Local agents on endpoints (Defender for Endpoint in active mode) and Copilot Studio real-time protection (Power Platform admin, matching App ID)."}],"related":["w12-t15","w12-t17","w07-t12","w17-t03","w17-t07","w12-l6"],"refs":["r16","r20","r29","tr13","tr14"]},{"id":"w12-t19","order":19,"group":"Identity and governance","title":"Runtime choice matrix","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Choose the agent runtime by control, effort, protocol needs, isolation and where code must run: a Foundry prompt agent, a hosted agent, self-hosting on AKS or Container Apps with registration, or a Copilot Studio agent.","useIf":"You must justify where an agent runs and what each option costs in control, operations and governance.","avoidIf":"The runtime is already fixed by policy and you only need to wire identity or governance.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The same agent can run in four homes. A **prompt agent** is config only and Foundry runs it with no code or compute to manage [[r1]]. A **hosted agent** is your code on per-session VM-isolated sandboxes with a dedicated Entra identity and endpoint [[r2]]. **Self-hosting on AKS or Container Apps** keeps code on infrastructure you run, and you register it for governance: Foundry Control Plane puts API Management in front as a proxy, and Agent 365 uses the SDK or registry sync [[r23]] [[r13]]. A **Copilot Studio** agent is built in a graphical, low-code studio and published to channels such as Teams, Microsoft Copilot, websites and mobile apps [[tr4]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w12-t19-d1","title":"Runtime decision tree","alt":"If code must run outside Foundry choose self-hosting with registration. If makers build it choose Copilot Studio. If developers build it and need custom code, protocols or files choose a hosted agent, otherwise a prompt agent.","src":"flowchart TB\n  Q1{Must code run outside Foundry?} -->|Yes| SH[Self-host on AKS or Container Apps, register it]\n  Q1 -->|No| Q2{Who builds it?}\n  Q2 -->|Makers, low-code| CS[Copilot Studio agent]\n  Q2 -->|Developers| Q3{Custom code, protocols or files?}\n  Q3 -->|Yes| HA[Hosted agent]\n  Q3 -->|No| PA[Prompt agent]"},{"t":"p","md":"Each home trades the same four dials. **Control**: configuration only, your own code, total control, or low-code with harness choices. **Effort**: a prompt agent has nothing to run, a hosted agent has code but no cluster, and a self-hosted agent adds scaling, identity wiring, session persistence and patching (inference). **Protocols and isolation**: hosted agents offer Responses, Invocations, WebSocket, Activity and A2A inside per-session VM-isolated sandboxes, while self-hosting leaves both to you. **Governance**: Foundry identity and tracing for Foundry agents, registration for self-hosted ones, and the Agent 365 registry for Copilot Studio agents [[r16]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Status caveats on 2026-10-13","head":["Runtime","Status note"],"rows":[["Prompt agent","Agents (core) GA [[r9]]"],["Hosted agent","No explicit GA statement found: UNVERIFIED. Long-running resilience is preview [[tr2]]"],["Self-hosted with registration","Your platform. Agent 365 GA since 2026-05-01, some modes and discovery preview [[r20]]"],["Copilot Studio","App creation experience is preview [[tr4]]"]]},{"t":"dials","id":"w12-t19-pick","title":"Pick a runtime","prompt":"Flip the three dials to see which runtime leads and why.","dials":[{"id":"control","label":"Control needed","values":[{"id":"config","label":"Configuration is enough"},{"id":"code","label":"Custom code, protocols or files"}]},{"id":"builder","label":"Who builds it","values":[{"id":"devs","label":"Developers"},{"id":"makers","label":"Makers, low-code"}]},{"id":"where","label":"Where it may run","values":[{"id":"managed","label":"Microsoft-managed runtime is fine"},{"id":"mine","label":"Must run on my infrastructure"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prompt","name":"Prompt agent","summary":"Config only, run by Foundry."},{"id":"hosted","name":"Hosted agent","summary":"Your code in per-session sandboxes."},{"id":"self","name":"Self-host and register","summary":"AKS or Container Apps plus registration."},{"id":"cs","name":"Copilot Studio","summary":"Low-code agent for Microsoft 365 channels."}],"scores":{"prompt":{"control":{"config":{"s":2,"why":"Instructions, model and tools cover it [[r1]]."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"No place for custom runtime code."}},"builder":{"devs":{"s":1,"why":"SDK or REST definitions fit CI/CD."},"makers":{"s":1,"why":"Portal authoring needs no code."}},"where":{"managed":{"s":2,"why":"Foundry runs it."},"mine":{"s":-2,"why":"It cannot run on your own hosts."}}},"hosted":{"control":{"config":{"s":-1,"why":"Container effort for a config-level need."},"code":{"s":2,"why":"Your framework, protocols and `$HOME` state [[r2]]."}},"builder":{"devs":{"s":2,"why":"Developers bring code and ship an image or .zip."},"makers":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs engineering skills."}},"where":{"managed":{"s":2,"why":"Foundry provisions per-session compute."},"mine":{"s":-2,"why":"It runs on Foundry-managed sandboxes, not your hosts."}}},"self":{"control":{"config":{"s":-2,"why":"Heavy for a configuration-level need."},"code":{"s":2,"why":"Total control of code and protocols."}},"builder":{"devs":{"s":1,"why":"Developers and platform engineers run it."},"makers":{"s":-2,"why":"Not a maker tool."}},"where":{"managed":{"s":-1,"why":"You take on operations you could avoid."},"mine":{"s":2,"why":"Code stays on your infrastructure and registration adds governance [[r23]]."}}},"cs":{"control":{"config":{"s":1,"why":"Describe the agent, connect knowledge and tools [[tr4]]."},"code":{"s":-2,"why":"Low-code studio with harness choices, not arbitrary code."}},"builder":{"devs":{"s":0,"why":"Professional makers get depth, but code is not the model."},"makers":{"s":2,"why":"Built for makers [[tr4]]."}},"where":{"managed":{"s":1,"why":"Microsoft-managed and published to channels."},"mine":{"s":-2,"why":"It does not run on your hosts."}}}},"takeaway":"Start from where code may run and who builds it, then add control only as far as the requirement demands."}],"whereToUse":["Architecture reviews that decide the runtime for a new agent","Migration plans moving agents between runtimes without losing governance","Interview answers that must defend a runtime with dials and flip conditions","Platform standards that set a default and name the exceptions"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Default to the prompt agent for config-level needs, because it is the least to manage","Move to a hosted agent when you need your code, custom protocols or persistent files","Self-host when code must run on your infrastructure, then register it for governance","Choose Copilot Studio when makers own the agent and Microsoft 365 channels are the main surface"],"avoid":["Hosting everything yourself by habit when a managed runtime meets the requirement","Choosing hosted agents on a policy that needs a documented GA label until you confirm status","Choosing by tool preference rather than by control, effort, protocols, isolation and governance"],"flips":["A webhook or custom protocol requirement flips prompt agent to hosted agent","A residency or on-premises requirement flips hosted to self-hosted with registration","A maker team that owns the agent flips developer-built runtimes to Copilot Studio"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Runtime options compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt agent","Config-level behavior, fastest path","Custom code, files or protocols","Limited control [[r1]]"],["Hosted agent","Custom code on managed, isolated compute","Code must stay off Foundry, or config suffices","Compute per active session; GA label unconfirmed [[r2]]"],["Self-hosted on AKS or Container Apps","Code must run on your infrastructure","You want managed sessions and identity","You operate everything; governance by registration [[r23]]"],["Copilot Studio agent","Makers, Microsoft 365 channels, connectors","Arbitrary code, custom protocols","Different governance surface; some features preview [[tr4]]"],["Direct Responses API call","Definition inside an existing app","You want Foundry to host and publish it","You host the caller [[r1]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Governance team cannot see an agent running on another cloud","cause":"The agent was self-hosted and never registered","fix":"Register it in Foundry Control Plane, use the Agent 365 SDK, or sync the registry [[r23]] [[r13]]."},{"symptom":"Cost and ops grow after moving to self-hosting 'for control'","cause":"No requirement forced it, and you now run scaling, identity wiring and patching","fix":"Revisit the decision tree and move back to a managed runtime unless residency or infrastructure rules demand it."},{"symptom":"A Copilot Studio agent appears twice in the registry","cause":"Separate publisher records for the same agent exist by design","fix":"Use the publisher filter and check each record's actions [[r16]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w12-t19-s1","title":"Business team builds its own agent","situation":"A business team with no developers wants an agent for Teams that calls approved connectors and a knowledge source.","constraints":["Makers own it","Microsoft 365 channels are the surface","Governance team will review it"],"unknowns":["Which connectors are needed?","Does it need custom code later?"],"question":"Which runtime do you pick?","options":[{"name":"Copilot Studio agent","verdict":"best","why":"A low-code studio for makers, published to Teams and Microsoft Copilot, and it appears in the Agent 365 registry.","tradeoff":"Limited code control, and some features are preview."},{"name":"Prompt agent in Foundry","verdict":"good","why":"No code and published to Teams.","tradeoff":"Authoring is still engineer-oriented and uses a different governance surface."},{"name":"Hosted agent","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs developers, a container and compute for a need that low-code covers.","tradeoff":"Right when custom code or protocols appear."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent needs custom code or webhooks","then":"Hand it to developers on a hosted agent."},{"if":"Agents must live in Foundry for tracing and evals","then":"Prompt agent."}],"takeaway":"Let the builder profile pick the home when control needs are modest."},{"id":"w12-t19-s2","title":"Code must stay in the data center","situation":"An existing agent runs in the company's own data center because of a residency rule. The governance team wants it visible and controlled alongside the cloud agents.","constraints":["Code cannot move","Central governance is required","Platform engineers are available"],"unknowns":["Does it speak A2A or an OpenAI-compatible API?","Does it need governed Work IQ tools?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Keep it on your infrastructure and register it (Control Plane, Agent 365 SDK or registry sync)","verdict":"best","why":"Code stays put, and registration gives inventory and access control.","tradeoff":"You keep operating the runtime, and the capabilities differ by registration path."},{"name":"Port it to a hosted agent with bring-your-own VNet","verdict":"good","why":"Managed sessions and identity while keeping network isolation.","tradeoff":"The code and data path move into Azure, which the residency rule may forbid."},{"name":"Rebuild it as a prompt agent","verdict":"poor","why":"Loses the custom code and moves the workload off premises.","tradeoff":"Right only for simple agents with no residency rule."}],"flips":[{"if":"Residency allows an Azure region","then":"Hosted agent becomes the lowest-ops path."},{"if":"Only inventory is needed","then":"Registry sync with no code change."}],"takeaway":"Where code may run decides the home, and registration carries governance."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w12-t19-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A residency rule says agent code must run in the company's data center, but central governance is required. What is the best fit?","options":["Hosted agent","Prompt agent","Self-host and register for governance","Copilot Studio agent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Self-hosting keeps code on your infrastructure, and registration (Control Plane, Agent 365 SDK or registry sync) gives governance. Hosted, prompt and Copilot Studio runtimes are Microsoft-managed.","ref":"r23"},{"id":"w12-t19-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each runtime to its defining trait.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt agent","r":"Config only, nothing to host"},{"l":"Hosted agent","r":"Your code in per-session VM-isolated sandboxes"},{"l":"Self-hosted on AKS or Container Apps","r":"Code on your infrastructure, registered for governance"},{"l":"Copilot Studio agent","r":"Low-code, built for makers"}],"explain":"These follow the Foundry overview, the hosted agents page, the Control Plane registration page and the Copilot Studio overview.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w12-t19-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are legitimate reasons to leave the prompt agent for a hosted agent?","options":["You need webhook payloads","You need persistent files across turns","You want fewer things to operate","You need to choose CPU and memory for sessions"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Webhooks, persistent files and compute control are the documented reasons. Wanting fewer things to operate points the other way, toward the prompt agent.","ref":"r2"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Decision order for the runtime choice?","back":"First: must code run outside Foundry? Then: who builds it (makers or developers)? Then: custom code, protocols or files needed? Prompt agent unless a requirement forces more."},{"front":"How does a self-hosted agent get governance?","back":"Register it in Foundry Control Plane (API Management proxy), add the Agent 365 SDK, or use registry sync for connected platforms. You still host and run it."},{"front":"Which runtime lacks a documented GA label as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Hosted agents: no separate GA row and no explicit GA statement found (UNVERIFIED). Voice-based agents are preview."}],"related":["w12-t01","w12-t03","w12-t07","w12-t17","w11-t25","w12-l1","w12-l7"],"refs":["r1","r2","r9","r13","r16","r20","r23","tr2","tr4"]}]});
